window.VALTHORNE_FEATURES = [{"index":0,"title":"Asset loading and caching","slug":"02-Core-Assets","summary":"Assets loads CPU-side asset representations asynchronously and caches futures by an asset key. Parameter objects describe the source and decoding options; registered loaders perform the actual conversion. This allows a loading screen to observe progress without doing all decoding in its render callback.","capabilities":[{"title":"Virtual-thread loading","description":"Each uncached load runs on the owned virtual-thread-per-task executor."},{"title":"Prepared batches","description":"Queue parameters with prepare, then call load and observe batch progress."},{"title":"Direct loads","description":"loadAsync requests an individual asset and returns a future."},{"title":"Engine-thread handoff","description":"Use JGL.runTask before creating or changing OpenGL resources."},{"title":"Key deduplication","description":"Requests with the same key share cache state; keys must distinguish variants that must coexist."},{"title":"Loader registration","description":"Custom parameter types can be paired with custom loaders."},{"title":"Failure and release","description":"Future completion, cancellation, cache removal, and shutdown have distinct effects."}],"examples":[{"title":"Assets example","note":"Asset manager for Valthorne that supports \"prepare then load\" and direct async loads with caching.","code":"// 1) Register loaders (optional if you rely on the defaults in the static initializer).\nAssets.addLoader(TextureParameters.class, new TextureLoader());\nAssets.addLoader(SoundParameters.class,   new SoundLoader());\nAssets.addLoader(FontParameters.class,    new FontLoader());\n\n// 2) Queue assets you want to load as a batch.\nAssets.resetProgress();\nAssets.prepare(TextureParameters.fromClasspath(\"ui/button.png\", \"btn\"));\nAssets.prepare(FontParameters.fromClasspath(\"ui/font.ttf\", \"font\", 18));\n\n// 3) Kick off the batch load. Loader work runs on virtual threads.\nAssets.load().thenRun(() -> {\n    TextureData btn = Assets.get(\"btn\", TextureData.class);\n    FontData font = Assets.get(\"font\", FontData.class);\n\n    // Completion is not the OpenGL thread. Queue GPU creation back to JGL.\n    JGL.runTask(() -> {\n        buttonTexture = new Texture(btn);\n        buttonFont = new Font(font);\n    });\n});\n\n// 4) You can also load something immediately (deduped by key).\nAssets.loadAsync(TextureParameters.fromClasspath(\"ui/bg.png\", \"bg\"), TextureData.class)\n      .thenAccept(bg -> JGL.runTask(() -> backgroundTexture = new Texture(bg)));\n\n// 5) Progress reporting for loading screens.\nfloat p = Assets.getProgress(); // 0..1\n\n// 6) Shutdown at the end of the app if you want to stop background work.\nAssets.shutdown();"},{"title":"AssetLoader — load","note":"Loads asset data using the given parameters. This operation performs the work synchronously on the calling thread; scheduling and cache reuse belong to Assets. Loading failures may be reported as runtime exceptions, which complete an asynchronous asset request exceptionally. Concrete implementations define validation and ownership of the returned data.","code":"T load(P parameters)"},{"title":"key","note":"Returns the stable cache identity for this request. Requests with the same key share an existing load even if their parameter objects differ. The key must be non-null when used with Assets; generating it should not load the asset.","code":"String key()"},{"title":"addLoader","note":"Registers an AssetLoader for a specific AssetParameters type. This method maps the specified type of asset parameters to its corresponding loader, enabling the loading of assets defined by the given parameters type.","code":"public static <P extends AssetParameters, T> void addLoader(Class<P> parametersType, AssetLoader<P, T> loader)"},{"title":"resetProgress","note":"Resets the progress tracking for asset loading operations. This method sets both the number of prepared assets and the number of completed assets to zero. It is useful for initializing or restarting the loading progress tracking.","code":"public static void resetProgress()"}],"art":8,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Assets"},{"index":1,"title":"Audio performance and area sounds","slug":"02-Core-Audio-Overview","summary":"Valthorne audio supports buffered and streaming playback, managed players, and circle, rectangle, sphere, and box areas that attenuate gain from a logical listener. The system avoids inactive work, reuses decoder and upload storage, and keeps playback on its owned audio thread.","capabilities":[{"title":"Ambient coverage","description":"The self-contained Audio Studio generates two quiet, distinct mono tones and starts paused. Select A or B in the inspector (or click its colored source marker), then press Play / pause. Adjust volume, pitch, mute, looping, sphere/box shape, position, dimensions and fade distance. Scroll the inspector for additional controls on smaller windows. No external audio assets are required."},{"title":"Work avoided","description":"- Paused/inactive streams return before querying native playback state or decoding. - MP3 PCM transfer storage grows only for larger reads, instead of allocating a new byte array every refill. Decoder-internal allocations still exist. - OGG reuses the sample view for repeated reads into the same destination. - Stream uploads reuse the chunk buffer rather than creating a duplicate view. - WAV/OGG loops seek their…"},{"title":"Correctness fixes","description":"Unstarted stream queues detach safely during reset/disposal, buffered sources detach before their buffer is deleted, player disposal is idempotent, and disposed-player commands fail instead of touching deleted handles. OGG stream close is idempotent. Buffered OGG decoding frees the original PCM pointer without advancing it during the copy: the old path could free an interior pointer and crash the native heap. Loop…"},{"title":"Verification and measurements","description":"Run these engine checks from the Valthorne checkout with its local test and benchmark fixtures:"}],"examples":[{"title":"Library API","note":"Rectangle/box dimensions are half extents, measured from the center. Fade is measured from the nearest boundary, including Euclidean distance around corners. Zero fade creates a hard boundary; the boundary itself is inside. Non-finite coordinates and negative dimensions are rejected. Multiple zones use separate players and mix independently; their sum can…","code":"SoundPlayer rain = Audio.load(\"audio/rain.ogg\");\nrain.setArea(SoundArea.rectangle(100, 200, 40, 25, 12));\nrain.setVolume(0.6f);\nrain.setLooping(true);\nrain.play();\n\n// In the game update: 2D zones ignore Z.\nAudio.setListenerPosition(playerX, playerY, 0);\n\n// Other shapes:\nrain.setArea(SoundArea.circle(100, 200, 40, 12));\nrain.setArea(SoundArea.sphere(100, 200, 30, 40, 12));\nrain.setArea(SoundArea.box(100, 200, 30, 40, 25, 10, 12));\n\n// Remove attenuation, or release the player when no longer needed.\nrain.setArea(null);\nAudio.destroy(rain);"},{"title":"Audio playback and ambient areas — Audio example","note":"Related Audio playback and ambient areas example: This design avoids forcing engine users to manually update every individual sound player each frame. Instead, the audio subsystem owns that update lifecycle internally and keeps OpenAL interaction isolated to a single thread.","code":"Audio.init();\n\nSoundPlayer player = Audio.load(\"audio/music/theme.ogg\");\nplayer.play();\n\nAudio.run(() -> {\n    player.setLooping(true);\n    player.setGain(0.6f);\n});\n\nboolean onAudioThread = Audio.isAudioThread();\n\nAudio.destroy(player);\nAudio.dispose();"},{"title":"Audio playback and ambient areas — AudioFormat example","note":"Related Audio playback and ambient areas example: The main purpose of this enum is to provide a single place where format detection and decoder selection are defined. Callers typically use detect(byte[]) to inspect a file header or raw audio byte array and then obtain the appropriate decoder through getDecoder().","code":"byte[] bytes = Files.readAllBytes(Path.of(\"music.ogg\"));\nAudioFormat format = AudioFormat.detect(bytes);\nSoundDecoder decoder = format.getDecoder();\n\nif (decoder != null) {\n    SoundData sound = decoder.decode(bytes);\n}"},{"title":"Audio playback and ambient areas — SoundData example","note":"Related Audio playback and ambient areas example: Instances store decoded PCM data when the sound is buffered, or stream metadata and source information when the sound should be decoded incrementally at playback time. The factory methods load(byte[]) and load(String) perform format detection, metadata probing, compression classification, and buffered-versus-streamed selection automatically.","code":"SoundData click = SoundData.load(\"assets/sfx/click.wav\");\nSoundData theme = SoundData.load(Files.readAllBytes(Path.of(\"assets/music/theme.ogg\")));\n\nif (theme.streaming()) {\n    try (SoundStream stream = theme.openStream()) {\n        stream.seek(10f);\n    }\n}"},{"title":"Audio playback and ambient areas — SoundDecoder example","note":"Related Audio playback and ambient areas example: Implementations are format-specific. For example, WAV, OGG, and MP3 decoders each interpret the incoming bytes differently but all expose the same decode operation.","code":"SoundDecoder decoder = new WaveSoundDecoder();\nSoundData data = decoder.decode(bytes);"}],"art":4,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Audio-Overview"},{"index":2,"title":"Audio playback and ambient areas","slug":"02-Core-Audio","summary":"Audio separates encoded sources, decoded or streaming data, and an active player. Audio owns managed OpenAL execution; SoundPlayer provides playback, volume, looping, seeking, and streaming behavior. Ambient areas add position-based gain without forcing every sound to become a spatially panned source.","capabilities":[{"title":"Buffered playback","description":"Decode short sounds once and play from resident PCM buffers."},{"title":"Streaming","description":"Keep long audio encoded and replenish queued PCM chunks as playback progresses."},{"title":"Ambient coverage","description":"Circle, rectangle, sphere, and box areas define coverage and boundary falloff."},{"title":"Logical listener","description":"Publish ambient listener coordinates to reevaluate area attenuation."},{"title":"Gain and transport","description":"Volume, mute, loop, seek, and play/pause control the source independently of its encoded data."}],"examples":[{"title":"Audio example","note":"This design avoids forcing engine users to manually update every individual sound player each frame. Instead, the audio subsystem owns that update lifecycle internally and keeps OpenAL interaction isolated to a single thread.","code":"Audio.init();\n\nSoundPlayer player = Audio.load(\"audio/music/theme.ogg\");\nplayer.play();\n\nAudio.run(() -> {\n    player.setLooping(true);\n    player.setGain(0.6f);\n});\n\nboolean onAudioThread = Audio.isAudioThread();\n\nAudio.destroy(player);\nAudio.dispose();"},{"title":"AudioFormat example","note":"The main purpose of this enum is to provide a single place where format detection and decoder selection are defined. Callers typically use detect(byte[]) to inspect a file header or raw audio byte array and then obtain the appropriate decoder through getDecoder().","code":"byte[] bytes = Files.readAllBytes(Path.of(\"music.ogg\"));\nAudioFormat format = AudioFormat.detect(bytes);\nSoundDecoder decoder = format.getDecoder();\n\nif (decoder != null) {\n    SoundData sound = decoder.decode(bytes);\n}"},{"title":"SoundData example","note":"Instances store decoded PCM data when the sound is buffered, or stream metadata and source information when the sound should be decoded incrementally at playback time. The factory methods load(byte[]) and load(String) perform format detection, metadata probing, compression classification, and buffered-versus-streamed selection automatically.","code":"SoundData click = SoundData.load(\"assets/sfx/click.wav\");\nSoundData theme = SoundData.load(Files.readAllBytes(Path.of(\"assets/music/theme.ogg\")));\n\nif (theme.streaming()) {\n    try (SoundStream stream = theme.openStream()) {\n        stream.seek(10f);\n    }\n}"},{"title":"SoundDecoder example","note":"Implementations are format-specific. For example, WAV, OGG, and MP3 decoders each interpret the incoming bytes differently but all expose the same decode operation.","code":"SoundDecoder decoder = new WaveSoundDecoder();\nSoundData data = decoder.decode(bytes);"},{"title":"SoundLoader example","note":"SoundLoader is the asset-loader bridge between generic asset loading and the sound system. It accepts SoundParameters, inspects the underlying SoundSource, and delegates loading to the appropriate SoundData factory method.","code":"SoundLoader loader = new SoundLoader();\nSoundData data = loader.load(SoundParameters.fromPath(\"music.ogg\", \"theme\"));"},{"title":"SoundMetadata example","note":"SoundMetadata stores lightweight information about a piece of audio without requiring the full decoded PCM data to be retained. It is primarily used during probing and stream setup so the loader can make buffering and streaming decisions.","code":"SoundMetadata metadata = WaveSoundDecoder.probe(bytes);\nlong estimated = metadata.estimatedPcmBytes();"},{"title":"SoundPlayer example","note":"For streaming sounds, update() must be called from the audio system so processed buffers can be unqueued, timed correctly, refilled, and requeued. In Valthorne, that update lifecycle is handled by Audio, so engine users normally do not need to update each player manually.","code":"SoundPlayer player = Audio.load(\"audio/music/theme.ogg\");\n\nplayer.setLooping(true);\nplayer.setVolume(0.65f);\nplayer.play();\n\nfloat duration = player.duration();\nfloat progress = player.getProgress();\n\nplayer.fastForward(10f);\nplayer.pause();\nplayer.resume();\n\nplayer.setPlaybackSpeed(1.25f);\nplayer.toggleMute();\nplayer.stop();\n\nplayer.dispose();"},{"title":"SoundSource example","note":"SoundSource describes where raw sound data originates. It is modeled as a sealed interface with compact record implementations for file-path sources and in-memory byte-array sources.","code":"SoundSource pathSource = new SoundSource.PathSource(\"music/theme.ogg\");\nSoundSource bytesSource = new SoundSource.BytesSource(bytes);"},{"title":"SoundStream example","note":"SoundStream represents a pull-based decoded PCM stream used for long or compressed audio playback. Implementations expose stream metadata, seeking, chunked reading, and resource cleanup.","code":"try (SoundStream stream = soundData.openStream()) {\n    ByteBuffer chunk = BufferUtils.createByteBuffer(64 * 1024);\n    stream.seek(5f);\n    int bytesRead = stream.read(chunk);\n}"},{"title":"WaveSoundDecoder example","note":"During probing, the decoder scans RIFF chunks until it finds the fmt chunk and then the audio data chunk, accepting both data and buffer as valid audio payload chunk names. Metadata is returned as a SoundMetadata instance so higher-level code can decide whether the file should be buffered or streamed.","code":"byte[] bytes = Files.readAllBytes(Path.of(\"assets/audio/click.wav\"));\n\nSoundMetadata metadata = WaveSoundDecoder.probe(bytes);\nSoundData sound = new WaveSoundDecoder().decode(bytes);\n\nSoundMetadata fileMetadata = WaveSoundDecoder.probe(\"assets/audio/click.wav\");"},{"title":"WaveSoundStream example","note":"Seeking is implemented by moving to a byte position derived from the requested time, aligned to the current frame size so channel/sample boundaries are preserved.","code":"SoundData sound = SoundData.load(\"assets/music/theme.wav\");\n\ntry (WaveSoundStream stream = new WaveSoundStream(sound)) {\n    ByteBuffer pcm = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(64 * 1024);\n\n    stream.seek(3.5f);\n    int bytesRead = stream.read(pcm);\n\n    int channels = stream.channels();\n    int sampleRate = stream.sampleRate();\n    int bitsPerSample = stream.bitsPerSample();\n    float duration = stream.duration();\n}"}],"art":4,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Audio"},{"index":3,"title":"Events and listeners","slug":"02-Core-Events","summary":"The event system separates event data from handlers and dispatch. Event types identify routes; EventPublisher maintains ordered subscriptions. The JGL convenience methods connect application code to the engine publisher. An event can be consumed during dispatch, so ownership of an interaction can be decided before later handlers act.","capabilities":[{"title":"Typed routes","description":"EventType and EventTypes associate handlers with the intended payload type."},{"title":"Priorities","description":"Registration priority and insertion sequence establish deterministic dispatch order."},{"title":"Consumption","description":"A handler can consume an accepted event to prevent unwanted downstream handling."},{"title":"Adapters","description":"Listener adapters let you implement only the keyboard or pointer callbacks you need."}],"examples":[{"title":"Publication hot path","note":"The event system separates event data from handlers and dispatch. Event types identify routes; EventPublisher maintains ordered subscriptions. The JGL convenience methods connect application code to the engine publisher. An event can be consumed during dispatch, so ownership of an interaction can be decided before later handlers act.","code":"EventHandler<Event>[] handlers = routes[event.typeId()];\nfor (int i = 0; i < handlers.length; i++) {\n    handlers[i].handle(event);\n    if (event.isConsumed()) {\n        return;\n    }\n}"},{"title":"Adding a new event type","note":"- Pass that constant to the concrete event's Event constructor chain.","code":"EventHandler<KeyPressEvent> listener = event -> {\n    System.out.println(event.getKey());\n};\nJGL.subscribe(EventTypes.KEY_PRESS, listener);\n// Remove the same listener when its owner no longer needs input.\nJGL.unsubscribe(EventTypes.KEY_PRESS, listener);"},{"title":"Event — Constructor","note":"Creates an event permanently associated with the supplied event type.","code":"protected Event(EventType<?> type)"},{"title":"typeId","note":"Returns the dense numeric route ID for this event.","code":"public final int typeId()"},{"title":"isConsumed","note":"Returns whether a handler has consumed the current publication.","code":"public final boolean isConsumed()"}],"art":9,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Events"},{"index":4,"title":"Keyboard, mouse, and cursor input","slug":"02-Core-Input","summary":"Use polling for continuous actions such as movement and events for discrete actions such as clicks or text entry. Keyboard tracks GLFW key and modifier state; Mouse tracks buttons, cursor position, scrolling, and cursor appearance. UI routing adds focus and coordinate conversion on top of these global inputs.","capabilities":[{"title":"Key polling","description":"Tests whether a physical key is currently held; it does not produce correctly composed Unicode text."},{"title":"Text input","description":"Character events carry text for editors and text fields independently of physical key codes."},{"title":"Mouse state","description":"Button and cursor queries support continuous drags and pointer feedback."},{"title":"Cursors and modifiers","description":"Cursor selection changes pointer appearance; modifier masks distinguish shortcut combinations."}],"examples":[{"title":"Keyboard example","note":"This method is useful for lightweight text entry and UI logic where a full text-input callback is not required.","code":"Keyboard.addKeyListener(event -> {\n    if (event instanceof KeyPressEvent && event.getKey() == Keyboard.ENTER) {\n        System.out.println(\"Enter was pressed.\");\n    }\n});\n\nif (Keyboard.isKeyDown(Keyboard.W)) {\n    System.out.println(\"Moving forward.\");\n}\n\nif (Keyboard.isShiftDown()) {\n    System.out.println(\"Sprint modifier active.\");\n}\n\nchar typed = Keyboard.getKeyChar(Keyboard.A);\nif (typed != '\\0') {\n    System.out.println(\"Typed character: \" + typed);\n}\n\nKeyboard.removeKeyListener(myListener);"},{"title":"Mouse example","note":"One important detail of this class is that it stores the raw GLFW Y coordinate internally and converts it to Valthorne's bottom-left style coordinate system when reporting public mouse positions and events. This keeps mouse behavior aligned with the rest of the engine's rendering conventions.","code":"Mouse.init();\n\nMouse.addMouseListener(event -> {\n    if (event instanceof MousePressEvent press && press.getButton() == Mouse.LEFT) {\n        System.out.println(\"Left click at: \" + press.getX() + \", \" + press.getY());\n    }\n});\n\nMouse.addScrollListener(event -> {\n    System.out.println(\"Scroll: \" + event.getXOffset() + \", \" + event.getYOffset());\n});\n\nMouse.setCursor(Mouse.CURSOR_HAND);\nMouse.setCursorMode(Mouse.CURSOR_NORMAL);\n\nif (Mouse.isButtonDown(Mouse.LEFT)) {\n    System.out.println(\"Holding left mouse button\");\n}\n\nshort mouseX = Mouse.getX();\nshort mouseY = Mouse.getY();\n\nMouse.resetScroll();\nMouse.dispose();"},{"title":"UNKNOWN","note":"Unknown key constant.","code":"public static final  int UNKNOWN"},{"title":"SPACE","note":"Space key.","code":"public static final  int SPACE"},{"title":"APOSTROPHE","note":"Apostrophe key (').","code":"public static final  int APOSTROPHE"}],"art":9,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Input"},{"index":5,"title":"Plugin loading and lifecycle","slug":"02-Core-Plugins","summary":"The plugin system discovers and initializes implementations of Plugin from supplied loading locations. PluginLoader coordinates asynchronous loading through an executor, and Plugin defines initialize/unload callbacks. Use this for application extensions whose lifecycle you control.","capabilities":[{"title":"Discovery and loading","description":"The loader inspects supplied files/folders using its supported class-loading mechanism."},{"title":"Initialization","description":"A discovered plugin performs setup in initialize."},{"title":"Loaded registry","description":"Successfully loaded plugins are retained for later access and cleanup."},{"title":"Unloading and shutdown","description":"Plugin cleanup and executor shutdown are distinct responsibilities."},{"title":"Failure context","description":"PluginLoadingException preserves a loading error and its cause."}],"examples":[{"title":"PluginLoader","note":"Example usage:","code":"PluginLoader loader = new PluginLoader(4); // Use 4 threads\nloader.loadFromFolder(new File(\"plugins\"));\nList<Plugin> plugins = loader.getLoadedPlugins();\nloader.shutdown();"},{"title":"initialize","note":"Initializes the plugin after instantiation. Implementations should perform any necessary setup here.","code":"void initialize()"},{"title":"unload","note":"Unloads the plugin and performs any necessary cleanup operations. Implementations should release resources, save states if required, and perform other shutdown tasks as needed.","code":"void unload()"},{"title":"PluginLoader — Constructor","note":"Constructs a PluginLoader with a specified thread pool size.","code":"public PluginLoader(int threadPoolSize)"},{"title":"PluginLoader — Constructor (2)","note":"Constructs a PluginLoader with the default thread pool size (DEFAULT_THREAD_POOL_SIZE).","code":"public PluginLoader()"}],"art":19,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Plugins"},{"index":6,"title":"Portable runtime services","slug":"02-Core-Portable-Runtime","summary":"The valthorne.portable package defines the shared application boundary used by desktop and browser builds. These interfaces keep application code independent of a concrete graphics, physics, input, media, or storage backend.","capabilities":[{"title":"Lifecycle","description":"FrameLoop wraps an Application with bounded fixed-step updates. Call start() once, then frame(elapsedSeconds) for each presentation frame. Large deltas are capped to step * maxSteps, preventing an update spiral. Pausing clears the partial accumulator but continues rendering. close() is idempotent and disposes a started application."},{"title":"Ownership rules","description":"Loaded models, images, sounds, physics bodies, scene backends, platform services, and frame loops are AutoCloseable. Close owned handles explicitly. Scene clearing or backend closure invalidates handles it created. Asset cancellation is idempotent; cancellation before completion reports a terminal failed(\"cancelled\") callback."}],"examples":[{"title":"Run a bounded portable frame loop","note":"FrameLoop starts one Application, caps fixed-step catch-up work, continues rendering while paused, and disposes the application when closed.","code":"var loop = new valthorne.portable.FrameLoop(application, 1f / 60f, 8);\nloop.start();\nloop.frame(elapsedSeconds);\nloop.close();"},{"title":"Application lifecycle and window management — Usage example","note":"Related Application lifecycle and window management example: This complete class launches the engine. Add your selected renderer and resources in the callbacks.","code":"import valthorne.Application;\nimport valthorne.JGL;\n\npublic final class MinimalApplication implements Application {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        JGL.init(new MinimalApplication(), \"Valthorne\", 800, 600);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void init() {\n        // Create application resources after engine initialization.\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void update(float delta) {\n        // Advance simulation using delta seconds.\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void render() {\n        // Submit drawing commands with your chosen renderer.\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void dispose() {\n        // Release resources created by this application.\n    }\n}"},{"title":"Application lifecycle and window management — JGLConfiguration example","note":"Related Application lifecycle and window management example: The actual application of stored values happens inside applyWindowHints(). That method resets GLFW to its default hint state by calling glfwDefaultWindowHints() and then pushes all stored configuration values into GLFW through the appropriate glfwWindowHint and glfwWindowHintString calls.","code":"JGLConfiguration configuration = new JGLConfiguration()\n        .title(\"My Game\")\n        .size(1920, 1080)\n        .samples(4)\n        .openglDebugContext(true)\n        .depthBits(24)\n        .stencilBits(8)\n        .srgbCapable(true);\n\nJGL.init(application, configuration);"},{"title":"Application lifecycle and window management — Window example","note":"Related Application lifecycle and window management example: GLFW window wrapper for Valthorne.","code":"// These are called inside your Application.init() method\nWindow.setSwapInterval(SwapInterval.ON); // vsync\nWindow.setResizable(true);\nWindow.center();\n\nWindow.addWindowResizeListener(evt -> {\n    System.out.println(\"Resize: \" + evt.getOldWidth() + \"x\" + evt.getOldHeight()\n            + \" -> \" + evt.getNewWidth() + \"x\" + evt.getNewHeight());\n});"},{"title":"Application lifecycle and window management — init","note":"Related Application lifecycle and window management example: Initializes the application. This method is called once when the application starts and should contain initialization logic such as creating resources, loading assets, and setting up the initial application state.","code":"void init()"}],"art":12,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Portable-Runtime"},{"index":7,"title":"Application lifecycle and window management","slug":"02-Core-Runtime","summary":"Start here when embedding Valthorne in a desktop application. Application supplies four callbacks; JGL establishes the engine and drives them. Window exposes the active native window, while JGLConfiguration supplies launch settings. Initialize graphics resources after the context exists, update simulation with elapsed seconds, and keep rendering separate from simulation.","capabilities":[{"title":"Application callbacks","description":"init creates application resources, update(delta) advances state, render draws it, and dispose releases what the application owns."},{"title":"Launch configuration","description":"Title, dimensions, context settings, and swap interval describe the initial window and graphics behavior."},{"title":"Main-thread work","description":"JGL.runTask provides a route back to engine execution for work that must leave a loading thread."},{"title":"Window controls","description":"Window operations expose sizing, state, and lifecycle of the current native window."}],"examples":[{"title":"Usage example","note":"This complete class launches the engine. Add your selected renderer and resources in the callbacks.","code":"import valthorne.Application;\nimport valthorne.JGL;\n\npublic final class MinimalApplication implements Application {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        JGL.init(new MinimalApplication(), \"Valthorne\", 800, 600);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void init() {\n        // Create application resources after engine initialization.\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void update(float delta) {\n        // Advance simulation using delta seconds.\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void render() {\n        // Submit drawing commands with your chosen renderer.\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void dispose() {\n        // Release resources created by this application.\n    }\n}"},{"title":"JGLConfiguration example","note":"The actual application of stored values happens inside applyWindowHints(). That method resets GLFW to its default hint state by calling glfwDefaultWindowHints() and then pushes all stored configuration values into GLFW through the appropriate glfwWindowHint and glfwWindowHintString calls.","code":"JGLConfiguration configuration = new JGLConfiguration()\n        .title(\"My Game\")\n        .size(1920, 1080)\n        .samples(4)\n        .openglDebugContext(true)\n        .depthBits(24)\n        .stencilBits(8)\n        .srgbCapable(true);\n\nJGL.init(application, configuration);"},{"title":"Window example","note":"GLFW window wrapper for Valthorne.","code":"// These are called inside your Application.init() method\nWindow.setSwapInterval(SwapInterval.ON); // vsync\nWindow.setResizable(true);\nWindow.center();\n\nWindow.addWindowResizeListener(evt -> {\n    System.out.println(\"Resize: \" + evt.getOldWidth() + \"x\" + evt.getOldHeight()\n            + \" -> \" + evt.getNewWidth() + \"x\" + evt.getNewHeight());\n});"},{"title":"init","note":"Initializes the application. This method is called once when the application starts and should contain initialization logic such as creating resources, loading assets, and setting up the initial application state.","code":"void init()"},{"title":"render","note":"Renders the application's graphics. This method is called once per frame and should contain all rendering logic for displaying the application's visual content.","code":"void render()"}],"art":12,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Runtime"},{"index":8,"title":"Scenes and game screens","slug":"02-Core-Scenes","summary":"Scene groups a screen's update, draw, UI, viewport, and listener infrastructure. GameScreen adapts one active scene to Application. This is appropriate for switching between a menu, gameplay, and a results screen while keeping their resources and input lifetimes distinct.","capabilities":[{"title":"Scene infrastructure","description":"Initialization prepares batch, viewport, UI, and event adapters before scene-specific setup."},{"title":"Draw lifecycle","description":"The scene coordinator controls the shared batch's begin/end sequence."},{"title":"Pause","description":"Pausing suppresses scene updates while rendering may continue."},{"title":"Replacement","description":"Changing the current scene disposes the previous scene; this is not a scene stack or transition animation system."}],"examples":[{"title":"Why this class exists example","note":"Your engine already has an application entry point and a scene abstraction. GameScreen gives those two systems a dedicated coordinator so your game code can stay clean. Instead of putting scene switching logic inside the application itself, you centralize it here.","code":"Scene menuScene = new MainMenuScene();\nGameScreen gameScreen = new GameScreen(menuScene);\n\nJGL.init(gameScreen, \"My Game\", 800, 600);"},{"title":"Pause behavior example","note":"updateScene(float) respects the scene's paused flag. When paused, update logic is skipped, but rendering can still continue. This is useful for pause menus, overlays, and frozen gameplay states where the scene should remain visible but not simulate.","code":"public final class GameplayScene extends Scene {\n\n    @Override\n    public void init() {\n\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void render(TextureBatch batch) {\n\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void update(float delta) {\n\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void dispose() {\n\n    }\n}"},{"title":"SceneKeyListener example","note":"This keeps the scene API clean while still allowing scene input registration through the engine's listener system.","code":"Scene scene = new GameplayScene();\nSceneKeyListener listener = new SceneKeyListener(scene);\n\nKeyboard.addKeyListener(listener);"},{"title":"Forwarded events example","note":"- mouseMoved(MouseMoveEvent)","code":"SceneMouseListener listener = new SceneMouseListener(scene);\nMouse.addMouseListener(listener);"},{"title":"SceneMouseScrollListener example","note":"SceneMouseScrollListener is a simple forwarding wrapper that routes mouse wheel events from the engine input system into a specific Scene. It exists so scenes can react to scroll input through scene-level methods rather than implementing the engine listener contract directly.","code":"SceneMouseScrollListener listener = new SceneMouseScrollListener(scene);\nMouse.addScrollListener(listener);"},{"title":"Typical use example","note":"Scenes commonly use resize events to update their valthorne.viewport.Viewport, reposition cameras, rebuild layout, or refresh screen-space UI.","code":"SceneWindowResizeListener listener = new SceneWindowResizeListener(scene);\nWindow.addWindowResizeListener(listener);"}],"art":12,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Scenes"},{"index":9,"title":"State machine system","slug":"02-Core-State-Machines","summary":"StateMachine models named modes, shared data, lifecycle callbacks, queued triggers, timed transitions, and guarded transitions. It is manually updated and single-threaded.","capabilities":[{"title":"Define states and transitions","description":"machine.state(\"Idle\") .onEnter(ctx -> ctx.data().stop()) .onUpdate((ctx, delta) -> ctx.data().idle(delta));"},{"title":"Transition selection","description":"Local transitions from the current state and transitions created with any() compete together. Eligible transitions are selected by higher priority, then earlier registration order. Trigger and elapsed-time requirements are checked before guards. Multiple when(...) and unless(...) clauses are combined with AND. Only the winning transition consumes its trigger; unconsumed triggers remain queued. Self-transitions are…"},{"title":"Lifecycle and timing","description":"Use pause(), resume(), stop(), end(), reset(), restart(...), or goTo(...) for explicit control. Lifecycle conditions mirror the Tick system:"},{"title":"Reuse a frozen graph","description":"When many entities share the same behavior, define it once and freeze it:"},{"title":"Updating many machines","description":"StateMachineManager updates registered machines in order and avoids normal per-frame allocation:"},{"title":"Callback safety","description":"State updates may fire triggers or change lifecycle. Avoid recursive update calls. Do not recursively change lifecycle from enter, exit, transition-action, or lifecycle-listener callbacks. Exceptions propagate to the caller; mutations completed before an exception remain completed. Configure graphs outside callbacks and keep each machine and manager on one owning thread."}],"examples":[{"title":"Define states and transitions","note":"Call machine.update(delta) once per simulation update. Fire discrete events with machine.fire(\"jump\"). trigger(\"jump\") returns a reusable numeric ID for hot paths, while a Trigger object can also be passed to fire.","code":"StateMachine<Player> machine = new StateMachine<>(player);\n\nmachine.state(\"Idle\")\n        .onEnter(ctx -> ctx.data().stop())\n        .onUpdate((ctx, delta) -> ctx.data().idle(delta));\n\nmachine.state(\"Run\")\n        .onUpdate((ctx, delta) -> ctx.data().move(delta));\n\nmachine.state(\"Jump\")\n        .onEnter(ctx -> ctx.data().jump());\n\nmachine.from(\"Idle\").when(ctx -> ctx.data().isMoving()).to(\"Run\");\nmachine.from(\"Run\").unless(ctx -> ctx.data().isMoving()).to(\"Idle\");\n\nmachine.from(\"Idle\")\n        .on(\"jump\")\n        .when(ctx -> ctx.data().isGrounded())\n        .priority(10)\n        .reason(\"jump requested\")\n        .to(\"Jump\");\n\nmachine.from(\"Jump\")\n        .after(250).milliseconds()\n        .when(ctx -> ctx.data().isGrounded())\n        .to(\"Idle\");\n\nmachine.start(\"Idle\");"},{"title":"Reuse a frozen graph","note":"The immutable graph shares definitions; each created machine keeps independent context, lifecycle, triggers, current state, and elapsed time.","code":"StateMachine<Player> definition = new StateMachine<>();\ndefinition.state(\"Idle\").onUpdate((ctx, delta) -> ctx.data().idle(delta));\ndefinition.state(\"Run\").onUpdate((ctx, delta) -> ctx.data().move(delta));\ndefinition.from(\"Idle\").when(ctx -> ctx.data().isMoving()).to(\"Run\");\ndefinition.from(\"Run\").unless(ctx -> ctx.data().isMoving()).to(\"Idle\");\n\nStateGraph<Player> graph = definition.freeze();\nStateMachine<Player> first = graph.create(firstPlayer).start(\"Idle\");\nStateMachine<Player> second = graph.create(secondPlayer).start(\"Idle\");"},{"title":"Updating many machines","note":"Registration does not start a machine. Additions made during update begin next frame; removing a machine before its turn skips it. removeStopped() is explicit because stopped machines may be restarted.","code":"StateMachineManager machines = new StateMachineManager(128);\nmachines.add(first);\nmachines.add(second);\nmachines.update(delta);"},{"title":"Transition selection","note":"By default, at most one automatic transition occurs per update. maxTransitionsPerUpdate(...) enables bounded chaining when one destination should immediately feed another transition.","code":"machine.any()\n        .when(ctx -> ctx.data().health() <= 0)\n        .priority(100)\n        .then(ctx -> playDefeatSound())\n        .to(\"Dead\");"},{"title":"Lifecycle and timing","note":"All time comes from update(delta). Pausing retains the current state and elapsed time. A zero time scale freezes state updates and transitions but lifecycle conditions still run. Timed transitions use scaled state time and do not carry leftover time into the destination.","code":"machine.pauseIf(ctx -> game.isPaused())\n       .continueIf(ctx -> !game.isPaused())\n       .endIf(ctx -> level.isFinished())\n       .runIf(ctx -> simulationEnabled)\n       .timeScale(0.5f)\n       .onEnd(ctx -> releaseBehaviorResources());"}],"art":12,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-State-Machines"},{"index":10,"title":"Tick system","slug":"02-Core-Timing","summary":"Tick is Valthorne's manually driven timer and scheduler. It does not create a thread or read the wall clock: call update(delta) from the game loop and it runs callbacks on that same thread.","capabilities":[{"title":"Basic timer","description":"spawnTimer.start();"},{"title":"Lifecycle and conditions","description":"The lifecycle is STOPPED, RUNNING, or PAUSED:"},{"title":"Delayed actions","description":"Attach ordered one-shot actions directly to the tick:"},{"title":"Catch-up and time scaling","description":"By default, at most one interval fires per update. Whole missed intervals are counted as skipped, while the fractional remainder is retained. Use catch-up only when every interval matters:"},{"title":"Updating many ticks","description":"TickManager updates an identity-based collection in registration order without allocating during normal updates:"},{"title":"Threading and callback safety","description":"Tick and TickManager are single-threaded and reject recursive updates. A callback may change its tick's lifecycle or timing, which interrupts remaining work when appropriate. Conditions and callbacks run on the thread that calls update; use the engine's normal thread-handoff facilities before touching resources owned by another thread."}],"examples":[{"title":"Basic timer","note":"repeat(5) ends after five callback invocations. Without a repeat limit, the tick continues until a condition or explicit lifecycle call ends it. A zero delay invokes the callback once per eligible update, including a zero-delta update.","code":"Tick spawnTimer = new Tick()\n        .delay(1f)\n        .repeat(5)\n        .callback(tick -> spawnEnemy())\n        .onEnd(tick -> waveFinished());\n\nspawnTimer.start();\n\n// In the update loop; delta is seconds.\nspawnTimer.update(delta);"},{"title":"Lifecycle and conditions","note":"Repeated runIf conditions are combined with AND; repeated conditions of the other kinds are combined with OR. Conditions can be evaluated more than once during an update, so keep them side-effect free. End and repeat limits take precedence over pause, which takes precedence over continue.","code":"Tick regeneration = new Tick()\n        .delay(0.5f)\n        .runIf(tick -> player.isAlive())\n        .skipIf(tick -> player.isAtFullHealth())\n        .pauseIf(tick -> game.isPaused())\n        .continueIf(tick -> !game.isPaused())\n        .endIf(tick -> level.isFinished())\n        .callback(tick -> player.heal(1));"},{"title":"Delayed actions","note":"Schedule time is unscaled. It advances while the tick is running or paused, even when runIf is false or the callback time scale is zero. It freezes while stopped. clearSchedule() removes pending actions, and maxActionsPerUpdate(...) bounds how many overdue actions can run in one frame.","code":"Tick round = new Tick()\n        .delay(1f)\n        .callback(tick -> updateRound())\n        .after(30).seconds().then(tick -> showWarning())\n        .after(1).minutes().thenPause()\n        .after(5).seconds().thenContinue()\n        .after(2).minutes().thenEnd();"},{"title":"Catch-up and time scaling","note":"With catch-up enabled, overdue work remains as backlog when the per-update maximum is reached. Inspect getIterations(), getSkippedIterations(), getElapsed(), getActiveTime(), getProgress(), and getRemaining() for status.","code":"Tick simulation = new Tick()\n        .delay(1f / 60f)\n        .catchUp(true)\n        .maxTicksPerUpdate(4)\n        .timeScale(0.5f)\n        .callback(tick -> simulateStep());"},{"title":"Updating many ticks","note":"Registration does not start a tick. Adding during a manager update takes effect next frame; removing a not-yet-visited tick skips it immediately. removeStopped() is explicit because stopped ticks may be restarted. Do not also update a tick individually after registering it with a manager.","code":"TickManager ticks = new TickManager(64);\nticks.add(spawnTimer);\nticks.add(regeneration);\n\nspawnTimer.start();\nregeneration.start();\n\n// Once per game update:\nticks.update(delta);"}],"art":12,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/02-Core-Timing"},{"index":11,"title":"Frame and transform animation","slug":"03-Graphics-2D-Animation","summary":"Frame animation selects an AnimationFrame over time; transform animation changes a 3D object's pose. Both belong in simulation updates rather than being advanced separately for each render pass. Listeners expose playback events so game logic can react without deriving state from a drawn image.","capabilities":[{"title":"Frame timing","description":"Per-frame durations control when the current frame advances."},{"title":"Playback direction","description":"Forward, reverse, and bidirectional modes define traversal."},{"title":"Looping and completion","description":"Loop limits, pause, and finish state determine whether playback continues."},{"title":"Listeners","description":"Callbacks expose frame and lifecycle changes."},{"title":"3D transforms","description":"TransformAnimation3D interpolates pose data, including quaternion orientation."}],"examples":[{"title":"Animation example","note":"A lightweight, time-driven frame animation player.","code":"// Build frames (each frame has a Drawable + a duration in seconds).\nAnimation walk = new Animation(\n        PlaybackMode.FORWARD,\n        new AnimationFrame(walk0, 0.08f),\n        new AnimationFrame(walk1, 0.08f),\n        new AnimationFrame(walk2, 0.08f),\n        new AnimationFrame(walk3, 0.08f)\n);\n\n// Configure.\nwalk.setLooping(true);     // keep looping forever\nwalk.setSpeed(1.0f);       // 1 = normal speed\n\n// Optional: events.\nwalk.setListener(new AnimationListener() {\n    @Override public void onFrameChanged(Animation a, int from, int to) {\n        // Example: play a footstep sound on certain frames.\n        // if (to == 1 || to == 3) footstep.play();\n    }\n    @Override public void onFinished(Animation a) {\n        // Example: switch state, fire event, etc.\n    }\n    @Override public void onLoop(Animation a, int loopsCompleted) {\n        // Example: count loops for debugging.\n    }\n});\n\nTextureBatch batch = new TextureBatch(512, 50);\n\n// Game loop usage.\nwalk.update(deltaSeconds);\nwalk.draw(batch, playerX, playerY, 64, 64);\n\n// Seek (optional).\nwalk.setTime(0.15f);       // jump to time in seconds along the timeline\nwalk.setFrame(2);          // force a specific frame\nwalk.pause();              // pause update\nwalk.play();               // resume update"},{"title":"restart","note":"This is equivalent to:","code":"reset();\nplay();"},{"title":"TransformAnimation3D example","note":"Samples immutable local-transform keyframes using linear position and scale interpolation and shortest-path quaternion interpolation. Time is measured in seconds. Sampling can clamp to the last frame or wrap over the animation duration; this class stores no playback clock and never advances time on its own.","code":"TransformAnimation3D animation = new TransformAnimation3D(\n        new TransformAnimation3D.Keyframe(0, new Vector3f(),\n                new Quaternionf(), new Vector3f(1, 1, 1)),\n        new TransformAnimation3D.Keyframe(2, new Vector3f(3, 0, 0),\n                new Quaternionf(), new Vector3f(2, 2, 2)));\nanimation.apply(instance, elapsedSeconds, true);"},{"title":"Animation — Constructor","note":"Creates an Animation using a playback mode and a frame list.","code":"public Animation(PlaybackMode playbackMode, AnimationFrame... frames)"},{"title":"Animation — draw","note":"Draws the current animation frame using the specified parameters.","code":"public void draw(TextureBatch batch, float x, float y, float width, float height, float regionX, float regionY, float regionWidth, float regionHeight, float originX, float originY, float rotation, Color tint)"}],"art":6,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/03-Graphics-2D-Animation"},{"index":12,"title":"Bitmap fonts and glyph styling","slug":"03-Graphics-2D-Bitmap-Fonts","summary":"The bitmap font system bakes glyphs into an atlas, measures text, and submits textured glyph quads. FontData contains metrics and atlas data; Font adds text layout, size, color, outlines, and styling. Use this path when an atlas-based font suits the expected display sizes.","capabilities":[{"title":"Baking parameters","description":"Source, size, and character range determine which glyphs are available."},{"title":"Measurement and layout","description":"Line metrics, tabs, advances, and fallback behavior determine positioning."},{"title":"Glyph styling","description":"FontStyler receives glyph context and can alter visibility, transform, or appearance."},{"title":"Outlines","description":"Offset glyph passes build an outline using configurable color and thickness."},{"title":"Cached versus immediate text","description":"Reuse stored layout for repeated strings or draw changing text through the immediate path."}],"examples":[{"title":"Typical usage","note":"The bitmap font system bakes glyphs into an atlas, measures text, and submits textured glyph quads. FontData contains metrics and atlas data; Font adds text layout, size, color, outlines, and styling. Use this path when an atlas-based font suits the expected display sizes.","code":"Font font = new Font(\"assets/fonts/inter.ttf\", 32);\nfont.setText(\"Inventory\");\nfont.setPosition(24f, 680f);\nfont.setColor(new Color(1f, 1f, 1f, 1f));\nfont.setShadow(2f, -2f, new Color(0f, 0f, 0f, 0.65f));\nfont.setOutline(1, new Color(0f, 0f, 0f, 1f));\n\n\nTextureBatch batch = new TextureBatch(4096);\n\nbatch.begin();\nfont.draw(batch);\nbatch.end();\n\nfont.dispose();\nbatch.dispose();"},{"title":"FontStyler","note":"Example usage:","code":"FontStyler styler = (style, ctx) -> {\n    style.setOffset(ctx.getCharIndex() * 2.0f, 0f);\n    style.setScale(1.5f, 1.5f);\n    style.setColor(1.0f, 0.5f, 0.0f, 1.0f);\n};"},{"title":"Font — Constructor","note":"Creates a new font renderer from existing baked FontData.","code":"public Font(FontData data)"},{"title":"Font — Constructor (2)","note":"Loads font data from disk and creates a font renderer from it.","code":"public Font(String path, int fontSize)"},{"title":"getText","note":"Returns the current text assigned to this font instance.","code":"public String getText()"}],"art":16,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/03-Graphics-2D-Bitmap-Fonts"},{"index":13,"title":"LDtk maps","slug":"03-Graphics-2D-LDtk","summary":"Valthorne 2.3.0 can load LDtk projects, external levels, tilesets, backgrounds, IntGrid data, entities, and custom fields. Parsing and image decoding are separated from GPU upload so asset work can stay off the render thread.","capabilities":[{"title":"Asynchronous loading","description":"Register the loader once before requesting an LDtk project. Assets runs uncached loader work on its virtual-thread-per-task executor. The completion callback is not the OpenGL thread, so return to it with JGL.runTask before calling asMap():"},{"title":"Rendering","description":"LdtkMap renders through TextureBatch; no raw OpenGL calls are required:"},{"title":"Gameplay data","description":"Project access includes levels, tilesets keyed by UID, and layer, entity, and enum definitions. Levels expose dimensions, world position, background data, layers, and fields. Layers expose dimensions, grid size, offsets, visibility, tiles, entities, fields, and a defensive copy from intGrid()."},{"title":"In-memory projects","description":"For packaged or downloaded data, provide the root project bytes, a virtual path used for relative resolution, and dependency bytes:"},{"title":"Ownership and disposal","description":"Keep the CPU project alive while its decoded image data is needed. Dispose the GPU map on the OpenGL thread, then dispose the project data when no longer needed:"}],"examples":[{"title":"Asynchronous loading","note":"LdtkLoader parses JSON and decodes referenced images without OpenGL. LdtkMap uploads those decoded tilesets and backgrounds, so construct and dispose it on the engine's OpenGL thread. See Asset loading and caching for the complete virtual-thread and ownership contract.","code":"Assets.addLoader(LdtkParameters.class, new LdtkLoader());\n\nAssets.loadAsync(\n        LdtkParameters.fromPath(\"maps/world.ldtk\", \"world\"),\n        LdtkProject.class\n).thenAccept(project -> JGL.runTask(() -> {\n    worldProject = project;\n    worldMap = project.asMap();\n}));"},{"title":"Rendering","note":"Other useful operations are:","code":"batch.begin();\nworldMap.render(batch, \"Level_0\");\nbatch.end();"},{"title":"Rendering (2)","note":"The renderer converts LDtk's top-left coordinates to Valthorne coordinates, draws the level background, then draws visible tile layers in reverse LDtk order. Grid tiles and auto-layer tiles honor flip bits, opacity, world offsets, layer offsets, spacing, and padding. Entity and IntGrid layers are data for gameplay and are not drawn automatically.","code":"LdtkLevel level = worldProject.level(\"Level_0\");\n\nworldMap.render(batch);                         // every level\nworldMap.render(batch, level);                  // one level\nworldMap.renderBackground(batch, level);        // background only\nworldMap.renderLayer(batch, level, \"Ground\");  // one layer"},{"title":"Gameplay data","note":"Project access includes levels, tilesets keyed by UID, and layer, entity, and enum definitions. Levels expose dimensions, world position, background data, layers, and fields. Layers expose dimensions, grid size, offsets, visibility, tiles, entities, fields, and a defensive copy from intGrid().","code":"LdtkLevel level = worldProject.level(\"Level_0\");\nLdtkLayer collisions = level.layer(\"Collisions\");\nint cell = collisions.intGridAt(column, row);\n\nfor (LdtkEntity entity : level.layer(\"Entities\").entities()) {\n    LdtkField health = entity.field(\"Health\");\n    int startingHealth = health == null ? 100 : health.asInt(100);\n    spawn(entity.identifier(), entity.x(), entity.y(), startingHealth);\n}"},{"title":"In-memory projects","note":"The dependency map is defensively copied. Paths in the LDtk project resolve relative to the supplied virtual project path, matching filesystem loading.","code":"Map<String, byte[]> files = new HashMap<>();\nfiles.put(\"maps/Level_0.ldtkl\", levelBytes);\nfiles.put(\"maps/tiles.png\", tilesetBytes);\n\nLdtkParameters parameters = LdtkParameters.fromBytes(\n        projectBytes,\n        \"maps/world.ldtk\",\n        files,\n        \"world\"\n);"},{"title":"Ownership and disposal","note":"LdtkMap.dispose() is idempotent. Do not create or destroy its textures in an asset-completion callback without the JGL.runTask handoff.","code":"JGL.runTask(() -> {\n    if (worldMap != null) worldMap.dispose();\n    if (worldProject != null) worldProject.dispose();\n});"}],"art":18,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/03-Graphics-2D-LDtk"},{"index":14,"title":"2D particles and spawn distributions","slug":"03-Graphics-2D-Particles","summary":"The 2D particle system combines emitter state, reusable particles, and configurable spawn distributions. Use distributions to shape where particles begin without embedding geometry sampling in the effect update loop. Keep visual resource ownership separate from particle lifetime.","capabilities":[{"title":"Emitter and system","description":"An emitter describes the effect; ParticleSystem coordinates active effects and updates."},{"title":"Particle state","description":"Position, motion, lifetime, and visual values evolve over time."},{"title":"Spawn distributions","description":"Point, line, box, circle, cone, ring, rectangle-edge, spiral, and radial-burst samplers create different birth patterns."},{"title":"Reuse","description":"Particle reuse reduces allocation during repeated effects."}],"examples":[{"title":"Notes example","note":"- The region values are stored in the same coordinate units expected by your Texture.setRegion(...) usage.","code":"ParticleEmitter e = new ParticleEmitter()\n    .setEmissionRate(120f)\n    .setLifetime(0.15f, 0.5f)\n    .setVelocity(40f, 180f)\n    .setAngleDeg(0f, 360f)\n    .setGravity(0f, -350f)\n    .setWind(25f, 0f)\n    .setBaseSize(12f, 12f)\n    .setStartEndScale(1f, 0f)\n    .setRotationSpeed(-90f, 90f)\n    .setStartEndColor(new Color(1f, 0.9f, 0.2f, 1f), new Color(1f, 0.2f, 0f, 0f))\n    .setSpawnCircle(32f, false)\n    .setRegion(0, 0, 16, 16);"},{"title":"Required render state example","note":"- The active window or viewport projection must be configured through the engine before drawing.","code":"Texture smoke = new Texture(\"./assets/particles.png\");\n\nParticleSystem ps = new ParticleSystem(smoke, 20000);\nps.setPosition(0, 0);\n\nps.getEmitter()\n  .setEmissionRate(300)\n  .setLifetime(0.4f, 1.2f)\n  .setVelocity(25f, 140f)\n  .setAngleDeg(70f, 110f)\n  .setGravity(0f, -220f)\n  .setWind(30f, 0f)\n  .setBaseSize(10f, 22f)\n  .setStartEndScale(1.0f, 0.0f)\n  .setRotationSpeed(-180f, 180f)\n  .setSpawnCircle(18f, false);\n\n// In your update loop:\nps.update(delta);\n\n// When you want a burst:\nps.burst(1200, 0.15f);\n\n// Before draw(), ensure your viewport/camera has loaded matrices (like your normal sprite pipeline).\nps.draw();\n\n// Cleanup:\nps.dispose();"},{"title":"BoxSpawnDistributor — Constructor","note":"Creates a centered rectangular distribution, clamping negative half-extents to zero. Nonfinite values are not rejected.","code":"public BoxSpawnDistributor(float halfWidth, float halfHeight)"},{"title":"getHalfWidth","note":"Returns the stored width half-extent.","code":"public float getHalfWidth()"},{"title":"setHalfWidth","note":"Sets the width half-extent, clamping negative values to zero. Nonfinite values are not rejected.","code":"public BoxSpawnDistributor setHalfWidth(float halfWidth)"}],"art":11,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/03-Graphics-2D-Particles"},{"index":15,"title":"Slug vector fonts","slug":"03-Graphics-2D-Slug-Fonts","summary":"Slug renders font outlines from curve and band textures rather than storing only a bitmap glyph image. SlugFont prepares shared curve data, SlugBatch submits glyph instances, and SlugTextRun retains laid-out glyph positions for text drawn repeatedly.","capabilities":[{"title":"Curve coverage","description":"The fragment program evaluates outline coverage from the font's curve data."},{"title":"Instanced glyphs","description":"The batch expands compact glyph records into quads."},{"title":"Reusable runs","description":"Precompute glyph placement, tabs, line breaks, and kerning for stable text."},{"title":"Scale and color","description":"A run or glyph uses world units per em and per-draw color."},{"title":"UI text nodes","description":"SlugLabel retains layout under ordinary or NanoVG containers and borrows a shared font and batch."},{"title":"CPU rejection and counters","description":"Whole-run and glyph bounds avoid submissions outside compatible viewport/clip bounds; counters distinguish accepted glyphs from actual draws."},{"title":"Scoped GL state","description":"Batch end or cancel restores the GL state it changed; texture uploads also restore pixel-unpack and texture-binding state."}],"examples":[{"title":"Optimized retained text and mixed UI","note":"Import valthorne.ui.nodes.SlugLabel. The label honors the active texture batch's translation and clip, uses the current projection and framebuffer viewport, and flushes at renderer boundaries to preserve painter order. Text/size setters update intrinsic layout dimensions. Font and batch are borrowed: dispose them on the graphics thread after the UI is…","code":"SlugBatch textBatch = new SlugBatch(2048); // share across labels\nSlugLabel title = new SlugLabel(font, textBatch, \"Curve-rendered UI\", 28);\npanel.add(title); // regular or NanoVG panel, managed by UIRoot"},{"title":"SlugBatch","note":"Use one graphics context thread. Begin captures the GL state changed by this batch; end flushes pending instances and restores it, while cancel restores it without drawing pending work. Clip and viewport bounds reject outside glyphs and crop intersecting glyph quads on the CPU without changing hardware scissor state.","code":"SlugTextRun run = font.createRun(\"Ready\", 24f);\nbatch.begin(projection, viewportWidth, viewportHeight);\ntry {\n    run.draw(batch, 20f, 40f, Color.WHITE);\n    batch.end();\n} finally {\n    batch.cancel();\n}"},{"title":"SlugFont","note":"The font retains backing bytes for STB fallback kerning queries and owns its two GPU textures. Dispose them with the context current after all drawing completes. Measurement data remains stored after disposal, but drawing then has no valid texture resources. Optional retained text, baseline position, size, and color support Dimensional usage; explicit…","code":"SlugFont font = SlugFont.load(\"assets/font.ttf\");\ntry {\n    font.setText(\"Hello\").setSize(32);\n    font.setPosition(20, 60);\n    font.draw(batch); // Inside the caller's SlugBatch drawing lifecycle.\n} finally {\n    font.dispose();\n}"},{"title":"SlugTextRun","note":"Use this for fair live-rendering benchmarks and for text that is drawn repeatedly. The run removes per-frame layout, newline handling, tab handling, and kerning lookups. It still renders live Slug curves every frame; it does not cache text into a texture.","code":"SlugTextRun run = font.createRun(\"The quick brown fox\", 36f);\n\nbatch.begin(projection, width, height);\nrun.draw(batch, 40f, 180f, Color.WHITE);\nbatch.end();"},{"title":"setClip","note":"Restricts subsequently submitted glyphs to intersection with a world-space rectangle. Outside glyphs are rejected; intersecting quads and their em-space coordinates are cropped before upload. Hardware scissor state and existing queued glyphs are unchanged. The rectangle follows world axes, including under rotated projections.","code":"public void setClip(float x, float y, float width, float height)"}],"art":16,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/03-Graphics-2D-Slug-Fonts"},{"index":16,"title":"Textures, sprites, atlases, and batching","slug":"03-Graphics-2D-Textures","summary":"Use Texture for a GPU image, TextureRegion for a pixel rectangle within it, and Sprite when the draw object also needs transform and tint state. TextureBatch submits many textured quads efficiently. Atlas and nine-patch tools address different problems: packing many images together and resizing UI images while retaining borders.","capabilities":[{"title":"Texture data and upload","description":"TextureData holds decoded pixels; Texture uploads or wraps the GPU image."},{"title":"Regions and sprites","description":"Regions select source pixels; sprites add scale, rotation, flipping, bounds, and color."},{"title":"Batching","description":"Begin, submit, and end group compatible draws; texture limits and state changes can flush queued work."},{"title":"Atlases and packing","description":"Pack images or subregions into shared storage and use returned regions."},{"title":"Nine-patch","description":"Split borders and center areas so panels can resize without stretching every edge equally."},{"title":"Offscreen drawing and clipping","description":"Framebuffers redirect output; scissor and translation stacks constrain or offset batch content."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sprite example","note":"The sprite supports direct rendering through the engine's explicit textured-quad renderer. It can also be consumed by systems like valthorne.graphics.texture.TextureBatch which read sprite state and render it in a batched way.","code":"Sprite sprite = new Sprite(\"assets/player.png\");\nsprite.setPosition(100f, 80f);\nsprite.setSize(64f, 64f);\nsprite.setScale(1.25f, 1.25f);\nsprite.setRotationOriginCenter();\nsprite.setRotation(15f);\nsprite.setFlip(false, true);\nsprite.setColor(new Color(1f, 1f, 1f, 0.9f));\n\nsprite.draw();\n\nTexture texture = sprite.getTexture();\nTextureRegion region = sprite.getRegion();\nFloatBuffer vertices = sprite.getVertexBuffer();\nFloatBuffer uvs = sprite.getUVBuffer();\n\nsprite.reset();\nsprite.dispose();"},{"title":"FrameBuffer example","note":"Simple 2D framebuffer (render target) you can draw to, then draw its color texture like a sprite.","code":"FrameBuffer fbo = new FrameBuffer(1280, 720, true);\n\n// render() binds the FBO, selects its viewport, and restores both afterward.\nfbo.render(() -> {\n    Window.clear3D(Color.BLACK);\n    // draw your world/batches here...\n});\n\n// Present to the window. Valthorne handles the clear operation too.\nWindow.clear(Color.BLACK);\nfbo.draw(0f, 0f, 1280f, 720f);\n\n// If your output is upside-down, use:\n// fbo.drawFlippedY(0f, 0f, 1280f, 720f);\n\n// Cleanup.\nfbo.dispose();"},{"title":"NinePatchTexture example","note":"This class performs immediate textured-quad rendering through the engine's shared shader-driven quad renderer. That means it is useful for standalone drawing and is also compatible with higher-level systems that want to inspect its nine-patch properties and feed them into a batch renderer such as TextureBatch.","code":"NinePatchTexture panel = new NinePatchTexture(\"panel.png\", 8, 8, 8, 8);\npanel.setPosition(100, 80);\npanel.setSize(320, 180);\npanel.setRotationOriginCenter();\npanel.setRotation(5f);\npanel.setColor(new Color(1f, 1f, 1f, 0.95f));\n\npanel.draw();\n\npanel.setFlip(true, false);\npanel.setScale(1.25f, 1.25f);\npanel.draw();\n\npanel.reset();\npanel.dispose();"},{"title":"Texture example","note":"Since this class implements Poolable, it can participate in pooling systems. Its reset() implementation restores the filter back to TextureFilter#NEAREST.","code":"Texture texture = new Texture(\"assets/player.png\");\ntexture.setFilter(TextureFilter.LINEAR);\n\nint width = texture.getWidth();\nint height = texture.getHeight();\n\ntexture.bind();\n\nSprite sprite = texture.sprite();\n\nTextureBatch batch = new TextureBatch(1000);\nbatch.begin();\nbatch.draw(texture, 100, 50, width, height);\nbatch.draw(sprite);\nbatch.end();\n\ntexture.reset();\ntexture.dispose();"},{"title":"TextureAtlas","note":"Each build allocates a separate TextureData and GPU texture and requires a current OpenGL context. The builder retains pending inputs for subsequent builds. The caller owns result cleanup; regions share the result texture. Duplicate keys are accepted during collection and rejected at build time.","code":"TextureAtlas atlas = new TextureAtlas().setPadding(2).setBorder(2);\natlas.add(\"icon\", sourceData);\nTextureAtlas.Result result = atlas.build();\ntry {\n    TextureRegion icon = result.getRegion(\"icon\");\n    // Use icon while the result texture remains alive.\n} finally {\n    result.getAtlasTexture().dispose();\n    result.getAtlasData().dispose();\n}"},{"title":"TextureBatch","note":"This class is intended to be reused every frame rather than recreated constantly. Create it once, use it for the lifetime of the renderer, and dispose it when the graphics context is being shut down.","code":"TextureBatch batch = new TextureBatch(2000);\nTexture texture = new Texture(...);\nTextureRegion region = new TextureRegion(texture);\nSprite sprite = new Sprite(region);\n\nsprite.setX(100);\nsprite.setY(50);\nsprite.setRotation(25f);\n\nbatch.begin();\nbatch.draw(sprite);\nbatch.draw(texture, 300, 100, 64, 64);\n\nbatch.pushTranslation(20, 10);\nbatch.draw(sprite, 400, 200, 96, 96);\nbatch.popTranslation();\n\nbatch.beginScissor(0, 0, 800, 600);\nbatch.drawRegion(region, 50, 50, 128, 128);\nbatch.endScissor();\n\nbatch.end();"},{"title":"TextureBatchContract example","note":"This class also generates shader source strings for the default vertex shader, the default fragment shader, and the reusable fragment preamble used when building custom fragment shaders that still need access to the batch's texture-sampling and clipping logic.","code":"Shader shader = new Shader(\n        TextureBatchContract.defaultVertexShader(),\n        TextureBatchContract.buildDefaultFragmentShader(8)\n);\n\nTextureBatchContract.bindAttributes(shader);\nshader.reload();\nTextureBatchContract.bindSamplers(shader, 8);"},{"title":"Typical usage example","note":"The most common use case is creating a fragment effect while still using the default batch vertex shader and batch sampler setup.","code":"TextureBatchShader shader = new TextureBatchShader(\n    8,\n    ShaderSources.load(\"examples/batch-tint.frag.glsl\")\n);\n\nTextureBatch batch = new TextureBatch(4096, 8);\nbatch.begin();\nbatch.setShader(shader);\nbatch.draw(texture, 100f, 100f, 64f, 64f);\nbatch.end();"},{"title":"TexturePacker example","note":"CPU-side texture atlas builder that copies pixels from existing TextureData, Texture, or TextureRegion sources into a new RGBA8 atlas buffer.","code":"// Suppose you already have CPU-side data for several textures/regions.\nTexture player = new Texture(\"assets/player.png\");       // Must have CPU pixels via getData()\nTexture ui     = new Texture(\"assets/ui.png\");\n\nTexturePacker packer = new TexturePacker(1024, 1024);\n\n// Copy a raw rectangle from a texture into the atlas at (0,0).\npacker.addRegion(player,  0, 0, 128, 128,   0,   0);\n\n// Copy a TextureRegion into the atlas at (256,0).\nTextureRegion button = new TextureRegion(ui, 16, 16, 64, 32);\npacker.addRegion(button, 256, 0);\n\n// Bake CPU atlas pixels.\nTextureData atlasData = packer.bake();\n\n// Upload to GPU (your rendering layer responsibility).\nTexture atlas = new Texture(atlasData);"},{"title":"TextureRegion example","note":"Regions can be freely modified after creation. Changing the region updates the pixel bounds used to compute UV coordinates but does not affect the underlying texture.","code":"Texture texture = new Texture(\"spritesheet.png\");\n\nTextureRegion playerIdle = new TextureRegion(texture, 0, 0, 32, 32);\nTextureRegion playerWalk = new TextureRegion(texture, 32, 0, 32, 32);\n\nfloat u = playerIdle.getU();\nfloat v = playerIdle.getV();\nfloat u2 = playerIdle.getU2();\nfloat v2 = playerIdle.getV2();\n\nplayerWalk.setRegion(64, 0, 32, 32);\n\nTextureBatch batch = new TextureBatch(1000);\nbatch.begin();\nbatch.drawRegion(playerIdle.getTexture(), 100, 100,\n                 playerIdle.getRegionWidth(),\n                 playerIdle.getRegionHeight(),\n                 playerIdle.getRegionX(),\n                 playerIdle.getRegionY(),\n                 playerIdle.getRegionWidth(),\n                 playerIdle.getRegionHeight());\nbatch.end();"}],"art":17,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/03-Graphics-2D-Textures"},{"index":17,"title":"Tiled maps and tilesets","slug":"03-Graphics-2D-Tiled-Maps","summary":"The Tiled integration separates parsed TMX/TSX data from runtime map resources. Sources and dependency resolvers supply the map, external tilesets, and images; TiledMap creates the runtime representation. Layers include finite tile data, chunks, objects, and images.","capabilities":[{"title":"Sources and resolvers","description":"Filesystem, classpath, and in-memory dependency lookup support different packaging strategies."},{"title":"Layer types","description":"Tile, image, and object layers carry different data and rendering responsibilities."},{"title":"Tile resolution","description":"Global IDs map to tilesets and local definitions, retaining flip flags when required."},{"title":"Properties and animations","description":"Custom properties and tile-animation frames carry authored content metadata."},{"title":"Encoded data","description":"CSV and Base64 payloads support specific compression paths."}],"examples":[{"title":"Resource ownership example","note":"This map owns the runtime TileSet instances it creates from TileSetData. Because of that, calling dispose() will dispose any tileset textures held by those runtime tilesets. This makes the map responsible for cleaning up its own texture resources when it is no longer needed.","code":"TiledMapData mapData = TiledMapLoader.load(\"assets/maps/world.tmx\", resolver);\nTiledMap map = new TiledMap(mapData);\n\nTextureBatch batch = new TextureBatch(4096);\n\n// Game loop\nmap.update(delta);\n\nbatch.begin();\nmap.render(batch);\nbatch.end();\n\n// Render only one named layer if needed\nbatch.begin();\nmap.renderLayer(batch, \"Foreground\");\nbatch.end();\n\nmap.dispose();"},{"title":"Supported TMX content example","note":"Unknown or unsupported tags are skipped safely so the parser can continue reading known content without failing immediately on extra data.","code":"TiledDependencyResolver resolver = new FileSystemResolver();\n\nTiledMapData data = TiledMapData.load(\"assets/map/TestMap.tmx\", resolver);\n\n// Later, on the OpenGL thread:\nTiledMap map = new TiledMap(data);"},{"title":"Convenience creation methods example","note":"- Load from classpath resources using an in-memory dependency map","code":"TiledMapParameters paramsA = TiledMapParameters.fromPath(\"assets/map/TestMap.tmx\", \"test-map\");\n\nMap<String, byte[]> files = new HashMap<>();\nfiles.put(\"assets/map/TestMap.tmx\", ValthorneFiles.readBytes(\"assets/map/TestMap.tmx\"));\nfiles.put(\"assets/map/Tiles.tsx\", ValthorneFiles.readBytes(\"assets/map/Tiles.tsx\"));\nfiles.put(\"assets/map/Tiles.png\", ValthorneFiles.readBytes(\"assets/map/Tiles.png\"));\n\nTiledMapParameters paramsB = TiledMapParameters.fromBytes(\n        files.get(\"assets/map/TestMap.tmx\"),\n        \"assets/map/TestMap.tmx\",\n        files,\n        \"test-map\"\n);"},{"title":"Virtual path usage example","note":"When loading from bytes, Tiled dependencies such as external TSX files or referenced images may still be resolved relative to a logical parent path. For that reason, BytesSource stores both the TMX bytes and a virtual path string.","code":"TiledMapSource sourceA = new TiledMapSource.PathSource(\"assets/maps/world.tmx\");\n\nbyte[] bytes = Files.readAllBytes(Path.of(\"assets/maps/world.tmx\"));\nTiledMapSource sourceB = new TiledMapSource.BytesSource(bytes, \"assets/maps/world.tmx\");"},{"title":"Caching example","note":"Texture regions are generated lazily and stored in regionCache. This avoids recalculating source rectangles for the same local tile ID over and over during rendering.","code":"TileSetData data = ...;\nTileSet tileSet = TileSet.fromData(data);\n\nint gid = 37;\nint localId = gid - tileSet.getFirstGlobalTileID();\n\nint drawId = tileSet.resolveAnimatedLocalId(localId, timeSeconds);\nTextureRegion region = tileSet.getRegionForLocalId(drawId);\n\nbatch.draw(region, 100, 100, tileSet.getTileWidth(), tileSet.getTileHeight());"},{"title":"Image dependency support example","note":"The tileset image is resolved through TiledDependencyResolver, which means the image does not need to come from the file system directly. It can come from disk, memory, cache archives, classpath resources, or any custom source supported by the resolver.","code":"TiledDependencyResolver resolver = new FileSystemResolver();\n\nTileSetData tileSetData = TileSetData.load(\n        tmxBytes,\n        \"assets/maps/world.tmx\",\n        resolver,\n        reader\n);\n\nint firstGid = tileSetData.getFirstGlobalTileID();\nint tileWidth = tileSetData.getTileWidth();\nTextureData image = tileSetData.getTextureData();"}],"art":18,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/03-Graphics-2D-Tiled-Maps"},{"index":18,"title":"Conservative 3D visibility and occlusion","slug":"04-Graphics-3D-Culling","summary":"OcclusionCuller3D answers whether a conservative bound can be rejected from the current camera pass. It projects actual opaque triangles and rejects a candidate only when its expanded projected rectangle lies strictly behind and inside one retained triangle. Use it to reduce eligible draw submissions while preserving geometry needed by shadows, reflections, refraction, and other passes.","capabilities":[{"title":"Camera-pass reset","description":"begin captures the current view-projection matrix, validates positive viewport dimensions, and discards prior-frame occluders."},{"title":"Opaque triangle collection","description":"addOccluder samples actual untextured, opaque, depth-tested, depth-writing, non-transmissive triangles that are not back-face-culled."},{"title":"Model and world bounds","description":"Test a model with its world transform or an already conservative world-space AABB. Neither overload owns or changes the source geometry."},{"title":"Three visibility outcomes","description":"VISIBLE includes uncertain results; OFFSCREEN proves a shared outside clip plane; OCCLUDED proves single-triangle coverage and depth separation."},{"title":"Bounded CPU work","description":"At most 2048 source triangles are examined and 128 sufficiently large projected triangles are retained per pass."},{"title":"Renderer integration","description":"ModelBatch3D uses submission order; Filament collects occluders before classification and preserves secondary-effect contributors."}],"examples":[{"title":"OcclusionCuller3D","note":"The camera matrix must already be rebuilt. Bounds and occluders must match the geometry actually drawn in this pass; repeat begin after camera or viewport changes.","code":"OcclusionCuller3D culler = new OcclusionCuller3D();\nculler.begin(camera.getCombined(), viewportWidth, viewportHeight);\nculler.addOccluder(wallModel, wallTransform, wallMaterial);\nif (culler.test(candidateModel, candidateTransform) == OcclusionCuller3D.Visibility.VISIBLE) {\n    // Submit the candidate to this camera pass.\n}"},{"title":"begin","note":"Resets fixed-size scratch for a camera pass; no previous-frame decisions survive.","code":"public void begin(Matrix4fc viewProjection, int width, int height)"},{"title":"getOccluderCount","note":"Reads retained occluder count without testing another candidate. The count is zero before the first begin and is reset at each subsequent begin.","code":"public int getOccluderCount()"},{"title":"addOccluder","note":"Adds a bounded sample of actual triangles, without reading textures or allocating. Textured, transparent, transmissive, back-face-culled and non-depth-writing materials are conservatively excluded. At most 2048 triangles are examined and 128 sufficiently large projected triangles retained per pass.","code":"public void addOccluder(Model3D model, Matrix4fc world, Material3D material)"},{"title":"test","note":"Tests a model's conservative bounds against the camera and accepted occluders.","code":"public Visibility test(Model3D model, Matrix4fc world)"}],"art":0,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-3D-Culling"},{"index":19,"title":"Filament desktop renderer","slug":"04-Graphics-3D-Filament","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run demo launch tasks there; the engine's local src/examples/ files remain ignored and excluded from library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"Engine integration","description":"Use SceneRenderer3D for ordinary application code: it performs capability and platform selection and owns the selected renderer. Request Backend.FILAMENT only when the application requires Filament-specific output and should fail on an unsupported system. Access renderer.filament() only for backend-specific quality, exposure, environment, or anti-aliasing controls after confirming that Filament is active."},{"title":"Lighting contract and limits","description":"- Emissive model instances create a point light at their world origin by default, using linear emission RGB and 1000 lumens per emission unit. Material3D.setEmissionLightEnabled(false) keeps the visible glow without an implicit point light, for example when a particle already owns an explicit light. Explicit Scene3D point lights follow their own position, intensity, range and shadow settings. The renderer respects…"},{"title":"Rebuild assets","description":"Material source and compiled packages are committed under src/main/resources/valthorne/filament. To edit them, obtain matc from Filament 1.75.0, then run:"},{"title":"Validation and measurements","description":"The native regression exercises shared-texture rendering, a red point light changing from lit to dark (102 → 0 in the sampled red channel), scene-resource eviction, resize, caller GL state, imported alpha-cutout edits and borrowed-texture survival. Full build, 3D, physics and UI suites and the studio's native input smoke test passed."}],"examples":[{"title":"Engine integration","note":"Use SceneRenderer3D for ordinary application code: it performs capability and platform selection and owns the selected renderer. Request Backend.FILAMENT only when the application requires Filament-specific output and should fail on an unsupported system. Access renderer.filament() only for backend-specific quality, exposure, environment, or anti-aliasing…","code":"// AUTO selects Filament when supported and the raster backend otherwise.\nSceneRenderer3D renderer = new SceneRenderer3D();\nrenderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n// Before destroying the current graphics context:\nrenderer.close();"},{"title":"3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — BillboardBatch3D","note":"Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Construction, rendering, and disposal require a current OpenGL context. Rendering restores captured GL state; construction leaves array bindings at zero. The shader, vertex array, and GPU buffer are owned; textures, sprites, cameras, and materials remain borrowed. Shared scratch vectors preclude concurrent use.","code":"BillboardBatch3D batch = new BillboardBatch3D();\ntry {\n    batch.begin(sprite.getTextureRegion().getTexture());\n    batch.draw(sprite, camera);\n    batch.render(new MeshRenderState3D().setCamera(camera));\n} finally {\n    batch.dispose();\n}"},{"title":"3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — Material3D","note":"Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Tint and emissive colors are owned mutable objects exposed directly by getters. Textures are borrowed and never disposed here; copies share the texture but have independent color storage. Sharing a material shares all later mutations. Set render pass before an explicit depth-write override, because pass assignment resets that flag. Instances provide no…","code":"Material3D material = new Material3D()\n        .setRenderPass(RenderPass3D.TRANSLUCENT)\n        .setTint(new Color(1f, 1f, 1f, 0.5f))\n        .setDepthWrite(false);"},{"title":"3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — MeshBatch3D","note":"Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Construction, upload, rendering, and disposal require a current OpenGL context. Public rendering and upload methods restore the state covered by RenderStateSnapshot3D; construction leaves array bindings at zero. The batch owns its shaders and GPU objects, but borrows models, textures, materials, cameras, and lighting. Mutable scratch storage makes it…","code":"MeshBatch3D mesh = new MeshBatch3D();\ntry {\n    mesh.begin();\n    mesh.box(0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 2, 0, color);\n    mesh.upload();\n    mesh.renderUploaded(new MeshRenderState3D().setCamera(camera));\n} finally {\n    mesh.dispose();\n}"},{"title":"3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — ModelBatch3D","note":"Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Construction and rendering use GPU resources on the owning graphics thread. Material snapshots are cached by source identity per frame, so the first submission using a material determines the snapshot reused by later submissions of that same source. Custom renderable geometry remains borrowed until end or cancellation.","code":"ModelBatch3D batch = new ModelBatch3D();\nbatch.begin(state);\ntry {\n    batch.submit(instance);\n    batch.end();\n} finally {\n    batch.cancel();\n}\n// After the batch is no longer needed:\nbatch.close();"}],"art":0,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-3D-Filament"},{"index":20,"title":"3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards","slug":"04-Graphics-3D-Models","summary":"The 3D model system separates reusable geometry from instances, materials, scene organization, and rendering. Model3D is shared mesh data; ModelInstance3D adds transform state. SceneNode3D builds a hierarchy, Scene3D collects scene content, and render batches submit geometry with the required pass and state.","capabilities":[{"title":"Geometry creation","description":"ModelBuilder3D creates common meshes; OBJ/MTL loading imports grouped materials and geometry."},{"title":"Instances and hierarchy","description":"Instances and parent transforms let multiple objects share geometry."},{"title":"Materials and passes","description":"Material3D selects appearance inputs and opaque/transparent behavior."},{"title":"Procedural geometry","description":"ProceduralRenderable3D emits geometry through a callback without requiring a fixed imported mesh."},{"title":"Billboards","description":"Billboard sprites orient textured quads toward a camera using a selected facing mode."},{"title":"Picking and bounds","description":"World bounds and pick results connect camera rays to scene selection."},{"title":"Conservative camera culling","description":"Frustum and opaque-triangle tests avoid eligible submissions while shadow passes retain their required geometry."}],"examples":[{"title":"BillboardBatch3D","note":"Construction, rendering, and disposal require a current OpenGL context. Rendering restores captured GL state; construction leaves array bindings at zero. The shader, vertex array, and GPU buffer are owned; textures, sprites, cameras, and materials remain borrowed. Shared scratch vectors preclude concurrent use.","code":"BillboardBatch3D batch = new BillboardBatch3D();\ntry {\n    batch.begin(sprite.getTextureRegion().getTexture());\n    batch.draw(sprite, camera);\n    batch.render(new MeshRenderState3D().setCamera(camera));\n} finally {\n    batch.dispose();\n}"},{"title":"Material3D","note":"Tint and emissive colors are owned mutable objects exposed directly by getters. Textures are borrowed and never disposed here; copies share the texture but have independent color storage. Sharing a material shares all later mutations. Set render pass before an explicit depth-write override, because pass assignment resets that flag. Instances provide no…","code":"Material3D material = new Material3D()\n        .setRenderPass(RenderPass3D.TRANSLUCENT)\n        .setTint(new Color(1f, 1f, 1f, 0.5f))\n        .setDepthWrite(false);"},{"title":"MeshBatch3D","note":"Construction, upload, rendering, and disposal require a current OpenGL context. Public rendering and upload methods restore the state covered by RenderStateSnapshot3D; construction leaves array bindings at zero. The batch owns its shaders and GPU objects, but borrows models, textures, materials, cameras, and lighting. Mutable scratch storage makes it…","code":"MeshBatch3D mesh = new MeshBatch3D();\ntry {\n    mesh.begin();\n    mesh.box(0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 2, 0, color);\n    mesh.upload();\n    mesh.renderUploaded(new MeshRenderState3D().setCamera(camera));\n} finally {\n    mesh.dispose();\n}"},{"title":"ModelBatch3D","note":"Construction and rendering use GPU resources on the owning graphics thread. Material snapshots are cached by source identity per frame, so the first submission using a material determines the snapshot reused by later submissions of that same source. Custom renderable geometry remains borrowed until end or cancellation.","code":"ModelBatch3D batch = new ModelBatch3D();\nbatch.begin(state);\ntry {\n    batch.submit(instance);\n    batch.end();\n} finally {\n    batch.cancel();\n}\n// After the batch is no longer needed:\nbatch.close();"},{"title":"ModelBuilder3D","note":"No graphics context or GPU allocation is required. The builder is mutable and unsynchronized; completed models provide protected geometry access.","code":"Model3D floor = ModelBuilder3D.plane(10, 10);\nModel3D column = ModelBuilder3D.cylinder(0.5f, 3, 24);\nModelBuilder3D builder = new ModelBuilder3D();\nbuilder.triangle(new Vector3f(), new Vector3f(1, 0, 0),\n        new Vector3f(0, 1, 0), Color.WHITE);\nModel3D custom = builder.build();"},{"title":"ModelInstance3D","note":"Use on one thread while rendering or querying. Bounds access returns live cached storage that callers must not mutate. Transform validation is partly deferred: non-finite local values and zero scale introduced through live vectors fail when the world transform is next evaluated. This instance owns no GPU resources.","code":"ModelInstance3D instance = new ModelInstance3D()\n        .setModel(ModelBuilder3D.box(1, 1, 1))\n        .setPosition(3, 0, 0)\n        .setRotation(0, 0, 0.5f);\nAABBf bounds = instance.getWorldBounds();"},{"title":"Coordinate Conversion","note":"The record is immutable, but retains the resolver by reference. A custom resolver used for asynchronous loads must support the access pattern of those loads. Constructing parameters neither checks resource existence nor uploads GPU textures. Use distinct asset keys for variants that must coexist.","code":"ModelParameters parameters = ModelParameters.fromPath(\"models/tree.obj\", \"tree\")\n        .withConversion(true);\nObjModel3D model = new ModelLoader().load(parameters);\n// Upload textures later on the render thread, and dispose the model when done."},{"title":"ObjModel3D","note":"The convenience loader converts Y-up positions to Z-up, centers the X/Y footprint, and places the lowest point at Z zero. Disable that option to retain source coordinates. File references are resolved relative to their declaring OBJ or MTL path; a custom resolver can supply bytes from another storage source.","code":"ObjModel3D model = ObjModel3D.load(\"assets/scene.obj\", false);\ntry {\n    model.uploadTextures(); // With the rendering context current.\n    // Submit the model through the scene's model rendering path.\n} finally {\n    model.dispose();\n}"},{"title":"ProceduralRenderable3D","note":"Position, scale, and local bounds are exposed as live mutable objects. Transform evaluation notices direct position and scale edits, and world bounds are rebuilt from all eight local corners when requested. Material and emitter references are borrowed. This class owns no GPU resources and is not thread-safe.","code":"ProceduralRenderable3D surface = new ProceduralRenderable3D()\n        .setEmitter(emitter)\n        .setLocalBounds(-1, -1, 0, 1, 1, 1)\n        .setPosition(4, 2, 0)\n        .setScale(2);\nVector3f worldPoint = surface.transform(new Vector3f(0, 0, 1), new Vector3f());"},{"title":"RenderStateSnapshot3D","note":"Covered state includes depth testing and writes, blending factors and equations, face culling and winding, framebuffer sRGB enablement, shader program, vertex array and array-buffer bindings, selected texture bindings, and the sampler on unit two. Texture coverage is 2D bindings on units zero through two and buffer textures on units three and four. The…","code":"try (RenderStateSnapshot3D state = new RenderStateSnapshot3D()) {\n    // Issue rendering commands that modify the state covered by this snapshot.\n}"},{"title":"Scene3D example","note":"Groups loose renderables and root-node hierarchies for submission and model picking. Membership is stored in insertion order, with loose renderables traversed before root nodes. The rendering batch remains responsible for culling, material grouping and final pass ordering.","code":"Scene3D scene = new Scene3D();\nscene.add(instance);\nscene.addNode(rootNode);\nboolean submittedVisibleObjects = scene.render(batch, renderState);\nPickResult3D hit = scene.pick(ray);"},{"title":"SceneNode3D","note":"Submission prepares world transforms and aggregate bounds before traversing visible model nodes. Internal matrices and scratch instances are reused, so graph mutation and traversal must remain on one thread and must not be reentrant.","code":"SceneNode3D assembly = new SceneNode3D().setPosition(3, 0, 0);\nassembly.addChild(new SceneNode3D()\n        .setModel(ModelBuilder3D.box(1, 1, 1)).setPosition(0, 0, 2));\nbatch.begin(state);\nassembly.submit(batch);\nbatch.end();"}],"art":0,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-3D-Models"},{"index":21,"title":"3D in Valthorne","slug":"04-Graphics-3D-Overview","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"A scene in an Application","description":"Create GPU objects in Application.init(), after JGL creates the context. Request a depth buffer with JGLConfiguration.defaults().depthBits(24)."},{"title":"Coordinates and transforms","description":"Built-in geometry uses right-handed, Z-up coordinates; XY is the ground plane. Triangle fronts are counterclockwise. Camera defaults remain compatible with the 2D XY plane (Y-up, looking along -Z). Use the six-argument lookAt above to orient a Z-up scene. Screen projection and picking use a bottom-left origin, matching Mouse.getY()."},{"title":"Materials, textures and transparency","description":"Meshes interpolate UVs and per-vertex normals. The built-in triangle constructor without normals generates flat face normals. Spheres and cylinder sides have smooth normals. setLightingMix(0) makes a material unlit. Emissive color, fog contribution, radiance contribution, culling, alpha cutoff, depth test and depth writes are configurable."},{"title":"OBJ assets","description":"ModelParameters.fromClasspath(\"models/ship.obj\", \"ship\") loads packaged assets. The record also accepts a custom ObjModel3D.Resolver for memory, archives or other sources; the resolver receives each dependency path relative to its referring OBJ/MTL. Coordinates are preserved by default. Use .withConversion(true) to swap Y/Z, center horizontally and ground the model. The existing ObjModel3D.load(path) convenience…"},{"title":"Lighting and shadows","description":"The render state provides ambient and directional color, plus up to eight point lights with range, color and intensity. A light direction points toward the light."},{"title":"Sprites, particles and animation","description":"BillboardSprite3D accepts existing TextureRegion objects, including atlas regions or frames selected by the 2D animation system. Choose spherical camera-facing orientation or cylindrical Z-up orientation; set position, size, anchor, tint and material."},{"title":"Picking and resource ownership","description":"Picking returns the closest model triangle in world units, including transformed nodes. The result includes the world hit position and node (for hierarchical models). Hidden models/subtrees are excluded. Picking is geometric; it does not sample alpha textures or pick billboards. JOML Intersectionf and AABBf provide ray/triangle and ray/bounds tests."}],"examples":[{"title":"A scene in an Application","note":"In update(delta), move or animate models. In render():","code":"PerspectiveCamera camera = new PerspectiveCamera();\ncamera.setPosition(6, -8, 5);\ncamera.lookAt(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1);\ncamera.setClipPlanes(0.1f, 100f);\n\nPerspectiveViewport viewport = new PerspectiveViewport(\n        Window.getWidth(), Window.getHeight(), camera);\nModelBatch3D batch = new ModelBatch3D();\nMeshRenderState3D state = new MeshRenderState3D().setCamera(camera)\n        .setLightDirection(3, -4, 7)\n        .setAmbientLight(new Color(0.2f, 0.2f, 0.2f, 1))\n        .setDirectionalLight(Color.WHITE)\n        .setFog(20, 80, 0.7f);\n\nModelInstance3D cube = new ModelInstance3D()\n        .setModel(ModelBuilder3D.box(2, 2, 2))\n        .setMaterial(new Material3D().setTint(Color.BLUE));\nScene3D scene = new Scene3D();\nscene.add(cube);"},{"title":"A scene in an Application (2)","note":"Window.clear3D clears both color and depth, including when the previous pass disabled depth writes. Window.clear retains its color-only behavior. A FrameBuffer(width, height, true) also supports the 3D renderer.","code":"// Skip rendering while minimized (zero framebuffer dimensions).\nviewport.update(Window.getWidth(), Window.getHeight());\nWindow.clear3D(Color.BLACK);\nviewport.renderWithOverlay(\n        () -> scene.render(batch, state),\n        () -> { /* existing TextureBatch, fonts, shapes, or UI */ });"},{"title":"Materials, textures and transparency","note":"Meshes interpolate UVs and per-vertex normals. The built-in triangle constructor without normals generates flat face normals. Spheres and cylinder sides have smooth normals. setLightingMix(0) makes a material unlit. Emissive color, fog contribution, radiance contribution, culling, alpha cutoff, depth test and depth writes are configurable.","code":"Texture diffuse = new Texture(\"assets/crate.png\");\nMaterial3D material = new Material3D().setTexture(diffuse)\n        .setTint(Color.WHITE).setCullBackFaces(true);\ncube.setMaterial(material);\n\nMaterial3D glass = new Material3D()\n        .setRenderPass(RenderPass3D.TRANSLUCENT)\n        .setTint(new Color(0.5f, 0.8f, 1f, 0.35f));"},{"title":"OBJ assets","note":"ModelParameters.fromClasspath(\"models/ship.obj\", \"ship\") loads packaged assets. The record also accepts a custom ObjModel3D.Resolver for memory, archives or other sources; the resolver receives each dependency path relative to its referring OBJ/MTL. Coordinates are preserved by default. Use .withConversion(true) to swap Y/Z, center horizontally and ground…","code":"Assets.loadAsync(ModelParameters.fromPath(\"assets/ship.obj\", \"ship\"), ObjModel3D.class)\n        .thenAccept(model -> JGL.runTask(() ->\n                scene.add(new ModelInstance3D().setModel(model))));"},{"title":"Lighting and shadows","note":"The light camera defines a directional shadow volume. Fit its height and clip planes to the region that needs shadows. The map supports opaque/alpha-cutout meshes, 3x3 PCF filtering, configurable bias and strength, and per-material casting/receiving flags. Transparent objects and billboards do not cast shadows. Point lights are unshadowed. The shadow pass…","code":"state.addLight(new PointLight3D().setPosition(2, -1, 3)\n        .setColor(Color.RED).setRange(10).setIntensity(2));\n\nShadowMap3D shadows = new ShadowMap3D(1024);\nshadows.getCamera().setPosition(5, -5, 10);\nshadows.getCamera().lookAt(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1);\nshadows.getCamera().setWorldHeight(20);\nshadows.getCamera().setClipPlanes(0.1f, 100);\nstate.setLightDirection(5, -5, 10).setShadowMap(shadows);\n\n// Each frame before the main scene draw:\nshadows.render(scene);"},{"title":"Sprites, particles and animation","note":"Emitters pool particles, enforce capacity, support bursts and continuous emission, integrate velocity/acceleration and expire particles. Call setEmitting(false) to stop new births while existing particles finish; clear() removes them immediately.","code":"ParticleEmitter3D emitter = new ParticleEmitter3D(500, particle -> {\n    particle.setLifetime(2);\n    particle.getPosition().set(0, 0, 1);\n    particle.getVelocity().set(0, 0, 3);\n    particle.getAcceleration().set(0, 0, -2);\n    particle.getSprite().setTextureRegion(sparkRegion).setSize(0.2f, 0.2f);\n}).setEmissionRate(50);\n// update: emitter.update(delta);\n// between batch.begin/end: emitter.submit(batch);"},{"title":"Sprites, particles and animation (2)","note":"TransformAnimation3D applies reusable local transform tracks to instances or scene nodes. Keyframes start at time zero and have strictly increasing times, finite positions, unit quaternion orientations and nonzero scales. Position/scale interpolate linearly; rotation uses shortest-path SLERP. Scale signs cannot change between frames.","code":"TransformAnimation3D motion = new TransformAnimation3D(\n    new TransformAnimation3D.Keyframe(0, new Vector3f(), new Quaternionf(), new Vector3f(1, 1, 1)),\n    new TransformAnimation3D.Keyframe(2, new Vector3f(0, 0, 3),\n        new Quaternionf().rotationAxis(1.5f, 0, 0, 1), new Vector3f(1, 1, 1)));\nmotion.apply(cube, elapsedSeconds, true); // true loops; false clamps at the final frame"},{"title":"Picking and resource ownership","note":"Picking returns the closest model triangle in world units, including transformed nodes. The result includes the world hit position and node (for hierarchical models). Hidden models/subtrees are excluded. Picking is geometric; it does not sample alpha textures or pick billboards. JOML Intersectionf and AABBf provide ray/triangle and ray/bounds tests.","code":"Rayf ray = viewport.screenToRay(Mouse.getX(), Mouse.getY());\nPickResult3D hit = ray == null ? null : scene.pick(ray);"}],"art":0,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-3D-Overview"},{"index":22,"title":"3D particles and physics integration","slug":"04-Graphics-3D-Particles-API","summary":"ParticleEmitter3D manages a capacity-limited pool of reusable particle instances. Cosmetic particles use analytic constant-acceleration motion; optional physics integration gives particles Jolt bodies. A particle can contribute a mesh or billboard and configure appearance through its material.","capabilities":[{"title":"Spawn initialization","description":"A callback configures each recycled particle's lifetime, motion, and visual references."},{"title":"Rate and bursts","description":"Automatic emission and explicit bursts serve continuous and instantaneous effects."},{"title":"Visual modes","description":"Mesh particles can attach to Scene3D; billboards submit through the batch path."},{"title":"Physics mode","description":"A settings factory creates bodies and advances particle lifetime on the world clock."},{"title":"Pooling","description":"Released particles are reset and reused rather than permanently retained."}],"examples":[{"title":"ParticleEmitter3D","note":"The initializer and physics factory run synchronously for each spawn. They must not reenter or modify the emitter. Use one owning thread (the physics world's thread when attached), and treat pooled particle references as temporary. Closing releases owned bodies and scene membership, never borrowed resources.","code":"ParticleEmitter3D sparks = new ParticleEmitter3D(256, particle -> {\n    particle.setLifetime(0.8f);\n    particle.getPosition().set(0f, 0f, 1f);\n    particle.getVelocity().set(1f, 0f, 3f);\n    particle.getAcceleration().set(0f, 0f, -9.8f);\n    // Configure a borrowed sprite texture or model here.\n});\nsparks.setEmissionRate(40f);\n// Each frame, with a batch already begun:\nsparks.update(deltaSeconds);\nsparks.submit(batch);\n// Release emitter-owned state when the effect is no longer needed:\nsparks.close();"},{"title":"getLight","note":"Lazily creates and returns this particle's owned point light without enabling it. Defaults are white, range 10, intensity 1, and no shadow maps. An enabled light follows the canonical position plus a world-space offset; it does not make the particle material emissive. Pooled reuse resets its parameters.","code":"public PointLight3D getLight()"},{"title":"setLightEnabled","note":"Enables or removes the optional light in the attached scene immediately. Enabling lazily allocates the light; disabling an unallocated light is a no-op. The particle's render material is unchanged.","code":"public Particle3D setLightEnabled(boolean enabled)"},{"title":"isLightEnabled","note":"Reads the requested light-enabled state, which can be true without a scene.","code":"public boolean isLightEnabled()"},{"title":"setLightOffset","note":"Sets a finite world-space offset and immediately synchronizes light position and membership. It does not rotate with the body. A zero offset avoids allocation when no light exists; a nonzero offset does not enable the light.","code":"public Particle3D setLightOffset(float x, float y, float z)"}],"art":11,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-3D-Particles-API"},{"index":23,"title":"3D particles","slug":"04-Graphics-3D-Particles-Guide","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"Mesh particles with Jolt","description":"ParticleEmitter3D particles = new ParticleEmitter3D(256, particle -> { particle.setLifetime(3).setModel(mesh); particle.getModelInstance().setMaterial(material); particle.getPosition().set(0, 0, 3); particle.getVelocity().set(1, 0, 5); }).setPhysics(world, particle -> new BodySettings3D(shape, MotionType3D.DYNAMIC) .setMass(.05f).setRestitution(.7f).setContinuousCollision(true)) .setEmissionRate(40) .attach(scene);"},{"title":"Lights attached to particles","description":"Mesh particles already receive scene lighting. A particle can additionally carry an explicit point light:"},{"title":"Cosmetic motion and billboards","description":"Omit setPhysics and call particles.update(deltaSeconds) yourself. These particles use constant-acceleration integration and distribute births within the frame. They create no native bodies and do not collide. Set particle.getAcceleration() explicitly if they should fall. Mesh particles can still attach to a scene; billboards use the texture, color and size API on getSprite() and emitter.submit(modelBatch)."},{"title":"Ownership and performance","description":"- burst(count) fills available capacity; automatic overflow is dropped without a later backlog. setEmitting(false) stops automatic births while existing particles continue. - clear() releases live bodies and scene membership, retaining reusable particle objects. close() also unregisters world callbacks and releases the pool. The world and rendering assets remain caller-owned. - Change physics modes only while…"},{"title":"Interactive test","description":"Run ./gradlew runPhysicsStudio --args=\"--scenario=4\" to open the fountain, angled deflectors and catch tray. The left panel controls Jolt on/off, emission rate, lifetime, bounce, bursts and clearing. Lifetime/bounce changes apply to subsequent births. Changing physics mode clears and restarts the fountain. Pause, single-step, time scale and gravity work with both modes. The footer displays FPS and frame timings;…"}],"examples":[{"title":"Mesh particles with Jolt","note":"Configure collision layers and gravity on the world as usual. A physical particle is a normal Jolt dynamic body: it supports contacts, sleeping, gravity factors, damping, sensors, collision filters, forces, impulses and continuous collision detection. Prefer simple sphere or box shapes. Rendering scale does not resize collision shapes; configure both…","code":"Model3D mesh = ModelBuilder3D.sphere(.1f, 8, 4);\nCollisionShape3D shape = CollisionShape3D.sphere(.1f);\nMaterial3D material = new Material3D().setRoughness(.3f);\n\nParticleEmitter3D particles = new ParticleEmitter3D(256, particle -> {\n    particle.setLifetime(3).setModel(mesh);\n    particle.getModelInstance().setMaterial(material);\n    particle.getPosition().set(0, 0, 3);\n    particle.getVelocity().set(1, 0, 5);\n}).setPhysics(world, particle ->\n    new BodySettings3D(shape, MotionType3D.DYNAMIC)\n        .setMass(.05f).setRestitution(.7f).setContinuousCollision(true))\n  .setEmissionRate(40)\n  .attach(scene);\n\n// Each frame: the caller advances the world once. Fixed ticks drive particles.\nworld.update(deltaSeconds);\nrenderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n// At teardown: releases bodies, scene membership and listener registrations.\nparticles.close();\n// The caller separately owns world, scene, renderer, meshes and materials."},{"title":"Lights attached to particles","note":"getLight() lazily creates independently owned parameters; it does not enable illumination by itself. In Filament, intensity 1 maps to 1,000 lumens, matching the existing emissive-mesh convention. The light follows the particle plus a world-space offset, including Jolt motion. It works without a mesh for invisible transient lighting. Attach the emitter to a…","code":"particle.getLight().setColor(Color.ORANGE).setIntensity(12).setRange(6);\nparticle.setLightOffset(0, 0, .1f).setLightEnabled(true);"},{"title":"3D particles and physics integration — ParticleEmitter3D","note":"Related 3D particles and physics integration example: The initializer and physics factory run synchronously for each spawn. They must not reenter or modify the emitter. Use one owning thread (the physics world's thread when attached), and treat pooled particle references as temporary. Closing releases owned bodies and scene membership, never borrowed resources.","code":"ParticleEmitter3D sparks = new ParticleEmitter3D(256, particle -> {\n    particle.setLifetime(0.8f);\n    particle.getPosition().set(0f, 0f, 1f);\n    particle.getVelocity().set(1f, 0f, 3f);\n    particle.getAcceleration().set(0f, 0f, -9.8f);\n    // Configure a borrowed sprite texture or model here.\n});\nsparks.setEmissionRate(40f);\n// Each frame, with a batch already begun:\nsparks.update(deltaSeconds);\nsparks.submit(batch);\n// Release emitter-owned state when the effect is no longer needed:\nsparks.close();"},{"title":"3D particles and physics integration — getLight","note":"Related 3D particles and physics integration example: Lazily creates and returns this particle's owned point light without enabling it. Defaults are white, range 10, intensity 1, and no shadow maps. An enabled light follows the canonical position plus a world-space offset; it does not make the particle material emissive. Pooled reuse resets its parameters.","code":"public PointLight3D getLight()"},{"title":"3D particles and physics integration — setLightEnabled","note":"Related 3D particles and physics integration example: Enables or removes the optional light in the attached scene immediately. Enabling lazily allocates the light; disabling an unallocated light is a no-op. The particle's render material is unchanged.","code":"public Particle3D setLightEnabled(boolean enabled)"}],"art":11,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-3D-Particles-Guide"},{"index":24,"title":"Path-traced lighting and culling","slug":"04-Graphics-3D-Path-Tracing","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"Requirements and implementation","description":"Request JGLConfiguration.defaults().contextVersion(4, 3). The tracer uses the existing LWJGL OpenGL native bindings. Construction fails explicitly on older contexts; the existing OpenGL 3.3 raster renderer remains available."},{"title":"3D integration","description":"The tracer owns GPU buffers/images and borrows models/textures. It restores touched GL state, including image bindings, indexed storage-buffer ranges and pixel-transfer state. Work counters are getSceneBuildCount(), getTriangleCount(), getBvhNodeCount() and getAccumulatedSamples()."},{"title":"2D integration","description":"PathTracer2D gives scenery physical height instead of reconstructing it from a flattened color image. Use consistent world units, such as one unit per tile. Sprites are textured XY planes, walls are extruded boxes, and light sources are emissive spheres."},{"title":"Quality and measured cost","description":"The resolution scales below apply to PROGRESSIVE mode. REALTIME renders at 100% resolution for all three settings; samples/frame and maximum bounces remain as listed. Select the stationary algorithm with setRenderMode(PathTracer3D.RenderMode.PROGRESSIVE) or the studio dropdown. The older timings below predate the traversal improvements; see the current motion benchmark for current studio results."},{"title":"Engine draw culling","description":"- JOML FrustumIntersection extracts six normalized planes once per camera rebuild. Each box uses one support vertex per plane, replacing repeated eight-corner matrix transforms. Boundary geometry is conservatively retained. - ModelBatch3D culls loose meshes and billboards. Scene-node submission also rejects whole subtrees using aggregate bounds. Parent transforms, direct mutable-vector edits and descendants…"},{"title":"Verification","description":"verify3D includes 24,000 randomized frustum comparisons against an independent corner-transform reference, transformed sprite boundaries, GPU pixel equivalence with culling on/off, 100-model subtree rejection and mutable-transform restoration, actual compute shader execution, area lighting, texture alpha cutouts, orthographic lighting, accumulation invalidation and GL state restoration. Run build verify3D…"}],"examples":[{"title":"3D integration","note":"The tracer owns GPU buffers/images and borrows models/textures. It restores touched GL state, including image bindings, indexed storage-buffer ranges and pixel-transfer state. Work counters are getSceneBuildCount(), getTriangleCount(), getBvhNodeCount() and getAccumulatedSamples().","code":"// Create after OpenGL initialization, on the context thread.\nPathTracer3D tracer = new PathTracer3D()\n    .setQuality(PathTracer3D.Quality.HIGH)\n    .setSky(.02f, .03f, .05f) // linear environment radiance\n    .setMaxSamples(4096);\nMaterial3D gold = new Material3D()\n    .setTint(new Color(.94f, .7f, .32f, 1))\n    .setMetallic(1).setRoughness(.16f);\nMaterial3D glass = new Material3D()\n    .setTransmission(1).setIndexOfRefraction(1.5f);\nMaterial3D panel = new Material3D()\n    .setEmissive(1, .94f, .83f, 1).setEmissionStrength(14);\n\n// Existing Scene3D, ModelInstance3D, OBJ parts and SceneNode3D hierarchies.\n// Set the destination framebuffer and viewport first.\ntracer.render(scene, camera);\n// Draw the existing 2D UI afterward. At shutdown on the context thread:\ntracer.close();"},{"title":"2D integration","note":"Viewport aspect ratio controls orthographic world width. Keep the camera above all geometry. Move returned instances directly or remove them through getScene(). This alternative requires explicit scene geometry; existing Lighting2D.beginScene/endScene games continue using their cached light maps.","code":"PathTracer2D lighting = new PathTracer2D().setView(0, 0, 12, 100);\nlighting.addSurface(-8, -6, 16, 12, 0,\n    new Material3D().setTexture(floorTexture));\nModelInstance3D wall = lighting.addWall(-2, -2, .5f, 4, 1,\n    new Material3D().setTint(new Color(.7f, .12f, .06f, 1)));\nlighting.addAreaLight(2, 1, 3, .4f, Color.WHITE, 16);\nlighting.getTracer().setQuality(PathTracer3D.Quality.INTERACTIVE);\nlighting.render();"},{"title":"Shaders and visual effects — How blur radius works example","note":"Related Shaders and visual effects example: Where u_texelSize should be (1/textureWidth, 1/textureHeight) for the bound texture. Larger radius values increase the sampling offset distance.","code":"BlurShader blur = new BlurShader();\nTexture texture = ...;\n\n// Before drawing your textured quads:\nblur.apply(texture, 2f);"},{"title":"Shaders and visual effects — Threshold behavior example","note":"Related Shaders and visual effects example: The fragment computes noise n. If n < threshold the fragment is discarded. The edge band is smoothstep(th, th + 0.10, n) which creates a ~10% wide transition in noise-space used to blend between burn color and original color.","code":"BurnShader burn = new BurnShader();\n\n// Example: burn away from 0 -> 1 over time\nfloat t = (float)elapsedSeconds;\nfloat threshold = Math.min(1f, t * 0.25f);\n\nburn.bind(t, threshold, 1f, 0.35f, 0.05f, 1f);\n// draw your textured quad(s)...\nburn.unbind(); // optional"},{"title":"Shaders and visual effects — ComputeShader","note":"Related Shaders and visual effects example: All OpenGL operations require the appropriate current context. Uniform setters use glUniform and require this program to be bound; dispatch also uses the currently bound program. Binding helpers change global state and do not restore previous bindings. Created storage buffers belong to the caller and are not deleted when this shader is disposed.","code":"ComputeShader shader = new ComputeShader(source);\nshader.bind();\nshader.setUniform1i(\"u_count\", count);\nshader.dispatch(groups, 1, 1);\nComputeShader.memoryBarrierAll();\nshader.unbind();\nshader.dispose();"}],"art":10,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-3D-Path-Tracing"},{"index":25,"title":"Cameras and picking","slug":"04-Graphics-Cameras","summary":"Cameras convert world geometry into clip space and support picking and culling. Choose an orthographic camera for constant apparent size and a perspective camera for depth-dependent size. Camera3D owns derived view/projection data; changing pose requires rebuilding that derived state.","capabilities":[{"title":"2D cameras","description":"Orthographic and UI camera variants support planar rendering conventions."},{"title":"3D projections","description":"Perspective and orthographic projections control field of view or visible extents."},{"title":"Picking","description":"Unprojection and screen rays translate a cursor into world-space queries."},{"title":"Frustum culling","description":"Derived planes reject geometry outside the current view."},{"title":"Orbit control","description":"A controller maps interaction into a camera pose around a target."}],"examples":[{"title":"Camera3D","note":"Projection utilities use bottom-left viewport coordinates and normalized depth zero through one. Convert top-left input coordinates before calling them. Picking rays begin on the near plane, not at the camera position. Mutable accessors return owned storage; do not modify derived matrices independently or retain them as snapshots. Scratch vectors make…","code":"PerspectiveCamera camera = new PerspectiveCamera();\ncamera.setPosition(4, 4, 3);\ncamera.lookAt(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1);\ncamera.rebuild(1280, 720);\nRayf ray = camera.screenPointToRay(640, 360, 0, 0, 1280, 720, new Rayf());"},{"title":"OrbitCameraController","note":"Applying a pose does not rebuild camera matrices. Use finite input deltas and access the controller on the same thread as camera updates; it is unsynchronized.","code":"OrbitCameraController controller = new OrbitCameraController();\nPerspectiveCamera camera = new PerspectiveCamera();\ncontroller.orbit(20, -5);\ncontroller.apply(camera);\ncamera.rebuild(1280, 720);"},{"title":"Usage Example","note":"Cameras convert world geometry into clip space and support picking and culling. Choose an orthographic camera for constant apparent size and a perspective camera for depth-dependent size. Camera3D owns derived view/projection data; changing pose requires rebuilding that derived state.","code":"Camera camera = new OrthographicCamera();\n    camera.setCenter(0, 0);\n    camera.setZoom(1f);\n    camera.rebuild(worldWidth, worldHeight);"},{"title":"Use Cases example","note":"- Pixel-based or resolution-independent UI layouts","code":"Camera uiCamera = new UIOrthographicCamera();\n    uiCamera.setCenter(0, 0);   // Top-left of screen\n    uiCamera.setZoom(1f);\n    uiCamera.rebuild(windowWidth, windowHeight);"},{"title":"center","note":"Live world-space camera center used by concrete projection implementations.","code":"protected final  Vector2f center"}],"art":7,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-Cameras"},{"index":26,"title":"Graphics capability selection","slug":"04-Graphics-Capabilities","summary":"Valthorne supports a desktop OpenGL 3.3 raster baseline and optional higher-level paths. automaticContext() tries desktop core contexts in order: 4.3, 4.1, 3.3. An explicit contextVersion(major, minor) restores strict creation. Defaults remain strict 3.3, so existing applications do not silently change their requirements.","capabilities":[{"title":"Raspberry Pi work still required","description":"Upstream Filament supports Linux and OpenGL ES, and its community FFM project lists Linux ARM64 runtimes. Valthorne still packages the Windows runtime and calls WGL for context sharing. Adding another runtime alone does not port that sharing boundary. The raster, texture, UI and window paths also use desktop OpenGL bindings and GLSL 330; changing GLFW's client API is insufficient."},{"title":"Verification in this pass","description":"Pure tests cover renderer selection, unsupported explicit requests and configuration precedence. Native tests cover automatic and explicit 3.3/4.1/4.3 requests, actual capability reports, visible raster framebuffer output, light removal, error-free compute probes, failed creation recovery, and report cleanup on teardown."}],"examples":[{"title":"Graphics capability selection example","note":"SceneRenderer3D.Backend.AUTO selects Filament when the current adapter's platform and graphics prerequisites exist, otherwise RASTER. Explicit backend requests fail if unsupported. Asset, shader and native loading failures remain errors; they do not silently downgrade quality. The selected backend is available through getBackend(). The facade selects once,…","code":"JGL.init(app, JGLConfiguration.defaults().automaticContext());\n// In Application.init(), with the context current:\nSystem.out.println(Window.getGraphicsCapabilities());\nSceneRenderer3D renderer = new SceneRenderer3D();\n// In Application.render():\nrenderer.render(scene, camera);\n// In Application.dispose(), before destroying the context:\nrenderer.close();"},{"title":"Filament desktop renderer — Engine integration","note":"Related Filament desktop renderer example: Use SceneRenderer3D for ordinary application code: it performs capability and platform selection and owns the selected renderer. Request Backend.FILAMENT only when the application requires Filament-specific output and should fail on an unsupported system. Access renderer.filament() only for backend-specific quality, exposure, environment, or anti-aliasing…","code":"// AUTO selects Filament when supported and the raster backend otherwise.\nSceneRenderer3D renderer = new SceneRenderer3D();\nrenderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n// Before destroying the current graphics context:\nrenderer.close();"},{"title":"Filament desktop renderer — 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — BillboardBatch3D","note":"Related Filament desktop renderer example: Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Construction, rendering, and disposal require a current OpenGL context. Rendering restores captured GL state; construction leaves array bindings at zero. The shader, vertex array, and GPU buffer are owned; textures, sprites, cameras, and materials remain borrowed. Shared scratch vectors preclude concurrent use.","code":"BillboardBatch3D batch = new BillboardBatch3D();\ntry {\n    batch.begin(sprite.getTextureRegion().getTexture());\n    batch.draw(sprite, camera);\n    batch.render(new MeshRenderState3D().setCamera(camera));\n} finally {\n    batch.dispose();\n}"},{"title":"Filament desktop renderer — 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — Material3D","note":"Related Filament desktop renderer example: Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Tint and emissive colors are owned mutable objects exposed directly by getters. Textures are borrowed and never disposed here; copies share the texture but have independent color storage. Sharing a material shares all later mutations. Set render pass before an explicit depth-write override, because pass assignment resets that flag. Instances provide no…","code":"Material3D material = new Material3D()\n        .setRenderPass(RenderPass3D.TRANSLUCENT)\n        .setTint(new Color(1f, 1f, 1f, 0.5f))\n        .setDepthWrite(false);"},{"title":"Filament desktop renderer — 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — MeshBatch3D","note":"Related Filament desktop renderer example: Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Construction, upload, rendering, and disposal require a current OpenGL context. Public rendering and upload methods restore the state covered by RenderStateSnapshot3D; construction leaves array bindings at zero. The batch owns its shaders and GPU objects, but borrows models, textures, materials, cameras, and lighting. Mutable scratch storage makes it…","code":"MeshBatch3D mesh = new MeshBatch3D();\ntry {\n    mesh.begin();\n    mesh.box(0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 2, 0, color);\n    mesh.upload();\n    mesh.renderUploaded(new MeshRenderState3D().setCamera(camera));\n} finally {\n    mesh.dispose();\n}"}],"art":10,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-Capabilities"},{"index":27,"title":"Shaders and visual effects","slug":"04-Graphics-Shaders","summary":"Shaders are programs that define vertex, fragment, or compute work. The base classes manage compilation, linking, binding, and uniforms; specialized shaders implement common texture, shape, mesh, lighting, and post-effect contracts. Match the shader's expected vertex layout and uniforms to the renderer supplying its data.","capabilities":[{"title":"Program lifecycle","description":"Compile/link, bind for use, configure uniforms, and dispose the program."},{"title":"Resource sources","description":"ShaderSources loads packaged shader text and templates."},{"title":"Visual effects","description":"Blur, glow, outline, flash, burn, reflection, and water configure different texture effects."},{"title":"Compute dispatch","description":"ComputeShader performs non-raster work and requires appropriate OpenGL support."},{"title":"Renderer contracts","description":"Mesh, billboard, shape, and textured-quad shaders expect specific attributes and matrices."}],"examples":[{"title":"How blur radius works example","note":"Where u_texelSize should be (1/textureWidth, 1/textureHeight) for the bound texture. Larger radius values increase the sampling offset distance.","code":"BlurShader blur = new BlurShader();\nTexture texture = ...;\n\n// Before drawing your textured quads:\nblur.apply(texture, 2f);"},{"title":"Threshold behavior example","note":"The fragment computes noise n. If n < threshold the fragment is discarded. The edge band is smoothstep(th, th + 0.10, n) which creates a ~10% wide transition in noise-space used to blend between burn color and original color.","code":"BurnShader burn = new BurnShader();\n\n// Example: burn away from 0 -> 1 over time\nfloat t = (float)elapsedSeconds;\nfloat threshold = Math.min(1f, t * 0.25f);\n\nburn.bind(t, threshold, 1f, 0.35f, 0.05f, 1f);\n// draw your textured quad(s)...\nburn.unbind(); // optional"},{"title":"ComputeShader","note":"All OpenGL operations require the appropriate current context. Uniform setters use glUniform and require this program to be bound; dispatch also uses the currently bound program. Binding helpers change global state and do not restore previous bindings. Created storage buffers belong to the caller and are not deleted when this shader is disposed.","code":"ComputeShader shader = new ComputeShader(source);\nshader.bind();\nshader.setUniform1i(\"u_count\", count);\nshader.dispatch(groups, 1, 1);\nComputeShader.memoryBarrierAll();\nshader.unbind();\nshader.dispose();"},{"title":"Uniforms example","note":"- u_amount: 0..1 mix amount (0 = no flash, 1 = full flash color).","code":"FlashShader flash = new FlashShader();\n\n// Repeating flash (0.5 seconds per cycle).\nflash.apply(JGL.getTime(), 0.5f, 1f, 0.2f, 0.2f, 1.0f);\n// draw your quad(s)...\nflash.unbind(); // optional"},{"title":"Uniforms example (2)","note":"- u_glowColor (vec4): glow RGBA color (alpha is scaled by computed glow).","code":"GlowShader glow = new GlowShader();\n\nTexture texture = ...;\n\nglow.apply(texture, 6f, 1.25f, 1f, 0.8f, 0.2f, 1f); // warm glow"},{"title":"Output rules example","note":"- Else: transparent.","code":"OutlineShader outline = new OutlineShader();\nTexture texture = ...;\n\noutline.apply(texture, 1f, 0f, 0f, 0f, 1f); // 1px black outline"},{"title":"ReflectionShader","note":"Applying the effect temporarily moves and resizes the sprite below its original bounds, then restores those bounds after successful drawing. Failures can leave bounds changed or this program bound. Texture unit zero is used, and the previous shader program is not restored.","code":"ReflectionShader reflection = new ReflectionShader();\n// During rendering, with playerSprite already initialized:\nplayerSprite.draw();\nreflection.apply(playerSprite, elapsedSeconds, 0.35f, 0.65f,\n        0.6f, 0.75f, 1f, 2f, 18f, 2.5f);\n// During graphics shutdown:\nreflection.dispose();"},{"title":"Shader example","note":"OpenGL shader program wrapper that compiles, links, validates, and manages a GLSL vertex/fragment shader pair, with hot-reload support and cached lookups.","code":"// Build\nShader shader = new Shader(VERT_SRC, FRAG_SRC);\n\n// Optional: lock attribute indices BEFORE the first link (or call reload() after changing bindings)\nshader.bindAttribLocation(0, \"a_pos\");\nshader.bindAttribLocation(1, \"a_uv\");\nshader.reload();\n\n// Use\nshader.bind();\nshader.setUniform1i(\"u_tex0\", 0);\nshader.setUniform2f(\"u_resolution\", Window.getWidth(), Window.getHeight());\nshader.unbind();\n\n// Cleanup\nshader.dispose();"},{"title":"Shader — Constructor","note":"- vertexSource — GLSL vertex shader source (must not be null) - fragmentSource — GLSL fragment shader source (must not be null)","code":"Shader s = new Shader(v, f);\ns.bindAttribLocation(0, \"a_pos\");\ns.bindAttribLocation(1, \"a_uv\");\ns.reload(); // apply bindings by relinking"},{"title":"bindUniformBlock","note":"- blockName — uniform block name in GLSL (must not be null) - bindingPoint — binding point index used with glBindBufferBase","code":"shader.bindUniformBlock(\"Globals\", 0);\nglBindBufferBase(GL_UNIFORM_BUFFER, 0, uboId);"},{"title":"Visual behavior example","note":"- Because this uses raw texture2D sampling, you may see edge bleed unless your atlas has padding or you clamp regions appropriately.","code":"WaterShader water = new WaterShader();\nTexture waterTexture = ...;\n\nwater.apply(waterTexture, JGL.getTime(), 3f, 18f, 2.0f);\n// draw sprite(s)...\nwater.unbind(); // optional"}],"art":10,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-Shaders"},{"index":28,"title":"Viewport scaling and coordinate conversion","slug":"04-Graphics-Viewports","summary":"A viewport chooses how a logical world maps into available screen pixels. This is separate from the camera's projection model: the viewport decides scaling and placement, and the camera supplies the view. Use one policy consistently for drawing, resize handling, and pointer conversion.","capabilities":[{"title":"Fit","description":"Preserves aspect ratio while allowing unused borders."},{"title":"Fill","description":"Preserves aspect ratio while allowing content outside the visible screen."},{"title":"Stretch","description":"Maps dimensions independently and can distort aspect ratio."},{"title":"Screen","description":"Tracks screen-sized coordinates for pixel-oriented layouts."},{"title":"Perspective integration","description":"PerspectiveViewport connects a 3D camera to viewport dimensions and matrix application."}],"examples":[{"title":"Behavior example","note":"- In both cases the viewport is centered on the opposite axis","code":"FillViewport viewport = new FillViewport(1280f, 720f);\nviewport.update(windowWidth, windowHeight);\n\nviewport.bind();\ntry {\n    worldRenderer.render();\n} finally {\n    viewport.unbind();\n}"},{"title":"Behavior example (2)","note":"- Black bars or unused margins may appear on one axis","code":"FitViewport viewport = new FitViewport(1920f, 1080f);\nviewport.update(windowWidth, windowHeight);\n\nviewport.render(() -> {\n    game.render();\n});"},{"title":"PerspectiveViewport","note":"Viewport bounds use OpenGL bottom-left-origin pixel coordinates. Overlay dimensions are independent logical units, initially matching the constructor dimensions. Screen conversion methods use the camera's current matrices and reuse internal result objects; copy results that must survive another call.","code":"PerspectiveViewport viewport = new PerspectiveViewport(800, 600);\nviewport.renderWithOverlay(() -> {\n    // Draw world geometry with the viewport's camera.\n}, () -> {\n    // Draw UI in bottom-left-origin overlay coordinates.\n});"},{"title":"Behavior example (3)","note":"- No aspect preservation logic is needed because the world tracks the screen directly","code":"ScreenViewport viewport = new ScreenViewport(1280, 720);\nviewport.update(windowWidth, windowHeight);\n\nviewport.bind();\ntry {\n    ui.draw(batch);\n} finally {\n    viewport.unbind();\n}"},{"title":"Behavior example (4)","note":"- Content may appear wider or taller depending on the screen shape","code":"StretchViewport viewport = new StretchViewport(800f, 600f);\nviewport.update(windowWidth, windowHeight);\n\nviewport.render(() -> {\n    renderer.drawScene();\n});"},{"title":"Coordinate system example","note":"This class assumes a bottom-left world coordinate system, matching the rest of your engine and standard OpenGL-style orthographic rendering.","code":"Viewport viewport = new FitViewport(1280f, 720f);\nviewport.update(windowWidth, windowHeight);\n\nviewport.bind();\ntry {\n    batch.begin();\n\n    batch.draw(background, 0f, 0f, 1280f, 720f);\n\n    if (viewport.beginScissor(100f, 100f, 400f, 200f)) {\n        try {\n            batch.draw(panelTexture, 100f, 100f, 400f, 200f);\n        } finally {\n            viewport.endScissor();\n        }\n    }\n\n    batch.end();\n} finally {\n    viewport.unbind();\n}\n\nVector2f world = viewport.screenToWorld(mouseX, mouseY);\nif (world != null) {\n    System.out.println(\"Mouse in world: \" + world.x() + \", \" + world.y());\n}"}],"art":7,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/04-Graphics-Viewports"},{"index":29,"title":"Batched 2D lighting","slug":"05-Lighting-2D","summary":"Lighting2D renders visible planar lights in a batch and uses polar shadow data for occlusion. Separate light properties from occluder geometry, then let the renderer reuse results when the scene remains unchanged. This path suits many local lights without constructing a separate ray mesh for every visible contribution.","capabilities":[{"title":"Point lights","description":"Position, radius, color, and intensity define local illumination."},{"title":"Occluder indexing","description":"Occluder2D and its index identify geometry relevant to shadow updates."},{"title":"Polar shadows","description":"Angular shadow information describes blocking around a light."},{"title":"Caching and composition","description":"The renderer reuses compatible unchanged data and composites the light result."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sprite-shaped shadows","note":"This uses RGBA alpha to extract the sprite's pixel silhouette, preserving transparent holes and disconnected islands. Straight adjacent pixel edges are merged. No GPU readback, per-frame pixel scan, or extra shadow draw per sprite is needed. Geometry feeds the existing polar shadow cache, light category masks and spatial index.","code":"import valthorne.graphics.lighting2d.SpriteOccluder2D;\n\nSpriteOccluder2D treeShadow = new SpriteOccluder2D(treeSprite);\nlighting.addOccluder(treeShadow);\n// Draw treeSprite normally inside lighting.beginScene(...) / endScene().\n// Sprite position, size, scale, pivot, rotation, flips and atlas-frame setters\n// are followed automatically when lighting.endScene() updates the shadow map.\n\n// When removing the tree:\nlighting.removeOccluder(treeShadow);"},{"title":"Elevated lights and finite ground shadows (2.5D)","note":"Anchors are asset-specific: exclude transparent frame padding and anchor to the feet or trunk, not the canvas bottom. Stable anchors prevent animation bobbing. Each shadow samples its sprite's current atlas frame directly on the GPU, with perspective-correct projection, frame-clamped soft sampling and no per-frame CPU silhouette extraction. Higher lights…","code":"PointLight2D sun = new PointLight2D().setPosition(500, 600)\n        .setElevation(520).setRadius(1500).setIntensity(2);\nlighting.addLight(sun);\nGroundShadowRenderer2D shadows = new GroundShadowRenderer2D();\nSpriteGroundShadow2D playerShadow = new SpriteGroundShadow2D(player)\n        .setFrameAnchors(.42f, .35f, .64f) // foot U/V and artwork top, bottom-up\n        .setHeight(45).setMaxLength(70).setOpacity(.45f);\nvar casters = List.of(playerShadow);\n\n// Inside the matching world projection and lighting scene capture:\nbatch.begin();\ndrawGround(batch);\nshadows.draw(batch, sun, casters); // flushes pending ground, restores batch GL state\ndrawActors(batch);                // actors are not painted over by their own shadows\nbatch.end();\n// Complete lighting.endScene() as usual; close shadows on shutdown."},{"title":"Lighting2D","note":"Owns GPU resources and borrows light/occluder objects. Their revisions drive cache invalidation, so use their supported mutation APIs. Resource/configuration operations require the creating OpenGL thread. Capture is not nestable on one instance; finish existing batches before capture transitions.","code":"Lighting2D lighting = new Lighting2D();\nlighting.addLight(new PointLight2D().setPosition(100, 80));\nlighting.beginScene(0, 0, 800, 600, 800, 600);\ntry {\n    // Draw and finish scene batches with matching XY-world coordinates.\n    lighting.endScene();\n} finally {\n    lighting.cancelScene(); // No-op after successful completion.\n}\nlighting.close(); // At shutdown with the creating context still current."},{"title":"Occluder2D","note":"Construction checks vertex count, finite coordinates and nonzero signed area, but does not establish that edges never intersect. Callers are responsible for supplying a simple boundary. Either winding order is accepted. Coordinates and translation use the same world units as PointLight2D.","code":"Occluder2D wall = Occluder2D.rectangle(20, -10, 4, 20)\n        .setCategory(2);\nlighting.addOccluder(wall);\nlight.setOcclusionMask(2);"},{"title":"PointLight2D","note":"Setters validate before changing state. Geometry-related changes also advance the shadow revision; appearance changes advance only the general revision. Color and cone assignments always advance that revision, even for equal inputs. Use setters rather than changing package fields so rendering caches can detect updates. Access is not synchronized; avoid…","code":"PointLight2D light = new PointLight2D()\n        .setPosition(100, 80)\n        .setRadius(200)\n        .setIntensity(3)\n        .setCone(0, 0.3f, 0.6f);\nlighting.addLight(light);"},{"title":"PolarShadow2D","note":"Each segment updates only the angular bins it spans, including wraparound. An endpoint-distance fallback conservatively covers narrow obstacles when a bin-center ray misses their segment. This is a discretized shadow representation, not an exact continuous visibility query.","code":"PolarShadow2D shadow = new PolarShadow2D(512);\nPointLight2D light = new PointLight2D().setRadius(100);\nOccluder2D wall = Occluder2D.rectangle(20, -10, 4, 20);\nboolean rebuilt = shadow.update(light, List.of(wall));\nfloat normalizedDistance = shadow.getDistance(0);"}],"art":1,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/05-Lighting-2D"},{"index":30,"title":"Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps","slug":"05-Lighting-3D","summary":"Raster 3D lighting combines material shading, directional shadows, and point lights. LightGrid3D groups local lights into screen tiles so shading does not inspect every light everywhere. ShadowMap3D renders directional depth information for later light evaluation.","capabilities":[{"title":"Tiled lights","description":"Screen-space light lists reduce per-fragment light search work."},{"title":"Material response","description":"Lighting uses material inputs such as tint, roughness, metallic response, and emission."},{"title":"Directional depth","description":"A shadow map stores visibility from the light's view."},{"title":"Softness and bias","description":"Sampling radius and depth offsets control the appearance and artifacts of shadows."}],"examples":[{"title":"Lighting3D","note":"Create, prepare, bind, mutate, and close on the creating OpenGL thread. Binding uses texture units three and four and leaves their restoration to the batch. The grid's all-visible mode provides a diagnostic reference path with the same shading and no per-tile rejection.","code":"Lighting3D lighting = new Lighting3D();\nlighting.addLight(new PointLight3D().setPosition(0, 0, 5));\n// With the intended viewport and rebuilt camera already active:\nlighting.prepare(camera);\n// The compatible batch binds lighting when drawing the scene.\nlighting.close(); // While the creating OpenGL context remains current."},{"title":"ShadowMap3D","note":"Construction, rendering, and disposal require a current compatible OpenGL context. The scene and returned camera are borrowed; this object owns its depth texture, framebuffer, comparison sampler, and batch. A successful depth pass makes the matrix and texture ready for the main pass. Cached rendering requires the caller to advance a caster revision…","code":"try (ShadowMap3D shadows = new ShadowMap3D(2048)) {\n    shadows.getCamera().setWorldHeight(40);\n    shadows.renderIfChanged(scene, casterRevision);\n    // Supply this shadow map to the main pass's MeshRenderState3D.\n}"},{"title":"MAX_LIGHTS","note":"Maximum input lights, inline indices per tile, and integer tile-record stride.","code":"public static final  int MAX_LIGHTS"},{"title":"LIGHTS_PER_TILE","note":"Maximum input lights, inline indices per tile, and integer tile-record stride.","code":"public static final  int LIGHTS_PER_TILE"},{"title":"STRIDE","note":"Maximum input lights, inline indices per tile, and integer tile-record stride.","code":"public static final  int STRIDE"}],"art":1,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/05-Lighting-3D"},{"index":31,"title":"Lighting and FPS","slug":"05-Lighting-Overview","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"3D setup","description":"Create lighting after the OpenGL context exists. It is borrowed by the render state; close it yourself in Application.dispose()."},{"title":"2D setup","description":"valthorne.graphics.lighting2d contains Lighting2D, PointLight2D, and Occluder2D. It captures the scene's color into a texture, computes an HDR light map, and composites lighting in linear color. Finish a TextureBatch before beginning/ending scene capture. Draw UI afterward so it is unaffected by illumination."},{"title":"Measurements and verification","description":"The playgrounds have explicit benchmark modes. They disable VSync, simulate fixed steps, warm up for 120 frames, then measure 300 frames. A glFinish is used only in benchmark mode to include completed GPU work in render timing. These measurements exclude the physics update and include scene rendering and overlay work; they are not GPU-only timings or a guarantee of whole-game FPS."}],"examples":[{"title":"3D setup","note":"Imports are valthorne.graphics.lighting3d.Lighting3D, valthorne.graphics.model.*, and valthorne.graphics.Color. Add local lights to Lighting3D; the old eight-light list on MeshRenderState3D belongs to its compatibility shader. The directional light and fog settings are shared.","code":"Lighting3D lighting = new Lighting3D()\n        .setEnvironment(new Color(.16f, .21f, .3f, 1), new Color(.035f, .028f, .025f, 1))\n        .setExposure(1);\nlighting.addLight(new PointLight3D()\n        .setPosition(-3, -1, 4)\n        .setColor(new Color(.32f, .55f, 1, 1))\n        .setRange(9)\n        .setIntensity(22));\n\nMeshRenderState3D state = new MeshRenderState3D()\n        .setCamera(camera)\n        .setLighting(lighting)\n        .setDirectionalLight(new Color(3.8f, 3.45f, 2.9f, 1))\n        .setLightDirection(5, -5, 10)\n        .setFog(25, 70, .8f);\n\nMaterial3D metal = new Material3D()\n        .setTint(new Color(.9f, .6f, .25f, 1))\n        .setRoughness(.25f)\n        .setMetallic(.8f);\n\n// Render normally. ModelBatch3D prepares light data once for this pass.\nscene.render(batch, state);"},{"title":"Directional shadows","note":"The shadow pass now uses a dedicated depth-only shader with alpha cutouts. It performs no lighting, fog, or radiance calculations. The new color shader uses four bilinear hardware comparison samples for soft edges. Configure the light camera tightly around the relevant scene region; softness is in shadow texels, and bias is normalized depth.","code":"ShadowMap3D shadows = new ShadowMap3D(1024)\n        .setBias(.0006f).setSoftness(2.5f).setStrength(.85f);\nshadows.getCamera().setPosition(5, -5, 10);\nshadows.getCamera().lookAt(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1);\nshadows.getCamera().setWorldHeight(24);\nstate.setShadowMap(shadows);\n\n// Before the scene color pass:\nshadows.render(scene);"},{"title":"2D setup","note":"Capture covers the viewport active at beginScene; it replaces that viewport's color on composition and preserves the captured alpha. Provide an opaque scene background when that is what your game needs. The capture target has no depth attachment. World bounds are axis-aligned XY; offset/zoom them to match an unrotated orthographic camera. Rotated camera…","code":"Lighting2D lighting = new Lighting2D()\n        .setAmbient(new Color(.055f, .075f, .12f, 1))\n        .setResolutionScale(.5f);\nPointLight2D lamp = new PointLight2D()\n        .setPosition(250, 400).setRadius(420)\n        .setColor(new Color(.2f, .55f, 1, 1))\n        .setIntensity(5).setSourceRadius(15);\nlighting.addLight(lamp);\n\nOccluder2D wall = Occluder2D.rectangle(370, 260, 45, 235);\nlighting.addOccluder(wall);\n\n// Inside your active 2D viewport, using matching world bounds and pixel dimensions:\nlighting.beginScene(0, 0, worldWidth, worldHeight, pixelWidth, pixelHeight);\ntry {\n    batch.begin();\n    // Draw the scene's background, sprites, and obstacles.\n    batch.end();\n    lighting.endScene();\n} finally {\n    lighting.cancelScene(); // Restores the framebuffer if scene drawing fails.\n}\n// Draw the UI here."},{"title":"Batched 2D lighting — Sprite-shaped shadows","note":"Related Batched 2D lighting example: This uses RGBA alpha to extract the sprite's pixel silhouette, preserving transparent holes and disconnected islands. Straight adjacent pixel edges are merged. No GPU readback, per-frame pixel scan, or extra shadow draw per sprite is needed. Geometry feeds the existing polar shadow cache, light category masks and spatial index.","code":"import valthorne.graphics.lighting2d.SpriteOccluder2D;\n\nSpriteOccluder2D treeShadow = new SpriteOccluder2D(treeSprite);\nlighting.addOccluder(treeShadow);\n// Draw treeSprite normally inside lighting.beginScene(...) / endScene().\n// Sprite position, size, scale, pivot, rotation, flips and atlas-frame setters\n// are followed automatically when lighting.endScene() updates the shadow map.\n\n// When removing the tree:\nlighting.removeOccluder(treeShadow);"},{"title":"Batched 2D lighting — Elevated lights and finite ground shadows (2.5D)","note":"Related Batched 2D lighting example: Anchors are asset-specific: exclude transparent frame padding and anchor to the feet or trunk, not the canvas bottom. Stable anchors prevent animation bobbing. Each shadow samples its sprite's current atlas frame directly on the GPU, with perspective-correct projection, frame-clamped soft sampling and no per-frame CPU silhouette extraction. Higher lights…","code":"PointLight2D sun = new PointLight2D().setPosition(500, 600)\n        .setElevation(520).setRadius(1500).setIntensity(2);\nlighting.addLight(sun);\nGroundShadowRenderer2D shadows = new GroundShadowRenderer2D();\nSpriteGroundShadow2D playerShadow = new SpriteGroundShadow2D(player)\n        .setFrameAnchors(.42f, .35f, .64f) // foot U/V and artwork top, bottom-up\n        .setHeight(45).setMaxLength(70).setOpacity(.45f);\nvar casters = List.of(playerShadow);\n\n// Inside the matching world projection and lighting scene capture:\nbatch.begin();\ndrawGround(batch);\nshadows.draw(batch, sun, casters); // flushes pending ground, restores batch GL state\ndrawActors(batch);                // actors are not painted over by their own shadows\nbatch.end();\n// Complete lighting.endScene() as usual; close shadows on shutdown."}],"art":1,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/05-Lighting-Overview"},{"index":32,"title":"Screen-space radiance cascades","slug":"05-Lighting-Radiance-Cascades","summary":"Radiance cascades estimate planar, screen-space lighting by capturing scene data and solving a hierarchy of probe intervals. The hierarchy traces short intervals, extends them within each level, and merges levels back toward the finest result. The resolved light texture is then consumed by composition.","capabilities":[{"title":"Scene capture","description":"RadianceSceneBuffer supplies the scene textures used for tracing."},{"title":"Hierarchy settings","description":"Probe spacing, ray counts, interval lengths, and solve dimensions define cost and coverage."},{"title":"Trace and merge","description":"Compute stages build and combine lighting over progressively larger intervals."},{"title":"Resolved output","description":"The final texture integrates the finest merged radiance for image-space consumption."}],"examples":[{"title":"RadianceCascades","note":"Construction, solving, resizing, and disposal require a compute-capable OpenGL context. The solver owns its programs and textures, borrows the captured scene, and retains settings by reference. Configure hierarchy settings before creation: changing them does not automatically rebuild targets. Rendering leaves texture and image bindings changed and unbinds…","code":"RadianceSceneBuffer scene = new RadianceSceneBuffer(640, 360);\nRadianceCascades lighting = new RadianceCascades(640, 360);\n// Capture scene data into scene before solving.\nlighting.render(scene);\nTexture light = lighting.getLightTexture();\n// Consume light before resizing or disposing lighting.\nlighting.dispose();\nscene.dispose();"},{"title":"RadianceSceneBuffer","note":"Resizing discards captured pixels and invalidates borrowed texture wrappers. Dispose after consumers finish; this class does not manage their lifetime.","code":"RadianceSceneBuffer scene = new RadianceSceneBuffer(640, 360);\nscene.clear();\nscene.begin();\ntry {\n    // Draw encoded scene data using the active framebuffer.\n} finally {\n    scene.end();\n}\nscene.dispose();"},{"title":"getIndex","note":"Returns this level's zero-based level index. The value is fixed at construction and remains available after texture disposal.","code":"public int getIndex()"},{"title":"getProbeSpacing","note":"Returns this level's probe spacing in solver pixels. The value is fixed at construction and remains available after texture disposal.","code":"public int getProbeSpacing()"},{"title":"getRayCount","note":"Returns this level's directional rays per probe. The value is fixed at construction and remains available after texture disposal.","code":"public int getRayCount()"}],"art":1,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/05-Lighting-Radiance-Cascades"},{"index":33,"title":"Raycast lighting and shape occlusion","slug":"05-Lighting-Raycast","summary":"This lighting family builds planar light geometry by casting against registered occluders. A RayHandler coordinates lights and rendering, while a RayCastWorld supplies collision queries. Use this path when shape-based visibility and explicit ray-generated light meshes fit the scene.","capabilities":[{"title":"Light shapes","description":"Point, cone, polygon, and vertex-cast lights express different emission regions."},{"title":"Occluders","description":"ShapeRaycastWorld registers geometry and indexes broad-phase bounds."},{"title":"Light meshes","description":"Visibility results become renderable geometry; soft-shadow meshes extend shadow edges."},{"title":"Light maps","description":"LightMapRenderer composites light output and supports intermediate rendering."}],"examples":[{"title":"RayHandler","note":"Lights cache endpoints until dirtied. When occluders change, invalidate the affected lights explicitly. The render pass changes OpenGL state and assumes the caller has set the viewport. Resize the handler alongside the surface.","code":"RayHandler lighting = new RayHandler(800, 600);\nlighting.setAmbientLight(0.15f, 0.15f, 0.15f, 1f);\nPointLight lamp = new PointLight(lighting, 64,\n        new Color(1f, 0.9f, 0.7f, 1f), 200f, 400f, 300f);\nlighting.addLight(lamp);\n// Each frame, after drawing the scene with a matching viewport:\nlighting.update();\nlighting.render();\n// At shutdown, while the OpenGL context is still current:\nlighting.dispose();"},{"title":"ShapeRaycastWorld","note":"Queries test polygon edges rather than filled interiors. The implementation uses segment bounds for broad-phase candidates but does not explicitly cap the edge-intersection fraction at one; see the reusable rayCast overload for that limitation.","code":"ShapeRaycastWorld world = new ShapeRaycastWorld();\nworld.addShape(wall); // Existing Shape in the light coordinate space.\nworld.setMoving(wall);\nworld.prepare();      // Rebuild after geometry changes.\nRayCastHit hit = world.rayCast(0f, 0f, 100f, 100f);\nworld.setMoving(null);\n// Also dirty affected lights before reusing their cached endpoints."},{"title":"ConeLight — Constructor","note":"Creates an active, dirty cone light with copied color and borrowed handler. Construction does not register the light; add it to the handler separately.","code":"public ConeLight(RayHandler rayHandler, int rays, Color color, float distance, float x, float y, float directionDegrees, float coneDegrees)"},{"title":"ConeLight — update","note":"Rebuilds active, dirty geometry using uniform, boundary, and occluder-vertex angles. Sorting and compaction precede ray casting; a successful rebuild clears dirty state. Inactive or clean lights retain their previous results.","code":"public void update()"},{"title":"computeRayEnd","note":"Writes a uniformly sampled, unoccluded base-ray endpoint. The first and last rays lie on the cone edges; extra vertex samples are added by update.","code":"protected void computeRayEnd(int index, float[] output)"}],"art":1,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/05-Lighting-Raycast"},{"index":34,"title":"Lighting Studio","slug":"05-Lighting-Studio","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"Navigation and editing","description":"- Right-drag: orbit the 3D camera. Middle-drag: pan. Mouse wheel: zoom. - 3D / Top view switches projection. Middle-drag and wheel navigate the overhead view. - Click a visible emitter to select it, or use the light dropdown (including disabled lights). - Left-drag a selected emitter across its horizontal plane. Shift-drag changes height. - Place colored light, then click a surface, creates a light above it. Escape…"},{"title":"Reproducible validation","description":"The hidden smoke run invokes installed native cursor/button/wheel callbacks and checks orbiting, zooming, activation of the placement button, surface placement, light dragging, undo/redo, both projections and GL errors. It writes build/lighting-studio/studio.png and top-view.png for visual review."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reproducible validation","note":"The hidden smoke run invokes installed native cursor/button/wheel callbacks and checks orbiting, zooming, activation of the placement button, surface placement, light dragging, undo/redo, both projections and GL errors. It writes build/lighting-studio/studio.png and top-view.png for visual review.","code":".\\gradlew.bat runLightingStudio --args=\"--smoke\"\n.\\gradlew.bat runLightingStudio --args=\"--benchmark\"\n.\\gradlew.bat runLightingStudio --args=\"--benchmark-motion\"\n.\\gradlew.bat runLightingStudio --args=\"--benchmark-motion --progressive\"\n.\\gradlew.bat runLightingStudio --args=\"--visual-validation=build/visual-validation\"\n.\\gradlew.bat build"},{"title":"Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps — Lighting3D","note":"Related Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps example: Create, prepare, bind, mutate, and close on the creating OpenGL thread. Binding uses texture units three and four and leaves their restoration to the batch. The grid's all-visible mode provides a diagnostic reference path with the same shading and no per-tile rejection.","code":"Lighting3D lighting = new Lighting3D();\nlighting.addLight(new PointLight3D().setPosition(0, 0, 5));\n// With the intended viewport and rebuilt camera already active:\nlighting.prepare(camera);\n// The compatible batch binds lighting when drawing the scene.\nlighting.close(); // While the creating OpenGL context remains current."},{"title":"Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps — ShadowMap3D","note":"Related Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps example: Construction, rendering, and disposal require a current compatible OpenGL context. The scene and returned camera are borrowed; this object owns its depth texture, framebuffer, comparison sampler, and batch. A successful depth pass makes the matrix and texture ready for the main pass. Cached rendering requires the caller to advance a caster revision…","code":"try (ShadowMap3D shadows = new ShadowMap3D(2048)) {\n    shadows.getCamera().setWorldHeight(40);\n    shadows.renderIfChanged(scene, casterRevision);\n    // Supply this shadow map to the main pass's MeshRenderState3D.\n}"},{"title":"Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps — MAX_LIGHTS","note":"Related Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps example: Maximum input lights, inline indices per tile, and integer tile-record stride.","code":"public static final  int MAX_LIGHTS"},{"title":"Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps — LIGHTS_PER_TILE","note":"Related Raster 3D lighting and shadow maps example: Maximum input lights, inline indices per tile, and integer tile-record stride.","code":"public static final  int LIGHTS_PER_TILE"}],"art":1,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/05-Lighting-Studio"},{"index":35,"title":"3D physics with Jolt — Physics 3D Guide","slug":"06-Physics-3D-Guide","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"Pinned Jolt JNI compatibility","description":"The engine pins Jolt JNI 6.0.0. Its native broad-phase filter bridge reverses the object-layer and broad-phase-layer counts. PhysicsWorld3D compensates when constructing the table so all 16 user collision groups work correctly. Without that correction, non-default groups can miss contacts even though raycasts still see the geometry. Keep the complete layer-filter regression when upgrading the binding and revisit…"},{"title":"Create a world and bodies","description":"Use meters, kilograms, and seconds. Coordinates are right-handed and Z-up; the default gravity is (0, 0, -9.81). Box dimensions are full extents, and capsule and cylinder heights run along Z. Body positions describe the shape origin."},{"title":"Shapes and rendering","description":"Shape descriptions contain only Java data and can be reused across bodies and worlds. Invalid native hulls/meshes report a shape error at body creation. A moving concave mesh is not supported by this wrapper; use suitable convex collision geometry."},{"title":"Fixed steps, forces, and kinematic movement","description":"update(delta) accumulates elapsed seconds and defaults to 60 simulation steps per second, with at most eight steps per call. Long stalls discard excess whole steps to bound catch-up work. getDroppedTime() reports the discarded seconds, getStepCount() reports completed steps, and update returns the number of steps it simulated. step() advances exactly one fixed step without consuming the update accumulator."},{"title":"Collision layers, sensors, and events","description":"RigidBody3D trigger = filtered.createBody(new BodySettings3D( CollisionShape3D.box(4, 4, 2), MotionType3D.STATIC) .setSensor(true).setLayer(1)); trigger.setUserData(\"checkpoint\");"},{"title":"Physical raycasts and joints","description":"Raycasts return the nearest collider hit, including sensors, with its body, world position, outward surface normal, and distance. They use collision shapes independently of render visibility. A miss returns null; pass a nonzero ray and positive distance. Skip the call if a minimized viewport returns a null ray. Use Scene3D.pick when you want visible render-triangle selection instead."},{"title":"Capacity, threading, native resources, and packaging","description":"Keep world/body/joint operations on the thread that created the world. Native workers are internal; game listeners are still delivered on the owner thread. Exceeding body capacity throws before creation; native simulation capacity errors are reported as exceptions. optimizeBroadPhase() is available after bulk level creation."}],"examples":[{"title":"Create a world and bodies","note":"STATIC bodies are level geometry, DYNAMIC bodies respond to forces and collisions, and KINEMATIC bodies follow your commanded movement while pushing dynamic objects. Creation settings are reusable; changing them later does not modify existing bodies. You can set initial velocity, mass, friction, restitution, damping, gravity factor, collision layer, sensor…","code":"import valthorne.physics3d.*;\nimport valthorne.math.*;\nimport valthorne.graphics.model.*;\n\nPhysicsWorld3D physics = new PhysicsWorld3D();\n\nRigidBody3D floor = physics.createBody(new BodySettings3D(\n        CollisionShape3D.box(20, 20, 1), MotionType3D.STATIC)\n        .setPosition(0, 0, -0.5f));\n\nModelInstance3D crateModel = new ModelInstance3D()\n        .setModel(ModelBuilder3D.box(1, 1, 1));\nRigidBody3D crate = physics.createBody(new BodySettings3D(\n        CollisionShape3D.box(1, 1, 1), MotionType3D.DYNAMIC)\n        .setPosition(0, 0, 4)\n        .setMass(2)\n        .setFriction(0.6f)\n        .setRestitution(0.1f))\n        .bind(crateModel);\nscene.add(crateModel); // Your existing Scene3D.\n\n// Application.update(delta):\nphysics.update(delta);\n\n// Application.dispose():\nphysics.close();"},{"title":"Fixed steps, forces, and kinematic movement","note":"Each before-step listener runs once per actual substep, including catch-up steps. Command kinematic targets there so their velocity matches the fixed timestep. Dynamic bodies can also receive torque, explicit velocities, activation, and sleep.","code":"crate.addImpulse(new Vector3f(0, 0, 5));\ncrate.addImpulse(new Vector3f(3, 0, 0), worldHitPoint); // Also produces torque.\ncrate.addAngularImpulse(new Vector3f(0, 0, 1));\n\n// Forces are cleared after each physics step, so apply sustained forces here:\nphysics.addBeforeStepListener(world -> crate.addForce(new Vector3f(1, 0, 0)));\n\nRigidBody3D platform = physics.createBody(new BodySettings3D(\n        CollisionShape3D.box(3, 2, 0.3f), MotionType3D.KINEMATIC));\nphysics.addBeforeStepListener(world -> {\n    float time = (world.getStepCount() + 1) * world.getFixedTimeStep();\n    platform.moveKinematic(new Vector3f(3, 0, 1 + (float)Math.sin(time)),\n            new Quaternionf());\n});"},{"title":"Collision layers, sensors, and events","note":"There are 16 layers numbered 0–15. The symmetric matrix initially enables every pair; static/static pairs are always excluded. Configure it before world creation: the world copies the matrix. Sensors report contacts without collision response. Static sensors observe moving bodies; they do not generate static/static contacts.","code":"CollisionLayers3D layers = new CollisionLayers3D()\n        .setCollision(1, 2, false);\nPhysicsWorld3D filtered = new PhysicsWorld3D(PhysicsWorld3D.Settings.defaults(), layers);\n\nRigidBody3D trigger = filtered.createBody(new BodySettings3D(\n        CollisionShape3D.box(4, 4, 2), MotionType3D.STATIC)\n        .setSensor(true).setLayer(1));\ntrigger.setUserData(\"checkpoint\");\n\nfiltered.addContactListener(event -> {\n    if (event.type() == ContactEvent3D.Type.ADDED\n            && (event.bodyA() == trigger || event.bodyB() == trigger)) {\n        // Handle entry into the trigger.\n    }\n});"},{"title":"Physical raycasts and joints","note":"Raycasts return the nearest collider hit, including sensors, with its body, world position, outward surface normal, and distance. They use collision shapes independently of render visibility. A miss returns null; pass a nonzero ray and positive distance. Skip the call if a minimized viewport returns a null ray. Use Scene3D.pick when you want visible…","code":"PhysicsRayHit3D hit = physics.raycast(viewport.screenToRay(mouseX, mouseY), 100);\nif (hit != null && hit.body().getMotionType() == MotionType3D.DYNAMIC) {\n    hit.body().addImpulse(new Vector3f(0, 0, 3), hit.position());\n}\n\nDistanceJoint3D tether = physics.createDistanceJoint(\n        anchorBody, hangingBody, anchorWorldPoint, hangingWorldPoint, 2, 2);\n// Equal minimum/maximum lengths make a fixed-length tether.\ntether.close();"},{"title":"Capacity, threading, native resources, and packaging","note":"Keep world/body/joint operations on the thread that created the world. Native workers are internal; game listeners are still delivered on the owner thread. Exceeding body capacity throws before creation; native simulation capacity errors are reported as exceptions. optimizeBroadPhase() is available after bulk level creation.","code":"PhysicsWorld3D.Settings settings = new PhysicsWorld3D.Settings(\n        1f / 120f, // Fixed timestep.\n        8,         // Maximum catch-up substeps per update.\n        4096,      // Maximum bodies.\n        65536,     // Maximum body pairs.\n        20480,     // Maximum contact constraints.\n        2);        // Native worker threads; 0 uses the single-threaded job system."}],"art":5,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/06-Physics-3D-Guide"},{"index":36,"title":"3D physics with Jolt — Physics 3D Reference","slug":"06-Physics-3D-Reference","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"Pinned Jolt JNI compatibility","description":"The engine pins Jolt JNI 6.0.0. Its native broad-phase filter bridge reverses the object-layer and broad-phase-layer counts. PhysicsWorld3D compensates when constructing the table so all 16 user collision groups work correctly. Without that correction, non-default groups can miss contacts even though raycasts still see the geometry. Keep the complete layer-filter regression when upgrading the binding and revisit…"},{"title":"Create a world and bodies","description":"Use meters, kilograms, and seconds. Coordinates are right-handed and Z-up; the default gravity is (0, 0, -9.81). Box dimensions are full extents, and capsule and cylinder heights run along Z. Body positions describe the shape origin."},{"title":"Shapes and rendering","description":"Shape descriptions contain only Java data and can be reused across bodies and worlds. Invalid native hulls/meshes report a shape error at body creation. A moving concave mesh is not supported by this wrapper; use suitable convex collision geometry."},{"title":"Fixed steps, forces, and kinematic movement","description":"update(delta) accumulates elapsed seconds and defaults to 60 simulation steps per second, with at most eight steps per call. Long stalls discard excess whole steps to bound catch-up work. getDroppedTime() reports the discarded seconds, getStepCount() reports completed steps, and update returns the number of steps it simulated. step() advances exactly one fixed step without consuming the update accumulator."},{"title":"Collision layers, sensors, and events","description":"RigidBody3D trigger = filtered.createBody(new BodySettings3D( CollisionShape3D.box(4, 4, 2), MotionType3D.STATIC) .setSensor(true).setLayer(1)); trigger.setUserData(\"checkpoint\");"},{"title":"Physical raycasts and joints","description":"Raycasts return the nearest collider hit, including sensors, with its body, world position, outward surface normal, and distance. They use collision shapes independently of render visibility. A miss returns null; pass a nonzero ray and positive distance. Skip the call if a minimized viewport returns a null ray. Use Scene3D.pick when you want visible render-triangle selection instead."},{"title":"Capacity, threading, native resources, and packaging","description":"Keep world/body/joint operations on the thread that created the world. Native workers are internal; game listeners are still delivered on the owner thread. Exceeding body capacity throws before creation; native simulation capacity errors are reported as exceptions. optimizeBroadPhase() is available after bulk level creation."}],"examples":[{"title":"Create a world and bodies","note":"STATIC bodies are level geometry, DYNAMIC bodies respond to forces and collisions, and KINEMATIC bodies follow your commanded movement while pushing dynamic objects. Creation settings are reusable; changing them later does not modify existing bodies. You can set initial velocity, mass, friction, restitution, damping, gravity factor, collision layer, sensor…","code":"import valthorne.physics3d.*;\nimport valthorne.math.*;\nimport valthorne.graphics.model.*;\n\nPhysicsWorld3D physics = new PhysicsWorld3D();\n\nRigidBody3D floor = physics.createBody(new BodySettings3D(\n        CollisionShape3D.box(20, 20, 1), MotionType3D.STATIC)\n        .setPosition(0, 0, -0.5f));\n\nModelInstance3D crateModel = new ModelInstance3D()\n        .setModel(ModelBuilder3D.box(1, 1, 1));\nRigidBody3D crate = physics.createBody(new BodySettings3D(\n        CollisionShape3D.box(1, 1, 1), MotionType3D.DYNAMIC)\n        .setPosition(0, 0, 4)\n        .setMass(2)\n        .setFriction(0.6f)\n        .setRestitution(0.1f))\n        .bind(crateModel);\nscene.add(crateModel); // Your existing Scene3D.\n\n// Application.update(delta):\nphysics.update(delta);\n\n// Application.dispose():\nphysics.close();"},{"title":"Fixed steps, forces, and kinematic movement","note":"Each before-step listener runs once per actual substep, including catch-up steps. Command kinematic targets there so their velocity matches the fixed timestep. Dynamic bodies can also receive torque, explicit velocities, activation, and sleep.","code":"crate.addImpulse(new Vector3f(0, 0, 5));\ncrate.addImpulse(new Vector3f(3, 0, 0), worldHitPoint); // Also produces torque.\ncrate.addAngularImpulse(new Vector3f(0, 0, 1));\n\n// Forces are cleared after each physics step, so apply sustained forces here:\nphysics.addBeforeStepListener(world -> crate.addForce(new Vector3f(1, 0, 0)));\n\nRigidBody3D platform = physics.createBody(new BodySettings3D(\n        CollisionShape3D.box(3, 2, 0.3f), MotionType3D.KINEMATIC));\nphysics.addBeforeStepListener(world -> {\n    float time = (world.getStepCount() + 1) * world.getFixedTimeStep();\n    platform.moveKinematic(new Vector3f(3, 0, 1 + (float)Math.sin(time)),\n            new Quaternionf());\n});"},{"title":"Collision layers, sensors, and events","note":"There are 16 layers numbered 0–15. The symmetric matrix initially enables every pair; static/static pairs are always excluded. Configure it before world creation: the world copies the matrix. Sensors report contacts without collision response. Static sensors observe moving bodies; they do not generate static/static contacts.","code":"CollisionLayers3D layers = new CollisionLayers3D()\n        .setCollision(1, 2, false);\nPhysicsWorld3D filtered = new PhysicsWorld3D(PhysicsWorld3D.Settings.defaults(), layers);\n\nRigidBody3D trigger = filtered.createBody(new BodySettings3D(\n        CollisionShape3D.box(4, 4, 2), MotionType3D.STATIC)\n        .setSensor(true).setLayer(1));\ntrigger.setUserData(\"checkpoint\");\n\nfiltered.addContactListener(event -> {\n    if (event.type() == ContactEvent3D.Type.ADDED\n            && (event.bodyA() == trigger || event.bodyB() == trigger)) {\n        // Handle entry into the trigger.\n    }\n});"},{"title":"Physical raycasts and joints","note":"Raycasts return the nearest collider hit, including sensors, with its body, world position, outward surface normal, and distance. They use collision shapes independently of render visibility. A miss returns null; pass a nonzero ray and positive distance. Skip the call if a minimized viewport returns a null ray. Use Scene3D.pick when you want visible…","code":"PhysicsRayHit3D hit = physics.raycast(viewport.screenToRay(mouseX, mouseY), 100);\nif (hit != null && hit.body().getMotionType() == MotionType3D.DYNAMIC) {\n    hit.body().addImpulse(new Vector3f(0, 0, 3), hit.position());\n}\n\nDistanceJoint3D tether = physics.createDistanceJoint(\n        anchorBody, hangingBody, anchorWorldPoint, hangingWorldPoint, 2, 2);\n// Equal minimum/maximum lengths make a fixed-length tether.\ntether.close();"},{"title":"Capacity, threading, native resources, and packaging","note":"Keep world/body/joint operations on the thread that created the world. Native workers are internal; game listeners are still delivered on the owner thread. Exceeding body capacity throws before creation; native simulation capacity errors are reported as exceptions. optimizeBroadPhase() is available after bulk level creation.","code":"PhysicsWorld3D.Settings settings = new PhysicsWorld3D.Settings(\n        1f / 120f, // Fixed timestep.\n        8,         // Maximum catch-up substeps per update.\n        4096,      // Maximum bodies.\n        65536,     // Maximum body pairs.\n        20480,     // Maximum contact constraints.\n        2);        // Native worker threads; 0 uses the single-threaded job system."}],"art":5,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/06-Physics-3D-Reference"},{"index":37,"title":"Jolt rigid-body physics","slug":"06-Physics-Jolt-API","summary":"PhysicsWorld3D owns a native Jolt simulation with fixed-step advancement. BodySettings3D describes initial body state; CollisionShape3D describes its collision geometry. RigidBody3D exposes runtime controls and can synchronize a visual model with native simulation.","capabilities":[{"title":"Motion types","description":"Static, kinematic, and dynamic bodies serve stationary geometry, controlled motion, and simulation-driven motion."},{"title":"Shapes and layers","description":"Primitive/mesh colliders define contact geometry; layer rules filter interactions."},{"title":"Forces and impulses","description":"Forces act over simulation time, while impulses directly change momentum."},{"title":"Sensors and contacts","description":"Sensors report overlap without ordinary solid response; contact events expose interaction."},{"title":"Queries and joints","description":"Raycasts inspect the world and distance joints constrain body relationships."},{"title":"Step listeners","description":"Before/after-step hooks integrate systems that must share the physics clock."}],"examples":[{"title":"Creation and Reuse","note":"The world reads these values at creation; changing this object afterward does not update existing bodies. Position and rotation setters copy their inputs, while the shape definition is retained by reference. Construction allocates no native body. Do not mutate settings concurrently with body creation.","code":"BodySettings3D settings = new BodySettings3D(shape, MotionType3D.DYNAMIC)\n        .setMass(2f)\n        .setPosition(0, 0, 5)\n        .setRestitution(0.25f);\nRigidBody3D first = world.createBody(settings);\nsettings.setPosition(3, 0, 5);\nRigidBody3D second = world.createBody(settings);"},{"title":"CollisionLayers3D","note":"This mutable configuration has no synchronization. Finish configuring it before constructing a world that reads it.","code":"CollisionLayers3D layers = new CollisionLayers3D();\nlayers.setCollision(1, 2, false); // Disable both 1-to-2 and 2-to-1 collisions.\nPhysicsWorld3D world = new PhysicsWorld3D(PhysicsWorld3D.Settings.defaults(), layers);"},{"title":"Creating a Body","note":"A definition can be reused for multiple bodies and worlds. Each native creation invokes its factory again; this object does not hold a shared native shape instance. Model and vector inputs are captured when the definition is made, so subsequent source edits do not update collision geometry. Convex shapes support moving bodies; triangle meshes require…","code":"CollisionShape3D shape = CollisionShape3D.box(1f, 1f, 2f);\nBodySettings3D settings = new BodySettings3D(shape, MotionType3D.DYNAMIC)\n        .setPosition(0, 0, 5);\nRigidBody3D body = world.createBody(settings);"},{"title":"PhysicsWorld3D","note":"update(float) accumulates elapsed time, advances a bounded number of fixed steps, and discards excess whole steps after stalls. step() advances one fixed step independently of that accumulator. Bound models normally receive the latest pose; call syncModels(boolean) with true after updating to interpolate visuals with one step of display latency. Models…","code":"try (PhysicsWorld3D world = new PhysicsWorld3D()) {\n    RigidBody3D body = world.createBody(bodySettings);\n    body.bind(model);\n    world.update(frameSeconds);\n    world.syncModels(true);\n}"},{"title":"BodySettings3D — Constructor","note":"Retains the shape and motion category and initializes all other settings to their defaults. Static-only shapes are rejected for moving categories; choose a convex shape for a dynamic or kinematic body.","code":"public BodySettings3D(CollisionShape3D shape, MotionType3D motion)"}],"art":5,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/06-Physics-Jolt-API"},{"index":38,"title":"Advanced UI controls","slug":"07-UI-Advanced","summary":"All components use the existing UI tree and accept texture or Nano nodes without conversion. They are UI-thread-only. Resources follow the normal attach/detach lifecycle; retain durable data outside transient virtual rows.","capabilities":[{"title":"Variable-height lists and selection","description":"Measurements are explicit, not inferred by measuring every offscreen widget. rowHeight supplies the initial estimate; calling it again returns to fixed-height mode. Measurements preserve the visible scroll anchor and coalesce geometry work. Variable heights require one column. Changing gap adjusts all measurements and is a data-scale operation. itemCount preserves existing measurements and initializes new indices…"},{"title":"Lazy tabs and split panes","description":"Tabs instantiate pages once on first selection, retain their state and skip updates and drawing for inactive pages. Hidden pages remain attached and participate in full-tree layout/lifecycle operations. Headers support Left/Right/Home/End and skip disabled headers. Switching away from a focused page moves focus to the new header. Removing a page runs normal destruction once. Factories must return fresh unattached…"},{"title":"Searchable, sortable data tables","description":"The header stays fixed while the virtualized rows scroll. Click or keyboard-activate a header to cycle ascending, descending and original order. Sorting is stable: equal keys keep source order. Column widths are proportional weights, not pixels. The convenience text column sorts lexicographically, with nulls first. Supply typed comparators for numeric/date values and pure cell factories returning fresh unattached…"},{"title":"Collapsible sections","description":"The header supports mouse/Enter/Space activation and Left/Right collapse/expand. Collapsed contents retain state but are not drawn or updated, and the body consumes zero layout height. Collapsing focused or captured content returns focus to the header and cancels active capture. Re-expanding does not recreate the contents. The normal destruction lifecycle runs when the section is removed. Stack sections inside a…"},{"title":"Notifications and text costs","description":"ChangeSignal has allocation-free dispatch with a copy-on-mutation listener array. Subscribe/unsubscribe costs O(listener count); dispatch uses a stable snapshot. Changes during callbacks affect the next dispatch, including nested dispatch. Subscriptions are independent and closing them is idempotent. Callbacks are synchronous; exceptions propagate and stop remaining callbacks. Close externally owned subscriptions…"},{"title":"Remaining capability roadmap","description":"This is an expanded foundation, not \"every possible UI feature\". Significant remaining systems include virtual trees and advanced table column management, docking/reordering, drag-and-drop payload routing, menu bars/context menus, multiline/rich text, full text shaping/bidi/font fallback/IME composition, native accessibility bridges, animation scheduling, gamepad navigation, reusable row pools, and GPU…"}],"examples":[{"title":"Variable-height lists and selection","note":"Measurements are explicit, not inferred by measuring every offscreen widget. rowHeight supplies the initial estimate; calling it again returns to fixed-height mode. Measurements preserve the visible scroll anchor and coalesce geometry work. Variable heights require one column. Changing gap adjusts all measurements and is a data-scale operation. itemCount…","code":"VirtualList records = new VirtualList(100_000, index -> new NanoButton(\"Record \" + index));\nrecords.rowHeight(40).gap(4).variableHeights().selectable(true);\nrecords.getLayout().width(600).height(500);\nroot.add(records);\nrecords.itemHeight(12, 96); // content height excluding gap\nrecords.itemHeight(13, 72); // coalesced until the next layout/update\nrecords.getSelection().onChange(() -> {\n    int selectedCount = records.getSelection().selectedCount();\n});"},{"title":"Lazy tabs and split panes","note":"Tabs instantiate pages once on first selection, retain their state and skip updates and drawing for inactive pages. Hidden pages remain attached and participate in full-tree layout/lifecycle operations. Headers support Left/Right/Home/End and skip disabled headers. Switching away from a focused page moves focus to the new header. Removing a page runs…","code":"TabbedPane tabs = new TabbedPane();\ntabs.addTab(\"Document\", () -> new NanoTextField(\"Retained editor\"));\ntabs.addTab(\"Settings\", Panel::new);\nVirtualList records = new VirtualList(10_000, i -> new Button(\"Record \" + i));\nSplitPane workspace = new SplitPane(tabs, records)\n        .ratio(.55f).minimumSizes(200, 180).dividerSize(10);\nworkspace.getLayout().width(1000).height(600);\nroot.add(workspace);"},{"title":"Searchable, sortable data tables","note":"The header stays fixed while the virtualized rows scroll. Click or keyboard-activate a header to cycle ascending, descending and original order. Sorting is stable: equal keys keep source order. Column widths are proportional weights, not pixels. The convenience text column sorts lexicographically, with nulls first. Supply typed comparators for numeric/date…","code":"DataTable<MyRecord> table = new DataTable<>(List.of(\n    TableColumn.text(\"Name\", 3, MyRecord::name),\n    new TableColumn<>(\"Count\", 1,\n        row -> new Label(Integer.toString(row.count())),\n        Comparator.comparingInt(MyRecord::count))\n));\ntable.getLayout().width(800).height(500);\ntable.rows(records);\nroot.add(table);\ntable.filter(row -> row.name().contains(query));"},{"title":"Collapsible sections","note":"The header supports mouse/Enter/Space activation and Left/Right collapse/expand. Collapsed contents retain state but are not drawn or updated, and the body consumes zero layout height. Collapsing focused or captured content returns focus to the header and cancels active capture. Re-expanding does not recreate the contents. The normal destruction lifecycle…","code":"CollapsibleSection details = new CollapsibleSection(\"Details\", form);\ndetails.expanded(false);\nroot.add(details);"},{"title":"Standard UI controls — Checkbox example","note":"Related Standard UI controls example: - Changing the checked state triggers the resolved action if one exists","code":"Checkbox checkbox = new Checkbox();\n\ncheckbox.getLayout()\n        .width(24)\n        .height(24);\n\ncheckbox.action(c -> {\n    System.out.println(\"Checked: \" + c.isChecked());\n});\n\ncheckbox.checked(true);\ncheckbox.toggle();\n\nboolean checked = checkbox.isChecked();\nNodeAction<Checkbox> action = checkbox.getAction();\n\ncheckbox.draw(batch);"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Advanced"},{"index":39,"title":"Shared UI behavior and editing models","slug":"07-UI-Behavior","summary":"Behavior models keep interaction state reusable across standard and NanoVG controls. They cover activation, ranges, scrolling, text editing, and change notification. Use these models when you need custom painting without rewriting the underlying input policy.","capabilities":[{"title":"Activation","description":"Shared keyboard and pointer acceptance rules invoke an action and consume accepted input."},{"title":"Range state","description":"RangeModel converts values and pointer positions within configured bounds."},{"title":"Text editing","description":"TextEditModel tracks text, caret, selection, validation, and undo/redo state."},{"title":"Scroll policy","description":"ScrollBehavior computes clamped offsets and consumes only effective wheel movement."},{"title":"Change signals","description":"Listeners observe state changes so a view can refresh its caches."}],"examples":[{"title":"Range and Stepping","note":"Pointer coordinates are top-left-local track coordinates. Horizontal values increase to the right; vertical values increase upward. Instances are mutable and intended to be owned by the UI dispatch thread.","code":"RangeModel range = new RangeModel(10, 97, 20);\nrange.step(5);\nrange.percent(1); // Exact maximum 97, even though it is off the step grid.\nrange.increment(-1); // Applies one negative step and snaps within the range."},{"title":"TextEditModel","note":"Programmatic text replacement sanitizes content, collapses selection, and clears history. Insertion and deletion retain up to 100 undo states. A lowered maximum does not truncate existing text and still permits edits that do not increase an already-over-limit code-point count. Notifications can report text, selection, or validator changes and are not…","code":"TextEditModel model = new TextEditModel();\nmodel.maxLength(40);\nmodel.text(\"Hello\");\nmodel.move(model.text().length(), false);\nmodel.insert(\" world\");\nmodel.undo();"},{"title":"key","note":"Consumes an Enter or Space press on an enabled node and immediately invokes the action. Other keys and disabled nodes leave the event untouched. This helper does not independently verify focus or suppress key repeats.","code":"public static void key(UINode node, KeyPressEvent event, Runnable action)"},{"title":"release","note":"Runs an action when UINode#isActivationRelease(MouseReleaseEvent) accepts the release. That policy requires the left button, an enabled node attached to a root, and this node as the clickable hit-test result at the release position. It does not itself establish a matching earlier press.","code":"public static void release(UINode node, MouseReleaseEvent event, Runnable action)"},{"title":"subscribe","note":"Appends a listener without invoking it and returns a handle for removing only this registration. Duplicate callback instances are allowed. Closing the handle repeatedly is harmless and does not affect other registrations. An ongoing dispatch retains its original snapshot and does not see this addition; any later dispatch captures the updated array.","code":"public AutoCloseable subscribe(Runnable callback)"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Behavior"},{"index":40,"title":"Standard UI controls","slug":"07-UI-Controls","summary":"Standard controls render through the texture-oriented UI path. Choose controls by interaction rather than appearance alone: buttons activate, checkboxes toggle, sliders select a range value, and text fields edit text. Containers such as panels, grids, tabs, split panes, and modals organize other controls.","capabilities":[{"title":"Actions and values","description":"Button, checkbox, slider, and progress controls expose different interaction/value contracts."},{"title":"Text and images","description":"Label, Image, and DrawableNode display borrowed visual resources."},{"title":"Containers","description":"Panel, Grid, ScrollPanel, SplitPane, and TabbedPane arrange or reveal content."},{"title":"Transient UI","description":"Modal and Tooltip provide temporary or contextual information."},{"title":"Collapsible content","description":"CollapsibleSection hides or reveals a related group without replacing the application screen."},{"title":"Live curve labels","description":"SlugLabel draws Slug outlines with retained layout and updates measured dimensions when text or em size changes."}],"examples":[{"title":"Checkbox example","note":"- Changing the checked state triggers the resolved action if one exists","code":"Checkbox checkbox = new Checkbox();\n\ncheckbox.getLayout()\n        .width(24)\n        .height(24);\n\ncheckbox.action(c -> {\n    System.out.println(\"Checked: \" + c.isChecked());\n});\n\ncheckbox.checked(true);\ncheckbox.toggle();\n\nboolean checked = checkbox.isChecked();\nNodeAction<Checkbox> action = checkbox.getAction();\n\ncheckbox.draw(batch);"},{"title":"Grid example","note":"This class is especially useful for icon grids, inventory slots, gallery layouts, menu tiles, or any other UI where children should be laid out in a consistent multi-column arrangement.","code":"Grid grid = new Grid()\n        .columns(4)\n        .cellSize(64, 64)\n        .gap(8);\n\ngrid.add(new Button(\"One\"));\ngrid.add(new Button(\"Two\"));\ngrid.add(new Button(\"Three\"));\ngrid.add(new Button(\"Four\"));\ngrid.add(new Button(\"Five\"));\n\nint columns = grid.getColumns();\nLayoutValue width = grid.getCellWidth();\nLayoutValue height = grid.getCellHeight();\n\ngrid.update(delta);\ngrid.draw(batch);"},{"title":"Image example","note":"The class is intentionally minimal. It does not manage color tinting, region selection, scaling policies, or interaction behavior on its own. Instead, it serves as a lightweight textured UI element that can participate in the broader layout and node lifecycle system.","code":"Texture logo = new Texture(\"assets/ui/logo.png\");\n\nImage image = new Image(logo);\nimage.getLayout()\n     .width(128)\n     .height(128);\n\nTexture current = image.getTexture();\nimage.texture(new Texture(\"assets/ui/other.png\"));\n\nimage.update(delta);\nimage.draw(batch);"},{"title":"Label example","note":"The class does not perform its own interaction behavior and is typically used as a child inside higher-level components such as buttons, tooltips, or form controls.","code":"Label label = new Label(\"Hello World\");\n\nString text = label.getText();\nlabel.text(\"Updated Text\");\n\nlabel.update(delta);\nlabel.draw(batch);"},{"title":"Modal example","note":"When the modal is open and attached to a root, it is shown through the root's overlay mechanism. This ensures it renders above the normal UI tree. The modal may also be used without a root, in which case opening and closing only affect its visibility flag.","code":"Modal modal = new Modal(parentNode);\n\nLabel message = new Label(\"Are you sure?\");\nmodal.content(message)\n     .closeOnEscape(true)\n     .closeOnOutsideClick(true);\n\nPanel dialog = modal.getDialog();\nUINode content = modal.getContent();\nUINode owner = modal.getModalParent();\n\nmodal.open();\n\nboolean open = modal.isOpen();\n\nmodal.toggle();\nmodal.close();\n\nmodal.draw(batch);"},{"title":"Panel example","note":"- a layout wrapper for content blocks","code":"Panel panel = new Panel();\n\npanel.getLayout()\n     .width(300)\n     .height(200)\n     .padding(12);\n\npanel.add(new Label(\"Settings\"));\npanel.add(new Button(\"Apply\"));\n\nroot.add(panel);\n// Let the root's normal layout, update and draw lifecycle visit the panel."},{"title":"ProgressBar example","note":"When percentage display is enabled and a font is available, the bar caches a percentage string based on the animated value and draws it explicitly. Disabling percentage display clears the borrowed font's legacy text property. Drawable and font resources are borrowed from resolved style and are not disposed here. Although this class extends Panel, its…","code":"ProgressBar bar = new ProgressBar(0f, 100f);\n\nbar.getLayout()\n   .width(240)\n   .height(28);\n\nbar.progress(45f)\n   .displayPercentage(true)\n   .horizontal(true);\n\nfloat current = bar.getProgress();\nboolean vertical = bar.isVertical();\nboolean showingPercent = bar.isDisplayPercentage();\nFont font = bar.getFont();\n\nroot.add(bar);\n// The root's normal update and draw lifecycle visits the progress bar."},{"title":"ScrollPanel example","note":"Scrollbar geometry is computed lazily through an internal ScrollMetrics structure. This includes bar bounds, thumb bounds, visibility flags, and the space consumed by active scrollbars.","code":"ScrollPanel panel = new ScrollPanel()\n        .horizontal(true)\n        .vertical(true)\n        .horizontalBar(true)\n        .verticalBar(true)\n        .scrollSpeed(48f);\n\nGrid content = new Grid()\n        .columns(4)\n        .cellSize(64, 64)\n        .gap(8);\n\npanel.setContent(content);\n\npanel.scroll(0f, 0f);\npanel.scrollBy(24f, 48f);\n\nfloat x = panel.getScrollX();\nfloat y = panel.getScrollY();\nfloat maxX = panel.getMaxScrollX();\nfloat maxY = panel.getMaxScrollY();\n\npanel.update(delta);\npanel.draw(batch);"},{"title":"ScrollPanel.ScrollMetrics — internal support type example","note":"It is intentionally private and static because it only exists to support the internal layout and rendering calculations of ScrollPanel.","code":"// Internal to ScrollPanel:\nScrollMetrics metrics = getScrollMetrics();\n\nif (metrics.showHorizontalBar) {\n    // draw horizontal track and thumb\n}\n\nif (metrics.showVerticalBar) {\n    // draw vertical track and thumb\n}"},{"title":"Slider example","note":"- otherwise resolve ACTION_KEY from the current style","code":"Slider slider = new Slider(0f, 100f, 25f);\n\nslider.getLayout()\n      .width(220)\n      .height(24);\n\nslider.stepSize(5f)\n      .horizontal(true)\n      .action(s -> {\n          System.out.println(\"Slider value: \" + s.getValue());\n      });\n\nslider.increment();\nslider.decrement();\nslider.percent(0.5f);\n\nfloat value = slider.getValue();\nfloat percent = slider.getPercent();\nboolean dragging = slider.isDragging();\n\nslider.update(delta);\nslider.draw(batch);"},{"title":"SlugLabel","note":"The shared batch must be idle when this node draws. Container clipping is forwarded for glyph rejection and CPU quad cropping in the text's world-coordinate space.","code":"SlugLabel label = new SlugLabel(font, slugBatch, \"Score: 0\", 24f);\ncontainer.add(label);\nlabel.text(\"Score: 100\").color(Color.WHITE);"},{"title":"SplitPane","note":"Two mixed-renderer panes separated by a captured, keyboard-accessible divider. Vertical means top/bottom; horizontal means left/right. When minimum sizes cannot fit, available space is shared proportionally. Requested ratio survives resizing. Manage pane contents through first()/second(), not inherited structural methods. Initial layout is horizontal with…","code":"SplitPane split = new SplitPane(new Panel(), new Panel());\nsplit.ratio(0.3f).minimumSizes(120, 200).dividerSize(8);\nsplit.getLayout().width(800).height(600);"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Controls"},{"index":41,"title":"UI roots, nodes, and input routing","slug":"07-UI-Core","summary":"UIRoot coordinates a tree of UINodes: layout, drawing, focus, pointer capture, keyboard/text routing, overlays, and modal scopes. UIContainer owns child relationships. Start here before using individual controls so the tree receives a coherent lifecycle and coordinate system.","capabilities":[{"title":"Tree lifecycle","description":"Attachment, creation, update, drawing, removal, and disposal define when a node participates."},{"title":"Focus and routing","description":"Preview, target handling, and bubbling allow controls and ancestors to share an event."},{"title":"Pointer capture","description":"A drag can remain attached to a control after the cursor leaves its bounds."},{"title":"Overlays and modals","description":"Transient content draws above ordinary children; modal scopes restrict eligible input."},{"title":"Mixed rendering","description":"UIRenderContext coordinates texture and NanoVG phases."}],"examples":[{"title":"Yoga Layout Integration example","note":"Layout updates propagate through the tree and are eventually resolved by UIRoot#layout().","code":"UIContainer panel = new Panel();\n\nLabel label = new Label();\nButton button = new Button();\n\npanel.add(label);\npanel.add(button);\n\nroot.add(panel);\n\n// Update loop\npanel.update(delta);\n\n// Rendering\npanel.draw(batch);"},{"title":"Usage flow example","note":"- Read render-space values through getRenderX(), getRenderY(), getWidth(), and getHeight().","code":"public class MyNode extends UINode {\n\n    @Override\n    public void onCreate() {\n        getLayout().width(Value.pixels(120));\n        getLayout().height(Value.pixels(40));\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onDestroy() {\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void update(float delta) {\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void draw(TextureBatch batch) {\n        ResolvedStyle style = getStyle();\n        if (style != null) {\n            Drawable background = style.get(Panel.BACKGROUND_KEY);\n            if (background != null) {\n                background.draw(batch, getRenderX(), getRenderY(), getWidth(), getHeight());\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"},{"title":"Custom Container Integration","note":"A container can use drawChildren(UIContainer, UINode) to retain compatible sibling painting state without changing traversal order. Scoped translations and clips can be balanced with try-with-resources:","code":"UIRenderContext context = getRoot().getRenderContext();\nTextureBatch activeBatch = context.getBatch();\nchild.render(activeBatch);"},{"title":"Custom Container Integration (2)","note":"A container can use drawChildren(UIContainer, UINode) to retain compatible sibling painting state without changing traversal order. Scoped translations and clips can be balanced with try-with-resources:","code":"try (UIRenderContext.Scope offset = context.translate(12, 8)) {\n    context.drawChildren(container, null);\n}"},{"title":"UIRoot example","note":"- Call dispose() on shutdown","code":"UIRoot root = new UIRoot();\n\nPanel panel = new Panel();\npanel.getLayout()\n     .width(300)\n     .height(200)\n     .left(20)\n     .top(20);\n\nroot.add(panel);\nroot.layout();\n\n// Game loop\nroot.update(delta);\nroot.draw();\n\n// Focus management\nroot.focusNext();\nUINode focused = root.getFocused();\n\n// Optional viewport support\nroot.setViewport(viewport);\n\n// Overlay usage\nTooltip tooltip = new Tooltip();\nroot.showOverlay(tooltip);\nroot.hideOverlay(tooltip);\n\n// Shutdown\nroot.dispose();"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Core"},{"index":42,"title":"Virtual lists, tables, and selection","slug":"07-UI-Data","summary":"VirtualList and DataTable render large datasets by materializing only the visible row range. TableModel stores the source and filtered/sorted projection; SelectionModel handles selection policy. RowHeightIndex supports efficient offsets when rows are not all the same height.","capabilities":[{"title":"Virtualization","description":"Visible-range materialization limits the number of active row widgets."},{"title":"Binding","description":"Factories create row widgets and binders update them for the current data item."},{"title":"Sorting and filtering","description":"A visible projection can differ from source order and membership."},{"title":"Columns","description":"TableColumn describes displayed values, sizing, and optional comparison behavior."},{"title":"Selection and scrolling","description":"Selection tracks logical choices while scroll methods reveal projection indices."}],"examples":[{"title":"RowHeightIndex","note":"This mutable index is intended for UI-thread use and provides no synchronization. Successful queries and point updates allocate no objects; resizing replaces backing arrays. Empty indexes are supported, with zero total height and zero as the end sentinel returned by position lookup.","code":"RowHeightIndex rows = new RowHeightIndex(1000, 28f);\nrows.set(3, 52f);\ndouble fifthRowTop = rows.offset(4);\nint firstVisible = rows.indexAt(120);\nif (firstVisible < rows.size()) {\n    float visibleRowStride = rows.height(firstVisible);\n}"},{"title":"SelectionModel","note":"Selections refer to positions, not data identities. Changing item order requires application-level remapping or clearing; changing the item count only removes out-of-range membership and adjusts invalid anchor/lead values. This model does not manipulate focus, scroll position or rendered item state.","code":"SelectionModel selection = new SelectionModel(100, true);\nselection.select(10, false, false);\nselection.select(15, true, false);\nselection.select(30, false, true);\nselection.forEachSelected(index -> System.out.println(index));"},{"title":"DataTable","note":"- — row data type","code":"DataTable<String> table = new DataTable<>(List.of(\n        TableColumn.text(\"Name\", 1f, value -> value)));\ntable.rows(List.of(\"Maple\", \"Cedar\", \"Oak\"));\ntable.getLayout().width(400).height(300);\ntable.toggleSort(0);"},{"title":"VirtualList","note":"Measured heights are supplied explicitly, not automatically read from row widgets. Internal row storage owns the scroll content: do not replace it through inherited content setters. Use the list on its owning UI thread.","code":"VirtualList list = new VirtualList(10000, index -> new Button(\"Item \" + index));\nlist.rowHeight(36).overscan(3).selectable(false);\nlist.getLayout().width(400).height(500);"},{"title":"RowHeightIndex — Constructor","note":"Creates an index with every row initialized to the same estimated stride. The estimate is validated even for an empty index, and the cumulative structure is built immediately in linear time.","code":"public RowHeightIndex(int count, float estimatedHeight)"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Data"},{"index":43,"title":"UI layout and alignment","slug":"07-UI-Layout","summary":"Layout wraps Yoga's flexbox inputs and exposes computed node geometry through the UI tree. Use LayoutValue and LayoutUnit to distinguish automatic, absolute-point, and percentage values. Layout is a constraint calculation, so a requested dimension and a computed dimension are different states.","capabilities":[{"title":"Flex flow","description":"Direction, wrapping, grow/shrink behavior, and basis determine how children occupy space."},{"title":"Alignment","description":"Main-axis justification and cross-axis alignment position children and wrapped lines."},{"title":"Spacing","description":"Margins, padding, and gaps affect different boundaries."},{"title":"Constraints","description":"Minimum/maximum sizes and position mode constrain or remove items from normal flow."},{"title":"Geometry interfaces","description":"Locatable, Sizeable, and Dimensional expose position/size contracts used by layout and alignment tools."}],"examples":[{"title":"Align example","note":"Alignment affects how children are positioned relative to the container's cross axis (the axis perpendicular to the main flex direction).","code":"Panel panel = new Panel();\n\npanel.getLayout()\n     .flexDirection(FlexDirection.ROW)\n     .alignItems(Align.CENTER);\n\nint yogaAlign = Align.CENTER.yoga();"},{"title":"Reusable vector note example","note":"The combined align(Dimensional, Sizeable, Alignment, Alignment) methods return a reused internal Vector2f instance for performance. That means the returned vector should be used immediately and not stored long-term if another alignment call may happen later.","code":"Element panel = new Element();\npanel.setPosition(100f, 50f);\npanel.setSize(300f, 120f);\n\nFont label = new Font(fontData);\nlabel.setText(\"Play\");\n\nVector2f pos = Alignment.align(panel, label, Alignment.CENTER, Alignment.CENTER);\nlabel.setPosition(pos.x(), pos.y());\n\nVector2f bottomLeft = Alignment.align(panel, label, Alignment.START, Alignment.START);\nlabel.setPosition(bottomLeft.x(), bottomLeft.y());\n\nfloat rightX = Alignment.alignHorizontally(panel, label, Alignment.END);\nfloat centerY = Alignment.alignVertically(panel, label, Alignment.CENTER);\nlabel.setPosition(rightX, centerY);"},{"title":"FlexDirection example","note":"These values map directly to Yoga's YGFlexDirection constants.","code":"Panel panel = new Panel();\n\npanel.getLayout()\n     .flexDirection(FlexDirection.COLUMN);\n\nint yogaDirection = FlexDirection.COLUMN.yoga();"},{"title":"FlexWrap example","note":"This directly maps to Yoga's YGWrap values.","code":"Panel panel = new Panel();\n\npanel.getLayout()\n     .flexWrap(FlexWrap.WRAP);\n\nint yogaWrap = FlexWrap.WRAP.yoga();"},{"title":"JustifyContent example","note":"These values correspond directly to Yoga's YGJustify constants.","code":"Panel panel = new Panel();\n\npanel.getLayout()\n     .flexDirection(FlexDirection.ROW)\n     .justifyContent(JustifyContent.SPACE_BETWEEN);\n\nint yogaValue = JustifyContent.SPACE_BETWEEN.yoga();"},{"title":"LayoutUnit example","note":"- PERCENT \\u2014 percentage relative to parent","code":"Layout layout = element.getLayout();\n\nlayout.width(200, LayoutUnit.POINT);\nlayout.height(50, LayoutUnit.PERCENT);"},{"title":"Overflow example","note":"These values map directly to Yoga's YGOverflow constants.","code":"Panel panel = new Panel();\n\npanel.getLayout()\n     .overflow(Overflow.HIDDEN);\n\nint yogaOverflow = Overflow.HIDDEN.yoga();"},{"title":"PositionType example","note":"These values map directly to Yoga's YGPositionType constants.","code":"Tooltip tooltip = new Tooltip();\n\ntooltip.getLayout()\n       .positionType(PositionType.ABSOLUTE)\n       .left(100)\n       .top(50);\n\nint yogaValue = PositionType.ABSOLUTE.yoga();"},{"title":"Layout","note":"Percent values use 100 for the whole solver-selected reference extent. LayoutValue objects are immutable and shared safely; this Layout is mutable and intended for the UI thread. A widthFill or heightFill request sets flex growth on the parent's main axis rather than a percentage on the named axis.","code":"Panel panel = new Panel();\npanel.getLayout().column().width(400).height(300).gap(8);\npanel.getLayout().padding(12);\npanel.markLayoutDirty(); // Uniform padding does not notify the callback.\nButton button = new Button(\"Apply\");\nbutton.getLayout().fillWidth().height(36).noShrink();\npanel.add(button);"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Layout"},{"index":44,"title":"NanoVG UI controls","slug":"07-UI-NanoVG","summary":"NanoVG controls use vector-style painting while retaining the same UI tree, layout, focus, and behavior foundations. NanoNode defines the painting contract; NanoContainer and concrete controls provide panels, labels, buttons, images, fields, sliders, combo boxes, links, and scrollable content.","capabilities":[{"title":"Vector painting","description":"Controls draw through the root's prepared NanoVG context."},{"title":"Shared interaction","description":"Focus, activation, text editing, and scrolling integrate with the normal UI router."},{"title":"Measurement","description":"Text and automatic dimensions use the available NanoVG font context or documented fallbacks."},{"title":"Mixed trees","description":"The rendering context switches between texture and NanoVG work when needed."},{"title":"Navigation controls","description":"Combo boxes, hyperlinks, and modals manage transient interaction and display states."}],"examples":[{"title":"NanoComboBox","note":"Programmatic selection updates the label without invoking the change listener. User selection closes the popup and notifies only when the selected index changes. An attached, enabled control with nonempty items is required to open the popup.","code":"NanoComboBox<String> choice = new NanoComboBox<String>()\n        .items(List.of(\"Low\", \"Medium\", \"High\"))\n        .selectedIndex(1)\n        .onChange(value -> System.out.println(value));"},{"title":"NanoModal","note":"Escape dismissal is enabled by default; outside-click dismissal is opt-in and requires non-null content. Dialog style keys customize the inner panel, while inherited NanoPanel settings paint the surrounding overlay.","code":"NanoModal modal = new NanoModal(parentNode);\nmodal.getDialog().getLayout().width(360).height(200).padding(16);\nmodal.content(new NanoLabel(\"Settings\"));\nmodal.closeOnOutsideClick(true).open();\n// Dismiss through the modal API to unwind the root's focus scope:\nmodal.close();"},{"title":"NanoScrollPanel","note":"Scrollbar metrics reuse a mutable cache. Maximum offsets include the cached opposite scrollbar thickness, so range getters reflect the current metric cache rather than independently solving both bar visibilities. Use the normal layout and draw lifecycle after content/style changes. Configure the content through setContent rather than adding unrelated…","code":"NanoScrollPanel scroll = new NanoScrollPanel();\nscroll.getLayout().width(400).height(250);\nNanoPanel body = new NanoPanel();\nbody.getLayout().width(700).height(600);\nscroll.setContent(body);\n// After the root has laid out content:\nscroll.scroll(80, 120);"},{"title":"NanoTextField","note":"Attach to a root before operations requiring caret measurement: substring measurement and styled auto layout dereference the root. The root owns NanoVG fonts and frame state. Colors are borrowed and may be replaced by theme layout. Auto dimensions are replaced with measured numbers, so later text changes do not automatically restore content sizing.","code":"NanoTextField field = new NanoTextField(\"Name\");\nfield.getLayout().width(240).height(40);\nparent.add(field); // Attach before caret/text operations requiring measurement.\nfield.text(\"Albert\");\nfield.action(value -> System.out.println(value.getText()));"},{"title":"BACKGROUND_COLOR_KEY","note":"Theme background color used during layout; colors are retained by reference.","code":"public static final  StyleKey<Color> BACKGROUND_COLOR_KEY"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-NanoVG"},{"index":45,"title":"UI performance: measured costs and regression checks","slug":"07-UI-Performance","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions. Engine verify* and benchmark tasks in those records still require their optional local test/benchmark…","capabilities":[{"title":"Reproduce","description":"Run engine regression and benchmark tasks from the Valthorne checkout with its local test and benchmark fixtures:"},{"title":"Workloads","description":"The frame scene groups backends in runs of 20. Nano panels paint rounded fills and borders; texture panels have no background skin and exercise traversal/backend transitions, not textured fill throughput. The window is invisible, with no presentation or per-operation glFinish. Draw measurements include CPU submission and driver backpressure, not isolated GPU time. Native regression tests separately verify real…"},{"title":"Environment and provenance","description":"Recorded September 8, 2026 on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200, AMD Ryzen 9 7945HX (16 cores/32 logical processors), Oracle HotSpot JDK 25.0.2+10-LTS-69, JMH 1.37 and LWJGL 3.4.1. Enumerated adapters: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 (driver 32.0.16.1088), AMD Radeon 610M (32.0.21045.5002). The benchmark did not record GL_RENDERER, so adapter enumeration is not proof of which GPU was used."},{"title":"Recorded results","description":"Means below compare the relevant baseline file with final.json. Timing errors are JMH's reported 99.9% interval half-widths. Allocations are bytes per operation. ~0 means less than one measured byte/op, not a proof of no allocation anywhere."},{"title":"Table projection measurements (2026-09-09)","description":"./gradlew benchmarkUITable measures the new table model separately from the original 16-case allocation gate. Raw JMH data, JVM settings and confidence intervals are archived in table.json. Each case uses two forks, three one-second warmups and five one-second measurements per fork, with the GC profiler and a 256 MiB heap."},{"title":"Allocation gate","description":"verifyUIBenchmark rejects missing/duplicate cases, missing profiler data and exceeded per-operation Java allocation budgets:"},{"title":"Memory and CPU boundaries","description":"- gc.alloc.rate.norm measures Java allocation, not retained heap, native Yoga/ NanoVG allocations, driver memory or VRAM. No native leak-free claim follows from it. - Cursor/clean-text paths eliminate temporary collections/strings; actual insertion and undo still allocate. Undo retains at most 100 snapshots, not a byte budget. - Variable-height storage is approximately 12 bytes per data row plus array headers;…"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reproduce","note":"The last command runs all 16 parameterized benchmark cases (roughly five minutes on this host), writes build/reports/ui-benchmark/results.json, and checks allocation budgets. It is intentionally separate from build/check: timing/GL benchmarks should not silently burden every development build or require a display on CPU-only CI.","code":".\\gradlew.bat verifyUI\n.\\gradlew.bat verifyUIBenchmark"},{"title":"Reproduce (2)","note":"Run a benchmark subset from the Valthorne checkout without the complete-suite gate:","code":".\\gradlew.bat runUIShowcase --args=--smoke"},{"title":"Reproduce (3)","note":"JMH is isolated to the benchmark source set and is not a shipped library runtime dependency. The harness uses OpenJDK JMH, with returned values consumed by the harness, three one-second warmups, five one-second measurements, two forked JVMs, one benchmark thread, a fixed 256 MiB heap, and the GC allocation profiler. Output includes confidence intervals and…","code":".\\gradlew.bat benchmarkUI '--args=UIHotPathBenchmark -foe true -prof gc -rf json -rff build/reports/ui-benchmark/hot.json'\n.\\gradlew.bat benchmarkUI '--args=UIFrameBenchmark -foe true -prof gc -rf json -rff build/reports/ui-benchmark/frames.json'\n.\\gradlew.bat benchmarkUI '--args=UIDataBenchmark -foe true -prof gc -rf json -rff build/reports/ui-benchmark/data.json'"},{"title":"Virtual lists, tables, and selection — RowHeightIndex","note":"Related Virtual lists, tables, and selection example: This mutable index is intended for UI-thread use and provides no synchronization. Successful queries and point updates allocate no objects; resizing replaces backing arrays. Empty indexes are supported, with zero total height and zero as the end sentinel returned by position lookup.","code":"RowHeightIndex rows = new RowHeightIndex(1000, 28f);\nrows.set(3, 52f);\ndouble fifthRowTop = rows.offset(4);\nint firstVisible = rows.indexAt(120);\nif (firstVisible < rows.size()) {\n    float visibleRowStride = rows.height(firstVisible);\n}"},{"title":"Virtual lists, tables, and selection — SelectionModel","note":"Related Virtual lists, tables, and selection example: Selections refer to positions, not data identities. Changing item order requires application-level remapping or clearing; changing the item count only removes out-of-range membership and adjusts invalid anchor/lead values. This model does not manipulate focus, scroll position or rendered item state.","code":"SelectionModel selection = new SelectionModel(100, true);\nselection.select(10, false, false);\nselection.select(15, true, false);\nselection.select(30, false, true);\nselection.forEachSelected(index -> System.out.println(index));"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Performance"},{"index":46,"title":"Mixed UI rendering","slug":"07-UI-Rendering","summary":"See UI system guide for the showcase, shared behaviors, theme, input routing, text editing, inspector, virtualization, and migration notes.","capabilities":[{"title":"Drawing contract","description":"UIRenderContext selects each node's backend. Containers own child traversal. Adjacent same-backend siblings are grouped without reordering. Parent backgrounds, children, parent foregrounds, and later siblings retain painter order. Overlays render once, after the normal tree. Backend transitions flush pending work and restore the batch's GPU bindings while preserving its clip and translation stacks. The…"},{"title":"Coordinates and interaction","description":"- Layout and Nano painting use top-left positions; texture painting and hit testing use bottom-left world positions. Conversion occurs at the shared rendering boundary. - screenToWorld converts a pointer to world space. screenToContent also accounts for ancestor scrolling. screenToLayout returns the corresponding top-left position for Nano controls. Captured pointer movement can extend outside a viewport without…"},{"title":"Verification","description":"./gradlew test --tests valthorne.ui.MixedUIIntegrationTest runs the JUnit suite. It creates an invisible GLFW window and checks actual OpenGL pixels and input targets. A working graphics context is required."}],"examples":[{"title":"Mixed UI rendering example","note":"Regular and NanoVG controls share one UI tree. Either container family can contain the other. Use the existing add, setContent, modal-content, and overlay APIs, then call UIRoot.draw() once per frame.","code":"Panel panel = new Panel();\nNanoPanel vectorPanel = new NanoPanel();\nvectorPanel.getLayout().width(300).height(160);\nvectorPanel.add(new Button(\"Regular button\"));\npanel.add(vectorPanel);\npanel.add(new NanoButton(\"Nano button\"));\nroot.add(panel);"},{"title":"UI roots, nodes, and input routing — Yoga Layout Integration example","note":"Related UI roots, nodes, and input routing example: Layout updates propagate through the tree and are eventually resolved by UIRoot#layout().","code":"UIContainer panel = new Panel();\n\nLabel label = new Label();\nButton button = new Button();\n\npanel.add(label);\npanel.add(button);\n\nroot.add(panel);\n\n// Update loop\npanel.update(delta);\n\n// Rendering\npanel.draw(batch);"},{"title":"UI roots, nodes, and input routing — Usage flow example","note":"Related UI roots, nodes, and input routing example: - Read render-space values through getRenderX(), getRenderY(), getWidth(), and getHeight().","code":"public class MyNode extends UINode {\n\n    @Override\n    public void onCreate() {\n        getLayout().width(Value.pixels(120));\n        getLayout().height(Value.pixels(40));\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onDestroy() {\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void update(float delta) {\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void draw(TextureBatch batch) {\n        ResolvedStyle style = getStyle();\n        if (style != null) {\n            Drawable background = style.get(Panel.BACKGROUND_KEY);\n            if (background != null) {\n                background.draw(batch, getRenderX(), getRenderY(), getWidth(), getHeight());\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"},{"title":"UI roots, nodes, and input routing — Custom Container Integration","note":"Related UI roots, nodes, and input routing example: A container can use drawChildren(UIContainer, UINode) to retain compatible sibling painting state without changing traversal order. Scoped translations and clips can be balanced with try-with-resources:","code":"UIRenderContext context = getRoot().getRenderContext();\nTextureBatch activeBatch = context.getBatch();\nchild.render(activeBatch);"},{"title":"UI roots, nodes, and input routing — Custom Container Integration (2)","note":"Related UI roots, nodes, and input routing example: A container can use drawChildren(UIContainer, UINode) to retain compatible sibling painting state without changing traversal order. Scoped translations and clips can be balanced with try-with-resources:","code":"try (UIRenderContext.Scope offset = context.translate(12, 8)) {\n    context.drawChildren(container, null);\n}"}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Rendering"},{"index":47,"title":"UI system guide","slug":"07-UI-System","summary":"Runnable demos and assets are maintained in the public examples project. Run launcher commands from that repository. Example source and resources are separate from the engine checkout and library artifacts. See the example catalog. Historical measurements retain their original commands and source revisions.","capabilities":[{"title":"Shared behavior, distinct skins","description":"- ActivationBehavior supplies the same primary-release and Enter/Space policy to both buttons, both checkboxes, and hyperlinks. - RangeModel supplies finite range validation, endpoint clamping, snapping, pointer positioning, wheel increments, and keyboard stepping to both sliders. Programmatic setters remain silent; user changes invoke the widget action. - ScrollBehavior supplies fractional wheel handling and…"},{"title":"Input routing","description":"UIRoot routes input through a snapshot of the target's ancestor path:"},{"title":"Text input and validation","description":"Physical key commands and committed text are separate. GLFW character input publishes TextInputEvent; UI text fields no longer derive characters from physical key codes. Code that synthesizes typing should publish text explicitly:"},{"title":"Theme ownership","description":"UITokens defines semantic colors, spacing, radius, typography, and minimum control height. The default theme maps these to the existing skin keys and provides texture nine-patches and Nano colors with normal, hover, focus, pressed, and disabled states. Density is configurable from 0.5 to 3. Existing explicit widget dimensions and local style overrides remain authoritative."},{"title":"Coordinates and custom drawing","description":"node.screenToLocal(x, y) converts bottom-left screen coordinates to top-left node coordinates, accounting for the viewport and ancestor scrolling. Existing screenToWorld, screenToContent, and screenToLayout remain available."},{"title":"Layout and performance","description":"Layout setters invalidate their owner automatically and ignore identical values, including positioning shortcuts and reset methods. Default zero-point and full-percent values are shared immutable objects; numeric setters compare before allocating. Root updates coalesce layout work rather than recalculating in every child update. Automatic passes resubmit only dirty/style-invalidated nodes' inputs to Yoga. Yoga…"},{"title":"Inspector","description":"Enable root.getInspector().setEnabled(true) and optionally setOutlines(true). entries() provides actual translated bounds, effective clips, resolved style values, focused state, and capture state for the last frame. The showcase also shows the hovered/focused control's details. Inspection is opt-in and allocates snapshots only when enabled; counters remain available independently."},{"title":"Boundaries","description":"- Variable-height lists require explicitly supplied measurements and a single column; automatic wrapping-based measurement, variable-height grids and reusable row pools are not provided. - Keyboard navigation and readable focus states are implemented; native screen-reader bridges and gamepad navigation are not provided. - Text editing is Unicode-safe, but glyph coverage still depends on the supplied font. The…"}],"examples":[{"title":"Text input and validation","note":"Adding a change listener does not replace the widget's internal synchronization. Close the returned subscription when no longer needed. Validation reports validity and gates Enter submission, but does not prohibit temporarily invalid text while editing. Programmatic text(...) assignments reset history; the maximum length constrains user edits rather than…","code":"JGL.publish(new TextInputEvent(\"café\"));\n\nNanoTextField field = new NanoTextField(\"Display name\");\nfield.getEditor().maxLength(80); // user edits, measured in Unicode code points\nfield.getEditor().validator(value -> !value.isBlank());\nAutoCloseable subscription = field.getEditor().onChange(() -> {\n    boolean valid = field.getEditor().isValid();\n    // Update an error label or validation style here.\n});"},{"title":"Theme ownership","note":"UITokens defines semantic colors, spacing, radius, typography, and minimum control height. The default theme maps these to the existing skin keys and provides texture nine-patches and Nano colors with normal, hover, focus, pressed, and disabled states. Density is configurable from 0.5 to 3. Existing explicit widget dimensions and local style overrides…","code":"ProfessionalTheme skin = new ProfessionalTheme(false, 1f); // dark, standard density\nroot.setTheme(skin.create());\n// ... use the root ...\nroot.dispose();\nskin.close(); // free owned font atlas and nine-patch textures on the graphics thread"},{"title":"Coordinates and custom drawing","note":"The clip uses top-left layout coordinates; the translation uses top-left deltas. Both apply to both backends. Nested scopes restore in reverse order, including when drawing throws. Raw backend transformations are only suitable for a node's own drawing, not shared mixed-renderer child space.","code":"UIRenderContext context = getRoot().getRenderContext();\ntry (var clip = context.clip(getAbsoluteX(), getAbsoluteY(), getWidth(), getHeight());\n     var offset = context.translate(0, -scrollOffset)) {\n    child.render(context.getBatch());\n}"},{"title":"Layout and performance","note":"VirtualList handles lists and grids with fixed row heights, or measured variable heights in single-column mode. It keeps only visible rows plus overscan attached, and pins focused/captured rows until their interaction ends. Factories must return fresh unattached nodes. Keep persistent item state in the data model; offscreen row nodes are destroyed and…","code":"VirtualList grid = new VirtualList(10_000, index ->\n    index % 2 == 0 ? new Button(\"Texture \" + index) : new NanoButton(\"Nano \" + index));\ngrid.columns(4).rowHeight(44).gap(8).overscan(2);\ngrid.getLayout().width(800).height(500);\nroot.add(grid);\ngrid.scrollToIndex(9999);"},{"title":"Shared UI behavior and editing models — Range and Stepping","note":"Related Shared UI behavior and editing models example: Pointer coordinates are top-left-local track coordinates. Horizontal values increase to the right; vertical values increase upward. Instances are mutable and intended to be owned by the UI dispatch thread.","code":"RangeModel range = new RangeModel(10, 97, 20);\nrange.step(5);\nrange.percent(1); // Exact maximum 97, even though it is off the step grid.\nrange.increment(-1); // Applies one negative step and snaps within the range."}],"art":3,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-System"},{"index":48,"title":"Themes, styles, and design tokens","slug":"07-UI-Themes","summary":"Themes resolve typed style values from rules, node identity/state, and shared tokens. StyleKey gives a value a name and expected type; StyleMap stores values; ResolvedStyle is the result consumed by a control. Tokens let related controls share a consistent palette and sizing vocabulary.","capabilities":[{"title":"Typed keys","description":"Keys distinguish values such as colors, dimensions, and actions."},{"title":"Rules and states","description":"Rules select appearance for normal, hovered, pressed, focused, or disabled states."},{"title":"Shared tokens","description":"UITokens and ProfessionalTheme provide a common design vocabulary."},{"title":"Change propagation","description":"Theme listeners and invalidation refresh controls after theme data changes."},{"title":"Local overrides","description":"Control setters and explicit style values can supplement or override theme-derived values according to each control."}],"examples":[{"title":"ProfessionalTheme","note":"Builds a density-scaled light or dark skin for texture and NanoVG UI controls. The theme owns its bundled font and all nine-patch textures allocated by create(). Theme data shares these resources and palette colors, so keep this object alive while any styled root uses them. Create and close it on the graphics thread, detaching or disposing dependent roots…","code":"ProfessionalTheme theme = new ProfessionalTheme(false, 1.25f);\nThemeData styles = theme.create();\n// Apply styles to the UI and retain theme for the UI's lifetime.\n// After disposing all dependent roots:\ntheme.close();"},{"title":"ResolvedStyle example","note":"A resolved style is typically used by UI nodes during skinning, layout preparation, drawing, and runtime state updates. Because it represents the final merged result, callers generally treat it as the authoritative style view for the node at that moment.","code":"ThemeData theme = new ThemeData();\ntheme.setToken(MyStyleKeys.TEXT_COLOR, Color.WHITE);\n\nResolvedStyle style = theme.resolve(Button.class, StyleState.NONE, null);\n\nColor textColor = style.get(MyStyleKeys.TEXT_COLOR);\nboolean hasTextColor = style.contains(MyStyleKeys.TEXT_COLOR);\nStyleMap allValues = style.asMap();"},{"title":"StyleKey example","note":"This class is typically used as a static constant holder inside style or theme key definition classes. The usual pattern is to define keys once and reuse them across the engine.","code":"StyleKey<Float> PADDING = StyleKey.of(\"padding\", Float.class, 0f);\nStyleKey<String> FONT_NAME = StyleKey.of(\"fontName\", String.class, \"Default\");\n\nStyleMap map = new StyleMap();\nmap.set(PADDING, 12f);\n\nfloat padding = map.get(PADDING);\nString fontName = map.get(FONT_NAME);\n\nStyleKey<Float> samePaddingKey = StyleKey.of(\"padding\", Float.class, 0f);\nint id = samePaddingKey.getID();\nString name = samePaddingKey.getName();"},{"title":"StyleMap example","note":"A key detail of this class is that missing values do not simply return null. Instead, get(StyleKey) falls back to the key's default value when no stored value exists for that key.","code":"StyleKey<Float> PADDING = StyleKey.of(\"padding\", Float.class, 0f);\nStyleKey<String> FONT_NAME = StyleKey.of(\"fontName\", String.class, \"Default\");\n\nStyleMap map = new StyleMap();\nmap.set(PADDING, 12f);\n\nfloat padding = map.get(PADDING);\nString fontName = map.get(FONT_NAME); // Falls back to default value\n\nboolean hasPadding = map.contains(PADDING);\n\nStyleMap copy = map.copy();\ncopy.remove(PADDING);\n\nmap.putAll(copy);\nmap.clear();"},{"title":"StyleState example","note":"It also provides a convenience matches(short, short) method that mirrors the matching behavior used by ThemeRule. This makes it useful both for runtime state tracking and for direct comparisons against theme rule requirements.","code":"StyleState state = new StyleState();\n\nstate.add(StyleState.HOVERED)\n     .add(StyleState.FOCUSED)\n     .set(StyleState.PRESSED, true);\n\nboolean hovered = state.has(StyleState.HOVERED);\nboolean matches = state.matches(\n        (short) (StyleState.HOVERED | StyleState.FOCUSED),\n        StyleState.DISABLED\n);\n\nshort flags = state.getFlags();\n\nstate.remove(StyleState.PRESSED);\nstate.clear();\n\nStyleState restored = new StyleState(flags);"},{"title":"Theme example","note":"This interface is intentionally minimal. It does not prescribe how a theme should be stored internally, only that it must be able to produce a ThemeData object when requested.","code":"public final class DarkTheme implements Theme {\n\n    @Override\n    public ThemeData create() {\n        ThemeData data = new ThemeData();\n\n        data.setToken(MyStyleKeys.BACKGROUND, new Color(0.12f, 0.12f, 0.14f, 1f));\n        data.setToken(MyStyleKeys.FOREGROUND, Color.WHITE);\n\n        data.rule(Button.class)\n            .set(MyStyleKeys.PADDING, 8f)\n            .set(MyStyleKeys.CORNER_RADIUS, 6f);\n\n        return data;\n    }\n}\n\nTheme theme = new DarkTheme();\nThemeData themeData = theme.create();"},{"title":"ThemeData example","note":"Theme data is intended for UI-thread use and is not synchronized. All theme instances reuse one change event, so listeners should read its theme reference immediately and avoid retaining the event or causing nested theme publications. Listener registration is global to the event route, not filtered to this theme.","code":"ThemeData theme = new ThemeData();\n\ntheme.setToken(MyStyleKeys.TEXT_COLOR, Color.WHITE);\ntheme.setResource(\"buttonIcon\", someDrawable);\n\ntheme.rule(Button.class)\n     .set(MyStyleKeys.PADDING, 8f)\n     .set(MyStyleKeys.CORNER_RADIUS, 4f);\n\ntheme.rule(Button.class, \"primary\", StyleState.HOVERED)\n     .set(MyStyleKeys.BACKGROUND, hoverDrawable);\n\nStyleMap overrides = new StyleMap();\noverrides.set(MyStyleKeys.PADDING, 12f);\n\nResolvedStyle style = theme.resolve(Button.class, \"primary\", StyleState.HOVERED, overrides);\nColor textColor = style.get(MyStyleKeys.TEXT_COLOR);\n\ntheme.addThemeDataChangeListener(event -> {\n    if (event.getData() == theme) System.out.println(\"Theme changed\");\n});"},{"title":"ThemeRule example","note":"This class also provides a fluent set(StyleKey, Object) method so rules can be configured in a compact chained style when building theme definitions. Target metadata is fixed after construction, but the value map is live and mutable. Rules created through ThemeData publish theme changes through that map's callback; directly constructed rules have no change…","code":"ThemeRule base = new ThemeRule(Button.class, null)\n        .set(MyStyleKeys.PADDING, 8f)\n        .set(MyStyleKeys.CORNER_RADIUS, 6f);\n\nThemeRule hoveredPrimary = new ThemeRule(\n        Button.class,\n        \"primary\",\n        StyleState.HOVERED,\n        StyleState.DISABLED\n).set(MyStyleKeys.BACKGROUND, hoverDrawable)\n .set(MyStyleKeys.TEXT_COLOR, Color.WHITE);\n\nboolean matches = hoveredPrimary.matches(StyleState.HOVERED);\nint priority = hoveredPrimary.getPriority();\nStyleMap values = hoveredPrimary.getValues();"},{"title":"UITokens","note":"ProfessionalTheme populates these roles alongside concrete widget style properties. A role affects rendering only where a consumer reads it; setting a semantic color does not automatically rewrite every widget-specific color key. CONTROL_HEIGHT is also read by UINode when applying control styling.","code":"ThemeData theme = new ThemeData();\ntheme.setToken(UITokens.CONTROL_HEIGHT, 36f);\ntheme.setToken(UITokens.SPACING, 8f);\ntheme.setToken(UITokens.ACCENT, new Color(0.2f, 0.5f, 1f, 1f));"}],"art":22,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/07-UI-Themes"},{"index":49,"title":"Resizable and unordered arrays","slug":"08-Data-Arrays","summary":"Two array families serve different storage models. Array and its primitive counterparts provide indexed storage that grows when set writes beyond its length; they do not track a separate logical element count. SwapOnRemove variants are collections with add/remove and a logical size, using the last element to fill a removed slot.","capabilities":[{"title":"Indexed growth","description":"set(index, value) can allocate through index plus one, preserving earlier values and default-filling gaps."},{"title":"Length","description":"For ordinary Array variants, length is allocated storage, including untouched slots."},{"title":"Collection size","description":"SwapOnRemove variants track the number of added elements separately from capacity."},{"title":"Unordered removal","description":"Removing an element moves the last active element into its slot."},{"title":"Primitive variants","description":"Store numeric and character values without boxing."}],"examples":[{"title":"Usage example","note":"Place these statements in your initialization or application method; imports belong at the top of the Java file.","code":"import valthorne.collections.array.IntArray;\nimport valthorne.collections.array.SwapOnRemoveIntArray;\n\nIntArray slots = new IntArray(2);\nslots.set(4, 25);\nint allocatedLength = slots.length(); // 5, including untouched zero slots.\n\nSwapOnRemoveIntArray active = new SwapOnRemoveIntArray();\nactive.add(10);\nactive.add(20);\nactive.remove(0); // Moves the last active value into slot zero."},{"title":"Array — Constructor","note":"Allocates Object-backed storage of the requested length, initially filled with null. Prefer positive size to permit a first write at index zero.","code":"public Array(int size)"},{"title":"Array — Constructor (2)","note":"Allocates storage with the supplied runtime component type. A subsequent growth replaces it with Object-backed storage, so this constructor does not guarantee a persistent concrete array type.","code":"public Array(Class<T> type, int size)"},{"title":"Array — set","note":"Stores a value, replacing backing storage when index reaches or exceeds length. Growth uses an Object array of length index times two, losing any concrete component type selected at construction. Index zero cannot grow an empty array. Previously returned backing arrays no longer track the replacement.","code":"public void set(int index, T value)"},{"title":"Array — get","note":"Reads an allocated slot without resizing or changing membership. Untouched slots return null; length describes all readable indices.","code":"public T get(int index)"}],"art":13,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Arrays"},{"index":50,"title":"Bit fields and flags","slug":"08-Data-Bits","summary":"Bit-field wrappers store Boolean flags in an integral value. They are useful for compact state such as enabled/visible/pressed flags or a small set of options. Operations address bit positions or replace the complete backing pattern.","capabilities":[{"title":"Individual flags","description":"Set, clear, toggle, and test selected positions."},{"title":"Whole patterns","description":"Read or replace the full integral representation."},{"title":"Width variants","description":"Choose byte, short, int, or long according to the number of flags."},{"title":"Generic contract","description":"Bits provides the shared abstraction used by concrete wrappers."}],"examples":[{"title":"Bits example","note":"This implementation is intentionally lightweight and performance-oriented. It does not validate every possible incorrect input and does not attempt to provide the same safety guarantees as java.util.BitSet. It is better suited for internal engine code, data structures, masks, occupancy flags, serialization helpers, and other systems where raw speed and…","code":"Bits bits = new Bits(128);\n\nbits.set(0);\nbits.set(5);\nbits.set(70);\n\nSystem.out.println(bits.get(0));   // true\nSystem.out.println(bits.get(1));   // false\n\nbits.clear(5);\n\nint nextSet = bits.nextSetBit(0);      // 0\nint nextClear = bits.nextClearBit(0);  // usually 1\n\nbyte[] raw = bits.toByteArray();\nBits restored = Bits.valueOf(raw);\n\nSystem.out.println(restored);\nSystem.out.println(restored.size());"},{"title":"Behavior notes example","note":"All bit operations in this class are restricted to the valid byte index range of 0..7. Any attempt to access a bit outside that range throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException. This makes the API safer and prevents accidental misuse that would otherwise silently operate on values outside the intended byte-width contract.","code":"ByteBits flags = new ByteBits();\n\nflags.set(0);\nflags.set(3);\nflags.set(7, true);\n\nboolean hasBit3 = flags.get(3);\nint firstSet = flags.nextSetBit(0);\nint firstClear = flags.nextClearBit(0);\nint count = flags.size();\n\nSystem.out.println(flags);        // Example: 10001001\nSystem.out.println(hasBit3);      // true\nSystem.out.println(firstSet);     // 0\nSystem.out.println(firstClear);   // 1\nSystem.out.println(count);        // 3\n\nflags.flip(3);\nflags.clear(7);\n\nByteBits required = new ByteBits((byte) 0b00000001);\nboolean containsRequired = flags.allMatch(required);"},{"title":"IntBits example","note":"Bit index 0 maps to the least significant bit and bit index 31 maps to the most significant bit. All index-based methods validate the supplied index to ensure operations stay inside the valid integer bit range.","code":"IntBits flags = new IntBits();\n\nflags.set(1);\nflags.set(4);\nflags.flip(4);\nflags.set(10, true);\n\nboolean hasBit1 = flags.get(1);\nint count = flags.size();\nint nextSet = flags.nextSetBit(0);\n\nIntBits required = new IntBits();\nrequired.set(1);\n\nboolean exact = flags.matches(required);\nboolean overlap = flags.anyMatch(required);\nboolean contains = flags.allMatch(required);"},{"title":"Behavior overview example","note":"- matches(LongBits), allMatch(LongBits), and anyMatch(LongBits) support exact, contained, and overlapping comparisons","code":"LongBits flags = new LongBits();\n\nflags.set(0);\nflags.set(5);\nflags.set(63);\n\nboolean enabled = flags.get(5);\nint count = flags.size();\nint nextSet = flags.nextSetBit(0);\nint nextClear = flags.nextClearBit(0);\n\nLongBits required = new LongBits();\nrequired.set(0);\nrequired.set(5);\n\nboolean containsAll = flags.allMatch(required);\nboolean overlaps = flags.anyMatch(required);\n\nSystem.out.println(flags);        // 64-bit binary string\nSystem.out.println(enabled);      // true\nSystem.out.println(count);        // 3\nSystem.out.println(nextSet);      // 0\nSystem.out.println(nextClear);    // 1\nSystem.out.println(containsAll);  // true\nSystem.out.println(overlaps);     // true"},{"title":"ShortBits example","note":"Bit indices range from 0 through 15. All index-based methods validate the requested index so invalid bit positions fail immediately.","code":"ShortBits flags = new ShortBits();\n\nflags.set(0);\nflags.set(7);\nflags.set(15);\n\nboolean topBit = flags.get(15);\nint count = flags.size();\n\nShortBits required = new ShortBits();\nrequired.set(0);\nrequired.set(7);\n\nboolean containsAll = flags.allMatch(required);\nboolean overlaps = flags.anyMatch(required);\n\nSystem.out.println(flags);\nSystem.out.println(topBit);\nSystem.out.println(count);\nSystem.out.println(containsAll);\nSystem.out.println(overlaps);"}],"art":13,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Bits"},{"index":51,"title":"Binary buffers and byte order","slug":"08-Data-Buffers","summary":"DynamicByteBuffer provides growable byte storage with independent read/write positions and support for typed values and bit operations. ByteOrder selects multibyte ordering. This is useful for custom binary formats where fields must be encoded predictably.","capabilities":[{"title":"Read/write cursors","description":"Reading and writing advance different positions within shared storage."},{"title":"Primitive values","description":"Typed operations encode numbers using the configured byte order."},{"title":"Bit packing","description":"Bit buffers accumulate partial-byte reads and writes."},{"title":"Capacity and extraction","description":"Growth and output-copy operations separate valid data from unused capacity."}],"examples":[{"title":"DynamicByteBuffer","note":"Note: When performing bit-level operations, ensure to call flushBits() after writing bits to ensure all buffered bits are written to the underlying byte array. Reading or writing bits after changing the read or write position resets the bit buffers to maintain consistency. The byte order affects only multi-byte types (char, short, int, long, float, double,…","code":"byte[] buffer = new byte[100];\nDynamicByteBuffer dbb = new DynamicByteBuffer(buffer);\ndbb.setByteOrder(DynamicByteBuffer.ByteOrder.LITTLE_ENDIAN)\n   .writeInt(12345)\n   .writeString(\"Hello\")\n   .writeBit(true)\n   .flushBits();\ndbb.setReadPosition(0);\nSystem.out.println(dbb.readInt());    // Outputs: 12345\nSystem.out.println(dbb.readString()); // Outputs: Hello\nSystem.out.println(dbb.readBit());    // Outputs: true"},{"title":"BIG_ENDIAN","note":"Big-endian order (the most significant byte first).","code":"public static final  ByteOrder BIG_ENDIAN"},{"title":"LITTLE_ENDIAN","note":"Little-endian order (the least significant byte first).","code":"public static final  ByteOrder LITTLE_ENDIAN"},{"title":"DynamicByteBuffer — Constructor","note":"Constructs a new instance of DynamicByteBuffer with an initial capacity of 16 bytes. This constructor initializes the internal buffer to a default size, allowing for dynamic resizing as new data is added.","code":"public DynamicByteBuffer()"},{"title":"DynamicByteBuffer — Constructor (2)","note":"Constructs a new DynamicByteBuffer with the specified initial capacity.","code":"public DynamicByteBuffer(int capacity)"}],"art":8,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Buffers"},{"index":52,"title":"Cache stores and checksums","slug":"08-Data-Cache","summary":"CacheStore groups named archives, and each CacheArchive contains ordered CacheFiles. The checksum model records archive/file information for integrity and version comparisons. Use this system when packaging or exchanging structured binary asset groups rather than loading each resource as an unrelated file.","capabilities":[{"title":"Store/archive/file hierarchy","description":"The hierarchy groups payloads and provides stable organizational boundaries."},{"title":"Compression identifiers","description":"Each archive records how its payload is stored."},{"title":"Serialization","description":"Store operations encode and decode the supported cache format."},{"title":"Checksums","description":"ChecksumTable and row records describe CRC-based payload metadata and versions."}],"examples":[{"title":"CacheArchive example","note":"A single named archive inside a CacheStore.","code":"CacheArchive archive = new CacheArchive(\"ui\");\narchive.setCompression((byte) 1); // Example: gzip in your format.\n\narchive.addFile(new CacheFile(\"button.png\", buttonBytes));\narchive.addFile(new CacheFile(\"panel.png\", panelBytes));\n\n// Store it.\nCacheStore store = new CacheStore();\nstore.setVersion(1);\nstore.setArchives(archive);\n\n// Encode for disk.\nbyte[] payload = CacheArchive.compress(archive);\n\n// Decode from disk.\nCacheArchive decoded = CacheArchive.decompress(payload, archive.getCompression());\nCacheFile f = decoded.getFile(\"button.png\");"},{"title":"CacheFile example","note":"A single named file entry inside a CacheArchive.","code":"CacheFile file = new CacheFile(\"player.png\", playerPngBytes);\nString name = file.getName();\nbyte[] data = file.getData();\n\nfile.setName(\"player_idle.png\");\nfile.setData(otherBytes);"},{"title":"CacheStore example","note":"Top-level container for a Valthorne cache on disk.","code":"// Build a store in memory.\nCacheStore store = new CacheStore();\nstore.setVersion(1);\n\nCacheArchive sprites = new CacheArchive(\"sprites\",\n        new CacheFile(\"player.png\", playerPngBytes),\n        new CacheFile(\"enemy.png\", enemyPngBytes)\n);\nsprites.setCompression((byte) 1); // Example: GZIP in your archive format (see CacheArchive constants).\n\nstore.setArchives(sprites);\n\n// Save to disk.\nstore.save(Path.of(\"assets/cache.vthn\"));\n\n// Load it back.\nCacheStore loaded = CacheStore.load(Path.of(\"assets/cache.vthn\"));\nCacheArchive a = loaded.getArchive(\"sprites\");\nCacheFile f = a.getFile(\"player.png\");\nbyte[] bytes = f.getData();\n\n// Modify.\nloaded.removeArchive(\"sprites\");\nloaded.addArchive(new CacheArchive(\"ui\"));\nloaded.save(Path.of(\"assets/cache.vthn\"));"},{"title":"ArchiveRow example","note":"Represents a checksum row for a single CacheArchive.","code":"// Build a row (usually created by ChecksumTable.build()).\nFileRow[] files = FileRow.EMPTY;\nArchiveRow row = new ArchiveRow(\"textures\", (byte) 1, 0x12345678, 4096, files);\n\n// Read fields directly (records expose accessor methods).\nSystem.out.println(row.name());\nSystem.out.println(row.archiveCrc32());"},{"title":"ChecksumTable example","note":"Builds and verifies a CRC32-based checksum table for a CacheStore.","code":"// Build a cache store (or load one).\nCacheStore store = CacheStore.load(Path.of(\"cache.dat\"));\n\n// Build a checksum table for the current store (include file rows for per-file validation).\nChecksumTable table = ChecksumTable.build(store, true);\n\n// Encode to bytes (store alongside your cache file).\nbyte[] encoded = ChecksumTable.encode(table);\nFiles.write(Path.of(\"cache.chk\"), encoded);\n\n// Later: load and verify the checksum table bytes.\nbyte[] chkBytes = Files.readAllBytes(Path.of(\"cache.chk\"));\nChecksumTable loaded = ChecksumTable.decode(chkBytes); // throws if CRC mismatches\n\n// Compare two tables to find which archives changed.\nChecksumTable newer = ChecksumTable.build(store, false); // maybe you don't need file rows\nString[] changed = newer.diffChangedArchives(loaded);\n\n// Example: if changed contains \"textures\", re-download or rebuild that archive.\nfor (String name : changed) {\n    System.out.println(\"Changed archive: \" + name);\n}"},{"title":"FileRow example","note":"Represents a checksum row for a single CacheFile.","code":"FileRow row = new FileRow(\"player.png\", 0xDEADBEEF, 1024);\nSystem.out.println(row.name());\nSystem.out.println(row.fileLength());"}],"art":13,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Cache"},{"index":53,"title":"Compression strategies","slug":"08-Data-Compression","summary":"CompressionStrategy exposes byte-array compression and decompression. Concrete implementations wrap gzip, deflate, ZIP, BZIP2, LZMA, and XZ behavior. Choose the format required by the consuming file or protocol; similarly named algorithms and containers are not interchangeable byte streams.","capabilities":[{"title":"Format-specific encoding","description":"Each strategy produces its own encoded representation."},{"title":"Decompression","description":"The matching decoder expands bytes into independent output."},{"title":"In-memory operation","description":"These APIs accumulate complete results rather than exposing an incremental stream interface."},{"title":"Failure translation","description":"I/O or format failures are translated according to the concrete strategy."}],"examples":[{"title":"Usage example","note":"Place these statements in your initialization or application method; imports belong at the top of the Java file.","code":"import valthorne.compression.GZIP;\nimport java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;\n\nGZIP gzip = new GZIP();\nbyte[] original = \"Valthorne\".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\nbyte[] encoded = gzip.compress(original);\nbyte[] decoded = gzip.decompress(encoded);\nString text = new String(decoded, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);"},{"title":"compress","note":"Encodes the complete input as a BZIP2 stream using the compressor's default settings. The output stream is finalized before its bytes are returned. Input contents remain unchanged, and each call uses independent temporary buffers.","code":"public byte[] compress(byte[] data)"},{"title":"decompress","note":"Expands a BZIP2 payload into a new in-memory byte array. Bytes are copied until the decoder reports end of input, with no configured output-size limit. The supplied encoded array is not modified.","code":"public byte[] decompress(byte[] data)"},{"title":"LZMA","note":"A predefined instance of the LZMAStrategy class, which implements the CompressionStrategy interface for performing data compression and decompression using the LZMA (Lempel\\u2013Ziv\\u2013Markov chain algorithm) algorithm.","code":"LZMAStrategy LZMA"},{"title":"XZ","note":"A predefined instance of the XZStrategy class, which implements the CompressionStrategy interface for performing data compression and decompression using the XZ algorithm.","code":"XZStrategy XZ"}],"art":20,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Compression"},{"index":54,"title":"Encryption and hashing adapters","slug":"08-Data-Encryption","summary":"The encryption package adapts several Java cryptography operations to the engine's byte-array interfaces. Symmetric encryption, asymmetric operations, hashing, and ISAAC random generation serve different purposes even when a common interface presents encrypt/decrypt-style names. This guide documents the adapters, not a complete key-management or authentication protocol.","capabilities":[{"title":"Symmetric adapters","description":"AES, AESGCM, Blowfish, and ChaCha20Poly1305 operate with symmetric key material and format-specific parameters."},{"title":"Asymmetric adapters","description":"RSA and ECC use public/private key roles and provider-supported transformations."},{"title":"Hashing","description":"SHA256, SHA512, and Whirlpool produce digests; a digest is not reversible encryption."},{"title":"Pseudorandom generation","description":"ISAAC maintains generator state and is separate from the encryption adapter lifecycle."}],"examples":[{"title":"AES","note":"This class is not thread-safe. If multiple threads access an instance concurrently, and at least one of the threads modifies the instance, it must be synchronized externally.","code":"SecretKey secretKey = ... // Obtain or generate a secret key\nAES aesEncryption = new AES(secretKey);\n\nbyte[] plaintext = \"Hello, World!\".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\nbyte[] encryptedData = aesEncryption.encrypt(plaintext);\nbyte[] decryptedData = aesEncryption.decrypt(encryptedData);\n\nString decryptedText = new String(decryptedData, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\nSystem.out.println(decryptedText);  // Outputs: Hello, World!"},{"title":"AESGCM","note":"This class is not thread-safe. External synchronization is required for concurrent access.","code":"SecretKey secretKey = // Generate or obtain a key\nbyte[] iv = // Generate a random 12-byte IV\nEncryptionStrategy aesGcm = new AESGCM(secretKey, iv);\nbyte[] plaintext = \"Confidential Data\".getBytes(\"UTF-8\");\nbyte[] encryptedData = aesGcm.encrypt(plaintext);\nbyte[] decryptedData = aesGcm.decrypt(encryptedData);\nSystem.out.println(new String(decryptedData, \"UTF-8\")); // Outputs: Confidential Data"},{"title":"Blowfish","note":"This class is not thread-safe. External synchronization is required for concurrent access.","code":"SecretKey secretKey = // Generate or obtain a Blowfish key\nEncryptionStrategy blowfish = new Blowfish(secretKey);\nbyte[] plaintext = \"Sensitive Info\".getBytes(\"UTF-8\");\nbyte[] encryptedData = blowfish.encrypt(plaintext);\nbyte[] decryptedData = blowfish.decrypt(encryptedData);\nSystem.out.println(new String(decryptedData, \"UTF-8\")); // Outputs: Sensitive Info"},{"title":"ChaCha20Poly1305","note":"Note: This implementation requires Java 11+ with the JCE provider supporting ChaCha20-Poly1305 (e.g., Bouncy Castle if not natively available).","code":"SecretKey secretKey = // Generate or obtain a 256-bit key\nbyte[] nonce = // Generate a random 96-bit nonce\nEncryptionStrategy chacha20 = new ChaCha20Poly1305(secretKey, nonce);\nbyte[] plaintext = \"Secure Data\".getBytes(\"UTF-8\");\nbyte[] encryptedData = chacha20.encrypt(plaintext);\nbyte[] decryptedData = chacha20.decrypt(encryptedData);\nSystem.out.println(new String(decryptedData, \"UTF-8\")); // Outputs: Secure Data"},{"title":"ECC","note":"Note: ECIES is not natively supported in JCA's default configuration. This implementation assumes a simplified approach using \"ECIES\" from a provider like Bouncy Castle.","code":"KeyPair keyPair = ECC.generateKeyPair();\nECC eccEncryption = new ECC(keyPair.getPublic(), keyPair.getPrivate());\nbyte[] plaintext = \"Secret Message\".getBytes(\"UTF-8\");\nbyte[] encryptedData = eccEncryption.encrypt(plaintext);\nbyte[] decryptedData = eccEncryption.decrypt(encryptedData);\nSystem.out.println(new String(decryptedData, \"UTF-8\")); // Outputs: Secret Message"},{"title":"EncryptionStrategy","note":"The above example shows how an implementation of the EncryptionStrategy interface can be used to encrypt and decrypt data. In this case, the AES class, which implements the interface, is instantiated and used for symmetric encryption.","code":"EncryptionStrategy encryptionStrategy = new AES(secretKey);\nbyte[] encryptedData = encryptionStrategy.encrypt(plainTextData);\nbyte[] decryptedData = encryptionStrategy.decrypt(encryptedData);"},{"title":"ISAAC example","note":"ISAAC (Indirection, Shift, Accumulate, Add, and Count) pseudo-random number generator.","code":"// Unseeded (still generates output, but deterministic based on default state).\nISAAC rng = new ISAAC();\nint a = rng.nextValue();\nint b = rng.nextValue();\n\n// Seeded (recommended): the seed is copied into the internal results buffer before init.\nint[] seed = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 };\nISAAC seeded = new ISAAC(seed);\nint r0 = seeded.nextValue();\nint r1 = seeded.nextValue();\n\n// Re-key / re-seed: create a new instance with a different seed.\n// (This implementation does not expose a \"setSeed\" method.)"},{"title":"RSA","note":"This class is not thread-safe. If multiple threads access an instance concurrently, and at least one of the threads modifies the instance, it must be synchronized externally.","code":"KeyPair keyPair = RSA.generateKeyPair(2048);\nRSA rsaEncryption = new RSA(keyPair.getPublic(), keyPair.getPrivate());\n\nbyte[] plaintext = \"Sensitive Data\".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\nbyte[] encryptedData = rsaEncryption.encrypt(plaintext);\nbyte[] decryptedData = rsaEncryption.decrypt(encryptedData);\n\nString decryptedText = new String(decryptedData, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\nSystem.out.println(decryptedText);  // Outputs: Sensitive Data"},{"title":"SHA256","note":"The above example shows how to use the SHA256Strategy to hash data. Since hashing is a one-way operation, the decrypt method is not applicable.","code":"EncryptionStrategy sha256Strategy = new SHA256Strategy();\nbyte[] hashedData = sha256Strategy.encrypt(plainTextData);"},{"title":"SHA512","note":"The above example shows how to use the SHA512 class to hash data. Since hashing is a one-way operation, the decrypt method is not applicable.","code":"EncryptionStrategy sha512Strategy = new SHA512();\nbyte[] hashedData = sha512Strategy.encrypt(plainTextData);"},{"title":"Whirlpool example","note":"Whirlpool hash function implementation (NESSIE reference style API).","code":"// One-shot hashing (byte[] slice).\nbyte[] digest = Whirlpool.whirlpool(data, 0, data.length);\n\n// Streaming-style hashing (NESSIE API).\nWhirlpool w = new Whirlpool();\nw.NESSIEinit();\nw.NESSIEadd(\"hello world\");          // ASCII helper\nw.NESSIEadd(moreBytes, moreBits);    // bit-precise update\nbyte[] out = new byte[64];           // Whirlpool digest is 512 bits\nw.NESSIEfinalize(out);"}],"art":20,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Encryption"},{"index":55,"title":"Classpath and filesystem utilities","slug":"08-Data-Files","summary":"ValthorneFiles reads classpath resources; FileUtility provides broader file-related convenience operations. Choose the namespace based on where the data lives. A resource inside a JAR is not necessarily a normal filesystem file, so native libraries that require filenames may need extraction.","capabilities":[{"title":"Classpath reads","description":"Read bytes or decoded text through the defining class loader."},{"title":"Resource streams","description":"Open a stream when the caller needs incremental access."},{"title":"Temporary extraction","description":"Copy a packaged resource to a temporary filesystem path for path-only APIs."},{"title":"Filesystem helpers","description":"FileUtility handles its documented file operations and conversions."},{"title":"Exceptions","description":"Dedicated file exceptions preserve lookup or I/O failure context."}],"examples":[{"title":"Design notes example","note":"- ZIP extraction includes a path-normalization safety check to prevent ZIP Slip attacks.","code":"Path assets = Paths.get(\"assets\");\nPath backupZip = Paths.get(\"backup/assets.zip\");\nPath extracted = Paths.get(\"temp/extracted-assets\");\n\n// Ensure destination directory exists.\nFileUtility.ensureDirectoryExists(backupZip.getParent());\n\n// Create an archive.\nFileUtility.zipDirectory(assets, backupZip);\n\n// Extract it safely.\nFileUtility.unzip(backupZip, extracted);\n\n// Find all PNG files.\nList<Path> pngFiles = FileUtility.findFilesByExtension(extracted, \"png\");\n\n// Compute a checksum for one of them.\nif (!pngFiles.isEmpty()) {\n    String sha256 = FileUtility.computeChecksum(pngFiles.get(0), \"SHA-256\");\n    System.out.println(\"Checksum: \" + sha256);\n}\n\n// Copy the extracted directory somewhere else.\nFileUtility.copyDirectory(extracted, Paths.get(\"build/copied-assets\"), true);"},{"title":"ValthorneFileException — Constructor","note":"Creates a failure with a descriptive message and no explicit cause.","code":"public ValthorneFileException(String message)"},{"title":"ValthorneFileException — Constructor (2)","note":"Creates a failure that preserves the underlying exception.","code":"public ValthorneFileException(String message, Throwable cause)"},{"title":"ValthorneFileNotFoundException — Constructor","note":"Creates a missing-resource failure with the supplied diagnostic text. The constructor only records the message; it does not attempt another lookup.","code":"public ValthorneFileNotFoundException(String message)"},{"title":"readBytes","note":"Reads the complete classpath resource into memory and closes its stream. The returned array is independent of the stream and can be modified by the caller. No size limit is imposed on resource contents.","code":"public static byte[] readBytes(String resourcePath)"}],"art":21,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Files"},{"index":56,"title":"Math and 2D geometry","slug":"08-Data-Math","summary":"MathUtils supplies interpolation, random sampling, statistics, easing, angle, distance, and bit helpers. The geometry package provides mutable 2D shapes and boundary points. Valthorne uses JOML for vectors, matrices, quaternions, rays, and 3D bounds; these helpers complement that dependency.","capabilities":[{"title":"Numeric helpers","description":"Clamp, normalize, map, average, median, and rounding implement specific arithmetic rules."},{"title":"Animation math","description":"Easing and angle interpolation convert normalized progress into motion."},{"title":"Geometry","description":"Circle, rectangle, rounded rectangle, triangle, and polygon expose boundaries and movement."},{"title":"Approximation","description":"Fast square-root and distance helpers trade accuracy for a compact approximation."},{"title":"Mutable boundaries","description":"Point arrays and cached centers can be live or reused rather than snapshots."}],"examples":[{"title":"MathUtils","note":"Random helpers share one pseudorandom generator. Statistical helpers preserve input arrays; median calculations sort copies. Fast square-root helpers trade accuracy and special-value behavior for a compact approximation.","code":"float progress = MathUtils.clamp(elapsed / duration, 0f, 1f);\nfloat x = MathUtils.lerp(startX, endX, progress);\ndouble mean = MathUtils.average(10, 20, 30);\nint wrappedFrame = MathUtils.wrap(frame, 0, frameCount - 1);"},{"title":"points","note":"Returns the points that define the shape or boundary of this area.","code":"Vector2f[] points()"},{"title":"contains","note":"Determines if the point specified by the coordinates (x, y) lies within the boundary defined by the area. This method uses the even-odd rule algorithm to check whether the point is inside the polygon formed by the area points.","code":"default boolean contains(float x, float y)"},{"title":"overlaps","note":"Determines if this area overlaps with the given area. Two areas are considered to overlap if any of their edges intersect or if one area contains any point from the other area.","code":"default boolean overlaps(Area other)"},{"title":"Border — Constructor","note":"Creates an opaque white border with thickness one, allocating an independent mutable color.","code":"public Border()"}],"art":23,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Math"},{"index":57,"title":"Object pooling","slug":"08-Data-Pooling","summary":"Pool reuses objects produced by a factory, while Poolable defines reset behavior where applicable. This reduces repeated allocation for temporary objects with clear acquire/release boundaries. Pool capacity describes retained free objects, not necessarily all objects ever created.","capabilities":[{"title":"Factory","description":"Creates an object when the pool cannot supply an existing one."},{"title":"Warm-up","description":"Initialization prepopulates reusable objects."},{"title":"Obtain/free","description":"Acquire temporary ownership and return the object after use."},{"title":"Reset and retention","description":"Reset behavior prepares reuse; capacity limits how much free storage is retained."}],"examples":[{"title":"Pool example","note":"- returning objects with free(Object)","code":"Pool<MyParticle> particlePool = new Pool<>(MyParticle::new, 256);\nparticlePool.initialize(32);\n\nMyParticle particle = particlePool.obtain();\nparticle.setPosition(100, 200);\nparticle.setVelocity(1.5f, -0.5f);\n\n// Use the particle...\n\nparticlePool.free(particle);\n\nint pooledCount = particlePool.size();\n\nparticlePool.clear();"},{"title":"Pool — Constructor","note":"Creates a new object pool with the provided factory and maximum retention size.","code":"public Pool(Supplier<T> factory, int maxSize)"},{"title":"initialize","note":"Pre-populates the pool by creating and storing a specified number of objects.","code":"public void initialize(int count)"},{"title":"obtain","note":"Obtains an object from the pool.","code":"public T obtain()"},{"title":"free","note":"Returns an object to the pool for future reuse.","code":"public void free(T obj)"}],"art":13,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Pooling"},{"index":58,"title":"Property settings","slug":"08-Data-Settings","summary":"The valthorne.utility.settings package provides a typed property map with binary and human-readable persistence.","capabilities":[{"title":"Core model","description":"PropertySet stores named values as PropertyValue wrappers. Use get, find, or getOrDefault for optional values and getRequired when absence is an error. Typed overloads verify the stored value's class. set returns the previous value, and asMap() exposes the set's map view as documented by the source API."},{"title":"Persistence","description":"- PropertySetIO reads and writes the binary format through paths, streams, DynamicByteBuffer, or byte arrays. - PropertySetTextIO reads and writes the text format through paths, streams, readers/writers, or strings. - PropertyTextOptions supplies optional header lines and per-key comments; none() creates the default and withHeaders(...) creates a header-only configuration."}],"examples":[{"title":"Store and read a typed property","note":"PropertySet stores named PropertyValue wrappers. Typed access verifies the stored class, while PropertyValue supplies explicit conversions.","code":"var settings = new valthorne.utility.settings.PropertySet();\nsettings.set(\"volume\", 0.8f);\nfloat volume = settings\n    .getRequired(\"volume\", Float.class)\n    .asFloat();"},{"title":"Binary buffers and byte order — DynamicByteBuffer","note":"Related Binary buffers and byte order example: Note: When performing bit-level operations, ensure to call flushBits() after writing bits to ensure all buffered bits are written to the underlying byte array. Reading or writing bits after changing the read or write position resets the bit buffers to maintain consistency. The byte order affects only multi-byte types (char, short, int, long, float, double,…","code":"byte[] buffer = new byte[100];\nDynamicByteBuffer dbb = new DynamicByteBuffer(buffer);\ndbb.setByteOrder(DynamicByteBuffer.ByteOrder.LITTLE_ENDIAN)\n   .writeInt(12345)\n   .writeString(\"Hello\")\n   .writeBit(true)\n   .flushBits();\ndbb.setReadPosition(0);\nSystem.out.println(dbb.readInt());    // Outputs: 12345\nSystem.out.println(dbb.readString()); // Outputs: Hello\nSystem.out.println(dbb.readBit());    // Outputs: true"},{"title":"Binary buffers and byte order — BIG_ENDIAN","note":"Related Binary buffers and byte order example: Big-endian order (the most significant byte first).","code":"public static final  ByteOrder BIG_ENDIAN"},{"title":"Binary buffers and byte order — LITTLE_ENDIAN","note":"Related Binary buffers and byte order example: Little-endian order (the least significant byte first).","code":"public static final  ByteOrder LITTLE_ENDIAN"},{"title":"Binary buffers and byte order — DynamicByteBuffer — Constructor","note":"Related Binary buffers and byte order example: Constructs a new instance of DynamicByteBuffer with an initial capacity of 16 bytes. This constructor initializes the internal buffer to a default size, allowing for dynamic resizing as new data is added.","code":"public DynamicByteBuffer()"}],"art":13,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Settings"},{"index":59,"title":"Primitive and generic stacks","slug":"08-Data-Stacks","summary":"FastStack and primitive counterparts implement last-in, first-out storage. They suit temporary traversal state, reusable worklists, and nested operations where the newest entry must be consumed first. Choose them for stack semantics rather than queue semantics.","capabilities":[{"title":"Push/pop","description":"Append at the top and consume the most recent entry."},{"title":"Peek","description":"Inspect the top without removing it."},{"title":"Capacity","description":"Backing arrays grow according to the implementation's policy."},{"title":"Primitive variants","description":"Avoid boxed values for numeric or character stacks."}],"examples":[{"title":"Usage example","note":"Place these statements in your initialization or application method; imports belong at the top of the Java file.","code":"import valthorne.collections.stack.IntFastStack;\n\nIntFastStack work = new IntFastStack(8);\nwork.push(10);\nwork.push(20);\nwhile (!work.isEmpty()) {\n    int next = work.pop(); // 20, then 10.\n    System.out.println(next);\n}"},{"title":"ByteFastStack — Constructor","note":"Creates an empty stack with ten storage slots. The backing array grows when later pushes fill its current capacity.","code":"public ByteFastStack()"},{"title":"ByteFastStack — Constructor (2)","note":"Allocates the requested initial storage without placing any elements in it. Use positive capacity; zero is accepted here but the doubling growth rule cannot make a zero-length array usable for push.","code":"public ByteFastStack(int size)"},{"title":"push","note":"Appends a value at the top, doubling and copying the array when full. Existing order is preserved. Storage itself does not box primitive values.","code":"public void push(byte data)"},{"title":"pop","note":"Removes the most recently pushed element and clears its vacated slot. Empty stacks return -1 without changing state; that result is not a unique emptiness indicator because the same value can be pushed.","code":"public byte pop()"}],"art":13,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Stacks"},{"index":60,"title":"ID reuse queues, string maps, and trees","slug":"08-Data-Structures","summary":"This guide covers the specialized structures that do not fit ordinary array or stack usage: StringObjectMap, integer-ID reuse queues, and IntBinaryTree. Their names do not imply every guarantee of the similarly named Java collections; use the documented implementation behavior.","capabilities":[{"title":"String map","description":"Stores values keyed by strings using the engine's specialized map operations."},{"title":"Returned IDs","description":"UUIDQueue variants reuse returned numeric identifiers and otherwise issue sequential values."},{"title":"Tree lookup","description":"IntBinaryTree exposes insertion/search over indexed nodes."},{"title":"Sentinels and growth","description":"Capacity, empty state, and numeric overflow matter to these compact structures."}],"examples":[{"title":"StringObjectMap example","note":"This class is useful when you want a lightweight map for string-keyed lookups without the overhead of a more general-purpose collection. It is especially appropriate in engine code, asset registries, style/resource lookups, and other systems where many repeated string-based queries occur.","code":"StringObjectMap<Integer> scores = new StringObjectMap<>();\n\nscores.put(\"Albert\", 10);\nscores.put(\"Saphira\", 25);\nscores.put(\"Turaya\", 17);\n\nInteger albert = scores.get(\"Albert\");\nInteger missing = scores.getOrDefault(\"Unknown\", 0);\n\nboolean hasTuraya = scores.containsKey(\"Turaya\");\nboolean hasScore25 = scores.containsValue(25);\n\nscores.remove(\"Albert\");\n\nString[] keys = scores.keys();\nInteger[] values = scores.values(new Integer[scores.size()]);\n\nint size = scores.size();\nint capacity = scores.capacity();\nboolean empty = scores.isEmpty();\n\nscores.ensureCapacity(16);\nscores.shrink(8);\nscores.clear();"},{"title":"IntBinaryTree","note":"The current root insertion does not increment the storage counter. Consequently, a subsequent insertion overwrites the root slot and can discard the first value; this implementation does not provide ordinary set-preservation semantics.","code":"IntBinaryTree tree = new IntBinaryTree();\ntree.insert(7);\nboolean found = tree.search(7);"},{"title":"StringObjectMap — Constructor","note":"Creates a new map using the default initial capacity and default load factor.","code":"public StringObjectMap()"},{"title":"StringObjectMap — Constructor (2)","note":"Creates a new map using the given initial capacity and the default load factor.","code":"public StringObjectMap(int initialCapacity)"},{"title":"StringObjectMap — Constructor (3)","note":"Creates a new map using the given initial capacity and load factor.","code":"public StringObjectMap(int initialCapacity, float loadFactor)"}],"art":13,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Structures"},{"index":61,"title":"Text, number, and reflection utilities","slug":"08-Data-Utilities","summary":"These utilities collect text processing, number conversion/formatting, and reflection operations that do not belong to a larger runtime subsystem. Use the component reference to select a narrow operation rather than relying on a broad class name to imply behavior.","capabilities":[{"title":"Text operations","description":"TextUtility provides its documented parsing, transformation, and string helpers."},{"title":"Numeric convenience","description":"NumberUtility handles supported numeric representations and conversions."},{"title":"Reflection","description":"ReflectionUtility inspects or accesses Java members through its documented lookup rules."},{"title":"Error behavior","description":"Conversion failures and reflective exceptions follow the selected method's contract."}],"examples":[{"title":"Important notes example","note":"- Because this class stores a double, floating-point precision rules still apply.","code":"NumberUtility damage = new NumberUtility(125);\n\ndouble result = damage\n        .incrByPerc(10)\n        .clamp(0, 200)\n        .round(2)\n        .doubleValue();\n\nSystem.out.println(result);\n\nString formatted = new NumberUtility(15342.875)\n        .divide(3)\n        .round(2)\n        .format(\"#,##0.00\");\n\nSystem.out.println(formatted);\n\nNumberUtility movement = new NumberUtility(64);\nmovement\n        .mult(1.5)\n        .subtr(4)\n        .ceil();\n\nSystem.out.println(movement.intValue());"},{"title":"Important design notes example","note":"- Regex-based methods use the Java regular-expression engine, not plain literal replacement.","code":"String result = TextUtility.of(\"hello amazing world\")\n        .upperFirst()\n        .withSuffix(\"from Valthorne\")\n        .abbreviate(24)\n        .toString();\n\nSystem.out.println(result);\n\nTextUtility utility = TextUtility.of(\"playerHealthValue\");\nutility.camelCaseToWords().toTitleCase();\nSystem.out.println(utility); // Player Health Value\n\nString encoded = TextUtility.of(\"save data\")\n        .base64Encode()\n        .toString();\n\nSystem.out.println(encoded);"},{"title":"NumberUtility — Constructor","note":"Creates a new utility initialized to zero.","code":"public NumberUtility()"},{"title":"NumberUtility — Constructor (2)","note":"Creates a new utility initialized with the provided number.","code":"public NumberUtility(double number)"},{"title":"NumberUtility — set","note":"Replaces the current internal value.","code":"public NumberUtility set(double number)"}],"art":13,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/08-Data-Utilities"},{"index":62,"title":"Performance overlays and UI inspection","slug":"09-Developer-Diagnostics","summary":"Diagnostics expose performance and UI state without making those measurements part of application logic. PerformanceOverlay displays timing/render information; UIFrameStats captures work counters; UIInspector records node information for inspection.","capabilities":[{"title":"Frame measurements","description":"Counters show the amount of work performed during UI/render traversal."},{"title":"Overlays","description":"Visible diagnostics make regressions easier to observe while interacting."},{"title":"Inspection snapshots","description":"Recorded node bounds, clipping, focus, capture, and style help explain UI behavior."},{"title":"Live references","description":"Some inspection entries retain node identity while capturing selected display values."}],"examples":[{"title":"UIInspector example","note":"Collects opt-in inspection data from nodes dispatched during a UI root draw. Entries describe translated layout bounds, the effective batch clip, focus and pointer-capture ownership, and resolved style values at recording time. Recording follows actual dispatch order rather than traversing the tree again.","code":"UIInspector inspector = root.getInspector();\ninspector.setEnabled(true);\ninspector.setOutlines(true);\nroot.draw();\nfor (UIInspector.Entry entry : inspector.entries()) {\n    System.out.println(entry.type() + \" \" + entry.bounds());\n}"},{"title":"PerformanceOverlay — Constructor","note":"Rasterizes 95 printable ASCII glyphs with a bold 22-point monospaced AWT font, encodes the atlas as PNG, and uploads one texture. Glyph cells are 18 by 28 pixels; no application font resource is borrowed.","code":"public PerformanceOverlay()"},{"title":"frame","note":"Call once per presented frame; measures wall time, independently of simulation delta. The first call initializes timing without counting a frame interval. Once a sample spans at least 0.25 seconds, computes average FPS and milliseconds per interval and starts a new sample. Does not query actual display presentation.","code":"public void frame()"},{"title":"PerformanceOverlay — draw","note":"Draw inside an active 2D TextureBatch. No font rasterization or texture upload per frame. Uses the most recently sampled text, so calling draw does not advance timing.","code":"public void draw(TextureBatch batch, float x, float y)"},{"title":"drawText","note":"Draws printable ASCII using 18-by-28 quads with a 14-unit advance. Unsupported characters leave an advance-sized gap; newlines do not create additional rows. Coordinates follow the active batch projection and current rendering state.","code":"public void drawText(TextureBatch batch, String value, float x, float y)"}],"art":15,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/09-Developer-Diagnostics"},{"index":63,"title":"Platform integration helpers","slug":"09-Developer-Platform-Integration","summary":"These small APIs centralize platform checks and launcher-level graphics diagnostics.","capabilities":[{"title":"Filament platform support","description":"FilamentPlatform reports whether the pinned Filament runtime is published for a supplied OS/architecture pair or for the current JVM. Supported combinations are Windows x64, Linux x64/ARM64, and macOS ARM64. This is an artifact-availability check, not proof that a machine's driver supports every renderer feature."},{"title":"Desktop diagnostics","description":"PlatformTools exposes launcher-oriented OpenGL helpers for viewport changes, GPU completion, error/renderer queries, active texture selection, PNG framebuffer capture, and deterministic input replay."},{"title":"Prefer engine operations in application code","description":"Valthorne's public rendering APIs already perform and, where documented, restore the underlying GL work. Start with these operations before importing LWJGL calls:"}],"examples":[{"title":"Check Filament support and capture a frame","note":"FilamentPlatform reports published native-runtime availability. PlatformTools capture requires an active desktop OpenGL context and writes a vertically corrected PNG.","code":"boolean filamentAvailable =\n    valthorne.graphics.FilamentPlatform.supported();\n\nvalthorne.PlatformTools.capture(\n    \"build/frame.png\",\n    Window.getWidth(),\n    Window.getHeight());"},{"title":"Graphics capability selection — Graphics capability selection example","note":"Related Graphics capability selection example: SceneRenderer3D.Backend.AUTO selects Filament when the current adapter's platform and graphics prerequisites exist, otherwise RASTER. Explicit backend requests fail if unsupported. Asset, shader and native loading failures remain errors; they do not silently downgrade quality. The selected backend is available through getBackend(). The facade selects once,…","code":"JGL.init(app, JGLConfiguration.defaults().automaticContext());\n// In Application.init(), with the context current:\nSystem.out.println(Window.getGraphicsCapabilities());\nSceneRenderer3D renderer = new SceneRenderer3D();\n// In Application.render():\nrenderer.render(scene, camera);\n// In Application.dispose(), before destroying the context:\nrenderer.close();"},{"title":"Graphics capability selection — Filament desktop renderer — Engine integration","note":"Related Graphics capability selection example: Related Filament desktop renderer example: Use SceneRenderer3D for ordinary application code: it performs capability and platform selection and owns the selected renderer. Request Backend.FILAMENT only when the application requires Filament-specific output and should fail on an unsupported system. Access renderer.filament() only for backend-specific quality, exposure, environment, or anti-aliasing…","code":"// AUTO selects Filament when supported and the raster backend otherwise.\nSceneRenderer3D renderer = new SceneRenderer3D();\nrenderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n// Before destroying the current graphics context:\nrenderer.close();"},{"title":"Graphics capability selection — Filament desktop renderer — 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — BillboardBatch3D","note":"Related Graphics capability selection example: Related Filament desktop renderer example: Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Construction, rendering, and disposal require a current OpenGL context. Rendering restores captured GL state; construction leaves array bindings at zero. The shader, vertex array, and GPU buffer are owned; textures, sprites, cameras, and materials remain borrowed. Shared scratch vectors preclude concurrent use.","code":"BillboardBatch3D batch = new BillboardBatch3D();\ntry {\n    batch.begin(sprite.getTextureRegion().getTexture());\n    batch.draw(sprite, camera);\n    batch.render(new MeshRenderState3D().setCamera(camera));\n} finally {\n    batch.dispose();\n}"},{"title":"Graphics capability selection — Filament desktop renderer — 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards — Material3D","note":"Related Graphics capability selection example: Related Filament desktop renderer example: Related 3D models, materials, scenes, and billboards example: Tint and emissive colors are owned mutable objects exposed directly by getters. Textures are borrowed and never disposed here; copies share the texture but have independent color storage. Sharing a material shares all later mutations. Set render pass before an explicit depth-write override, because pass assignment resets that flag. Instances provide no…","code":"Material3D material = new Material3D()\n        .setRenderPass(RenderPass3D.TRANSLUCENT)\n        .setTint(new Color(1f, 1f, 1f, 0.5f))\n        .setDepthWrite(false);"}],"art":14,"wikiUrl":"https://github.com/tehnewb/Valthorne/wiki/09-Developer-Platform-Integration"}];
